��� ���� �������� ��������� ���������� ��� ���������� �����������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n��� ����� ��� ���� ������ ���� �������� ����������� ������ ��� ������� ��������� ���� ��� ��� ��\n������ ������� ��������� ����� ��������� ��� ���� ���������������� ����������� ��������� ���\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������� ��� ���� "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|STARRED   |49", "t": "Delay in releasing AISHE Report", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No. 49 \nANSWERED ON 24.07.2023 \n \nDelay in releasing AISHE Report \n \n*49. SHRI RAVIKUMAR D.: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the reason for the inordinate delay in releasing the All India Survey on Higher Education \n(AISHE) 2020-21 report; and \n(b) whether the Government has taken any steps to release the AISHE Report and if so, the \ndetails thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n \n(a) and (b): A statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN RE PLY TO PART (a) and (b) OF THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 49 ANSWERED ON 24.07.2023 ASKED BY \nSHRI RAVIKUMAR D., HON’BLE MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT REGARDING \nDELAY IN RELEASING AISHE REPORT \n \n(a) The All India Survey on Higher Education (AISHE) is the comprehensive source of \nHigher Education Statistics in the country.  Data on various parameters, such as \nEnrolment, Institution information, Teacher Information, etc are uploaded by Higher \nEducation I nstitutions (HEIs) registered under AISHE in the portal aishe.gov.in, in \nspecially designed formats of data collection. The AISHE Surveys have been \nconducted annually since 2011.  The AISHE Survey is conducted through the co -\noperation and support of the State Governments, Universities, etc.  \n \nThe Report of the 2019 -20 Survey and launch of the 2020 -21 Survey was affected by \nthe unprecedented global Covid  outbreak. The Academic Session 2020 -21 and \nAdmission process in the HEIs got considerably delayed, extending upto December, \n2020 and in a few cases even after that. The comprehensive data on course -\nwise/Programme-wise enrolments gets available in HEIs onl y after the admission \nprocess is fully completed. Further, the severe outbreak of 2 nd wave of Covid in 2021, \nin which staff of various Departments, State Governments and Universities got \naffected, also delayed the launch of the Survey.  \n \nIn AISHE 2020 -21, the data collection mechanism was upgraded from old system of \ndata filling in the Adobe Acrobat format, to a dynamic, user friendly and \ntechnologically upgraded Web Data Capture Format (DCF) platform by the National \nInformatics Centre (NIC).Till 2019 -20, t he Adobe software (windows 2008) was \nbeing used for data collection in AISHE which was hosted on physical server instead \nof cloud Server. Hence, for compliance with NIC IT infra -Policy and to upgrade the \nAISHE portal with new IT features, Web -DCF was intro duced in AISHE for \ncollecting the information during year 2021 and was used for first time in AISHE \n2020-21 Survey.  Consequently, functional testing was done followed by detailed \nSecurity Audit of the system and trainings to the State Nodal Officers/HEIs before \noperationalisation of new system.  \n \nBeing the first DCF Survey, the earlier data structure and reports had to be modified.  \nThis had to be tested/matched with the unit level data of the Survey.  In view of this, \nintense scrutiny was undertaken after closure of the Survey. \n   \n(b) The AISHE 2020 -21 Report, has already been released in January 2023.  It may be \nmentioned that the enrolment of the country has increased from 3.85 crore in 2019 -20 \nto 4.14 crore in 2020-21.   \n \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|STARRED   |5", "t": "Works under Atal Bhujal Yojana and Jal Jeevan Mission", "head": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF DRINKING WATER & SANITATION \n  \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 5  \n  (5th Position) \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20/07/2023 \n \nWorks under Atal Bhujal Yojana and Jal Jeevan Mission \n†*5. SHRI SUDHAKAR TUKARAM SHRANGARE: \nSHRI SUNIL KUMAR SINGH: \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state: \n(a)  the works done by the Government under  the Atal Bhujal Yojana (ABY) and Jal Jeevan \nMission (JJM) in the country particularly in the States of  Maharashtra, Jharkhand, \nChhattisgarh, Rajasthan, Karnataka and North Eastern Region, district -wise along with the \ndetails of funds allocated and utilized in this regard; \n(b)  the efforts being made to speed up the  progress of work under the said scheme, State/  UT-\nwise particularly in Rajasthan; \n(c)  the number of households provided with tap  water connections under Har Ghar Jal Yojana \nunder JJM, State/ UT-wise;  \n(d)  whether complaints are being received about  irregularities and poor quality of works under \nthe JJM;  \n(e)  if so, the details of the works referred in such complaints, State/UT and district-wise; and  \n(f)  the list of the contractors identified and the details of the action taken on such defaulters? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF JAL SHAKTI  \n(SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH SHEKHAWAT) \n (a) to (f) A statement is laid on the table of the House. \n\n2 \n \nStatement referred in reply of Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 5 to be answered on \n20.07.2023 \n(a) to (c) Atal Bhujal Yojana is being implemented in water stressed Gram Panchayats (GPs) of \n229 administrative Blocks/ Talukas in 80 districts of 7 States, viz., Haryana, Gujarat, Karnataka, \nMadhya Pradesh, Maharashtra, Rajasthan  and Uttar Pradesh for a period of 5 years from \n01.04.2020.  The scheme aims at community led sustainable ground water management throug h \nconvergence of activities.  \nThe scheme is being implemented through active participation of the communities in the \nparticipating States in various activities such as monitoring and disseminating ground water data, \nwater budgeting, preparation of Gram Pan chayat wise Water Security Plans having demand side \nas well as supply interventions proposed and their implementation through convergence of \nongoing schemes and IEC activities related to sustainable groundwater management. \nThe fund under the scheme, inter alia, are being re leased as per performance based upon \nachievements of certain pre -defined indicators. As on 12.07.2023, following funds have been \nreleased to 7 participating States   \n(Rs. in crore) \nFinancial Year Funds released \n2020-21 109.00 \n2021-22 310.40 \n2022-23 1,138.53 \n2023-24 130.97 \nTotal 1,688.90 \n \nVarious steps viz. optimization of parameters as per the Scheme objectives and Standard \nOperating Procedures for data collection by the stakeholders , modifications in MIS with an aim \nto make it more user friendly, regular reviews at various levels along with field visits by National \nProgram Monitoring Unit (NPMU) officers  etc. have been taken for expeditious implementation \nof the scheme. \nSince August 2019, Government of India, in partnership with Stat e Governments, is \nimplementing Jal Jeevan Mission (JJM) -Har Ghar Jal to enable every rural household in the \ncountry, including those in the States of Maharashtra, Jharkhand, Chhattisgarh, Rajasthan, \nKarnataka and North Eastern Region, to have assured potable water through tap connection. \nAt the time of announcement of Jal Jeevan Mission, 3.23 Crore (17%) rural households were \nreported to have tap water connections. So far, as reported by States/UTs as on 17.07.2023, \naround 9.35 Crore additional rural households have been pro vided with tap water connections \nunder JJM. Thus, as on 17.07.2023, out of 19.46 Crore rural households in the country, around \n12.58 Crore (64.6%) households are reported to have tap water supply in their homes.  State/ UT-\nwise and year-wise details are at Annex-I. \nDistrict-wise fund allocations and details thereof are not made and maintained by this \ndepartment. However, y ear-wise and State/ UT -wise details of Central fund allocated, fund \ndrawn and fund utilization reported by the States/ U Ts under Jal Jeeva n Mission in 2019 -20, \n2020-21, 2021-22, 2022-23 & 2023-24 (as on 17.07.2023) are at Annex-II. \n\n3 \n \nTo achieve the goal of JJM in the whole country with speed and scale, Government of India is \nhandholding the States,  including Rajasthan,  to exp edite the implemen tation of the mission \nthrough number of steps inter alia, which includes joint discussion and finalization of annual \naction plan (AAP) of States/ UTs , regular review of implementation, workshops/ conferences/ \nwebinars for capacity buildin g and knowledge sh aring, field visits by multi -disciplinary team to \nprovide technical support, etc. A detailed Operational Guideline for the implementation of JJM; \nMargdarshika for Gram Panchayats & VWSCs to provide safe drinking water in rural \nhouseholds and Guidelines on"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|STARRED   |50", "t": "Additional Borrowings by States", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.50 \nANSWERED ON 20.07.2023 \n \nPOWER INFRASTRUCTURE IN TAMIL NADU \n \n50.  DR. T. SUMATHY (a) THAMIZHACHI THANGAPANDIAN: \nSHRI GNANATHIRAVIAM S.: \n \n Will the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Union Government has provided funds to Tamil Nadu \nGeneration and Distribution  Corporation Limited (TANGEDCO) through \nREC Ltd. and PFC Ltd. for various power generation projects  in the \nState; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and  the steps taken by the Government \nto develop power infrastructure in Tamil Nadu including installation of \nTransformers, smart meters and power transmission centres; \n \n(c)  the details of the total power generation capacity installed in \nTamil Nadu and power  sharing agreements entered into with other \nStates; and \n \n(d)  the funds allocated and disbursed to TANGEDCO during the last \nfive years for the development of new power projects including the \nKundah Power House? \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF POWER AND NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY  \n \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n \n(a) :  Yes, Sir.  REC Ltd. and PFC Ltd. have provided Loans to Tamil \nNadu Generation and Distribution Corporation Limited TANGEDCO for \nvarious Power Generation projects in the State. In all Rs.27,052.49 \nCrore have been sanctioned during the last five years to TANGEDCO. \n………2. \n\n- 2 - \n \n(b) :  The steps taken by the Government to develop power \ninfrastructure in Tamil Nadu are as follows:  \n \nI. Funds were released under IPDS and DDUGJY Schemes for  \nstrengthening of Distribution  projects and both these \nschemes were closed w.e.f 31.03.2022.  The details of \ninfrastructure created under IPDS and DDUGJY are enclosed \nas Annexure-I. \n \nII. Revamped Distribution Sector Scheme- (RDSS) \n \nThe Revamped Distribution Sector Scheme is a Reforms Bas ed \nand Results-Linked Scheme launched by Ministry of Power  to improve \nthe quality, reliability and affordability of power supply to consumers \nthrough a financially sustainable and operationally efficient \nDistribution Sector and to reduce the AT&C losses at  pan India levels \nto 12 – 15% and reduce the ACS –ARR gap to zero. \n \nThe infrastructure works proposed under RDSS scheme are \nenclosed at Annexure-II \n \n(c) :  The total power requirement of Tamil Nadu is 34,706 MW.  Out of \nthis 22,436 MW is met by TANGEDCO, 3190 MW by the Independent \nPower Producers (IPP) and  4698 MW by the Central Generating \nStations (CGS). \n \n The balance requirement is managed by Power generated \noutside Tamil Nadu. The share of IPP and CGS in the outside support is \n1910 MW and 2472 MW respectively.  \n \n(d) : The Funds allocated and disbursed to TANGEDCO during the last \n5 years for the development of new power projects including the \nKundah Power House in Tamil Nadu by PFC and REC  are enclosed as       \nAnnexure-III. \n \n*************** \n \n \n  \n\n \n \nANNEXURE-I \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED IN REPLY TO PART ( b) OF UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO.50   ANSWERED IN THE LOK SABHA ON 20.07.2023  \n************** \n  \n(i)    Major works executed under IPDS by PFC Ltd, as per closure \nreports submitted by TANGEDCO \n \n \n(ii)  Major works executed under DDUGJY including Saubhagya by REC \nLtd, as per closure reports submitted by TANGEDCO \n \n \n \n \n*********** \n \n  \nBOQ Unit Quantum  \n33/11 KV New Sub-stations Nos 68 +7 GIS Substations \nAugmentation of Sub-stations Nos 41 \nInstallation of New Distribution \nTransformer Nos 1094 \nNew HT Line cKm 4266 \nNew LT Lines cKm 1747 \nUG Cable cKm 1747 \nAB Cable cKm 153 \nSolar Panels KWp 2082 \nBOQ Unit Quantum  \nNew Sub-stations Nos 106 \nAugmentation of Sub-stations Nos 128 \nInstallation of New \nDistribution Transformer Nos 1189 \nNew HT Line cKm 3052 \nNew LT Lines cKm 1174 \nFeeder Separation  cKm 797 \nConsumers Meters  Nos 1195856 \n\n \n \nANNEXURE-II \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED IN REPLY TO PART ( b) OF UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO.50  ANSWERED IN THE LOK SABHA ON 20.07.2023  \n************** \n \ni) Loss Reduction works approved by MoP/ GoI. \nSl. \nNo. \nTargeted interventions  for \nloss reduction Unit \nPhysical Targets  Total Estimated \nOutlay  \n(Rs. Cr.) FY23 FY24 FY25 \n1 Agriculture Feeder \nSegregation \nNo. of \nFeeders 441  592 652 1685 6,227.81 \n2 High Voltage Distribution \nSystem \nNo. of \nFeeders 68 99 106 273 1,592.67 \n3 Separation of Double DTs \nusing HVDS Nos. 3207 4455 6230 13,892 1,069.92 \n4 Reconductoring of \nExisting 33 KV Feeders KM 206.50 213.18 122.40 542.08 39.46 \n5 \nDeployment of new ERP \nmodules (E-way Bill, GST \nan