�����������\n��\n������ �������� �������������������������� ������������ ������ ���������������������� �������� �����"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|STARRED   |72", "t": "Shortage of Milk and Milk Products", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 72 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON JULY 20, 2023 \n \nPROJECTS UNDER AMRUT  \n \nNO. 72.   SHRI C.R. PATIL: \nSHRI E.T. MOHAMMED BASHEER: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to \nstate: \n(a)  the details of funds released to all States under the Atal Mission for \nRejuvenation and Urban Transformation (AMRUT) since 2014, year-wise, \nincluding Gujarat and Kerala; \n(b)  the details of projects applied, approved and executed in the State of \nGujarat and Kerala under AMRUT since 2014; \n(c)  the expected dates of completion of AMRUT projects in the districts of \nSurat and Navsari; and \n(d)  whether it has come to the notice of the Government that the AMRUT \nfunds are underutilized and if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \n MINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n(a):   The year-wise funds released under Atal Mission for Rejuvenation and Urban \nTransformation (AMRUT) to all States/ Union Territories is at ANNEXURE.  \n(b): In Gujarat, against the approved plan size of ₹4,884 cr ore, 453 projects \nworth ₹5,168 crore have been grounded of which 412 projects worth ₹4,556 crore \nhave been completed. Overall, works worth ₹5,028 crore have been physically \ncompleted and ₹4,572 crore have been expended so far. \n…2/ - \n\n \n \n-2- \nIn Kerala, against the approved plan size of ₹2,359 cr ore, 1,010 projects \nworth ₹2,228 crore have been grounded of which 884 projects worth ₹1,293 crore \nhave been completed. Overall, works worth ₹1,896 crore have been physically \ncompleted and ₹1,615 crore have been expended so far.  \n(c): Only two cities are covered under AMRUT in the districts of Surat & Navsari \nof Gujarat, status of projects is as below: \nS.No Name of \nthe District \nNumber \nof \nProjects \ntaken \nTotal \nProjects \nCompleted \nUnder \nProgress Sector Date of \nCompletion \n1 Navsari 10 8 2 \nSewerage \nand Septage \nManagement \n31.12.2023 \nDrainage 01.11.2023 \n2 Surat 50 50 - - - \n(d): Under AMRUT, States have been empowered to select, apprise, propose and \nafter approval from Apex Committee of Ministry of Housing & Urban Affairs \n(MoHUA), implement projects within the broad framework of AMRUT Mission. \nAMRUT mission was initially launched for a period of 05 years i.e. Financial Year \n2015-16 to 2019-20 with committed central assistance of projects worth ₹35,990 \ncrore. However, so far ₹ 33,110.41 crore has been released and  ₹28,862 crore \ncentral assistance has been reported utilized by the States/ UTs. Implementation \nof some projects taken up under AMRUT Mission by States/ UTs have got delayed \ndue to various reasons, such as land disputes, natural calamities, delay in \nobtaining clearances/permissions from other departments and disruptions/ \nlockdowns caused by the COVID-19 pandemic etc. \n \n******\n\n \n \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 72 REGARDING \n“PROJECTS UNDER AMRUT”, DUE FOR ANSWER IN THE LOK SABHA ON 20 JULY, 2023. \n \nYEAR-WISE FUNDS RELEASED UNDER AMRUT TO ALL STATES/UTS \n(Amount in ₹ crore) \nComponent 2015-16 2016-17 2017-18 2018-19 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 2022-23 2023-24 \n(till now) Total \nAMRUT \nprojects 1,978.74 2,369.60 3,721.51 5,143.09 5,685.60 6,146.72 6,112.89 961.17 991.09 33,110.41 \nAdministrative \n& Office \nExpenses \n122.90 114.92 104.04 235.34 297.76 205.63 58.09 0.00 0 1,138.67 \nReforms \nIncentives 400.00 500.03 340.00 417.99 207.34 0.00 19.50 0.00 13 1,897.66 \nGIS based \nMaster plan \nsub-scheme \n0.00 0.00 62.07 27.28 11.10 27.70 20.16 18.74 6.40 173.45 \nLocal Area \nPlanning & \nTown Planning \nSchemes \n0.00 0.00 0.00 0.00 7.99 3.34 3.20 0.80 0 15.33 \nSpill over \nJnNURM \nprojects \n542.07 1,855.46 725.43 20.72 0.00 0.00 0.00 0.00 0 3,143.69 \nTotal 3,043.71 4,840.01 4,953.06 5,844.42 6,209.79 6,383.40 6,213.85 980.71 1010.5 39,479.44 \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|STARRED   |73", "t": "Protest by Women Wrestlers", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 73\n(To be answered on the 20th July 2023)\n \nINCREASE IN FLOW OF INTERNATIONAL PASSENGERS\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) whether it is true that flow of international passengers has almost doubled\nduring the first three months of 2023 as compared to the same period in 2022;\n \n(b) if so, whether the Government has tried to ascertain the reasons for spike\nin international air travel and if so, the details thereof;\n \n(c) whether it is true that the market share of scheduled domestic airlines has\nalso shown an increase and if so, the details thereof;\n \n(d) the details of the steps taken by the Government to attract domestic and\ninternational passengers to give boost to aviation industry in the country;\n \n(e) the details of percentage rise in international passengers during the above\nsaid period; and\n \n(f) the steps taken/being taken by the Government to reduce air fares?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of State in the Ministry of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) to (e) Passengers carried by scheduled Indian and Foreign carriers for\nscheduled international operations in the first quarter of 2023 show an\nincrease of about 88% over the passenger figures of first quarter of 2022 as\nper provisional figures available.\n73. SHRI DHAIRYASHEEL SAMBHAJIRAO MANE\nSHRI SUDHEER GUPTA\nSHRI SANJAY SADASHIVRAO MANDLIK\nSHRI SHRIRANG APPA BARNE\nSHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV\n be pleased to state:-\n (GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD))\n\n \nThe number of international passengers had been affected adversely due to\nCOVID-19. However, the situation has improved as witnessed by rebound in\nair travelers after the resumption of scheduled international commercial\npassenger operations w.e.f 27.03.2022.\n \nThe market share of scheduled domestic airlines for international passengers\nhas not shown an increase as per provisional figures available.\n \nTo attract domestic and international passengers and to give boost to aviation\nindustry in the country, the government has inter-alia, taken the following\nsteps:\n \n(i)  For enhancing regional connectivity in the domestic sector, the Ministry\noperates the UDAN scheme.\n(ii) In the international sector, 18 tourist destinations of India have been made\navailable for SAARC (except Afghanistan and Pakistan) and ASEAN\ncountries to/from which designated carriers of India as well as designated\ncarriers of SAARC and ASEAN countries can mount unlimited operations.\nAdditionally , in accordance with the National Civil Aviation Policy, 2016, the\ngovernment has, on reciprocal basis, offered open sky arrangements to\nSAARC countries and countries located beyond 5000 kms from Delhi. As on\ndate, India has open sky arrangements with 23 countries which has facilitated\nunlimited operations between India and these countries.\n \n(f) The airfare is market driven and is neither regulated nor established by the\nGovernment. Air fares are determined by the airlines based on interplay of\nmarket forces. The fares on any route is dependent inter-alia on seasonality,\nholidays and festivals, cost of Aviation Turbine Fuel, competition and other\nsimilar factors.\n \n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|STARRED   |74", "t": "Development of Animal Husbandry, Dairy and Poultry", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 74\n(To be answered on the 20th July 2023)\n \nAIRPORTS IN UTTAR PRADESH\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) the details of total number of airports sanctioned for Uttar Pradesh by the\nGovernment under its various schemes during the last five years, district-wise;\n(b) whether the Government has set any timeline to complete all the proposed aviation\nprojects in Uttar Pradesh;\n(c) if so, the details thereof; and\n(d) the initiatives taken by the Government to increase the number of international\nairports in the country?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of State in the Ministry of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) to (c): Government of India (GoI) has accorded 'In-Principle' approval for setting up\nof a Greenfield Airport viz. Noida (Jewar) International Airport in Gautam Budh Nagar\ndistrict in the State of Uttar Pradesh on 08.05.2018. Foundation stone for the same was\nlaid on 25.11.2021. The probable date of completion of the Noida (Jewar) Greenfield\nairport project is September, 2024.\n \n(d): Declaration of an airport as International Airport depends upon traffic potential,\ndemand from airlines for operation of international flights and bilateral Air Services\nAgreement besides provision of Ground Lighting facilities, Instrument Landing System,\nrunway length, Immigration, Health and Animal & Plant Quarantine services etc.\n \nIn the past nine years, four Greenfield airports at Kannur in Kerala, Shirdi in\nMaharashtra, Kushinagar in Uttar Pradesh and Mopa in Goa have been developed as\ninternational airports. In addition, two existing airports namely, Vijayawada and\nTirupati airports in Andhra Pradesh have been declared as international airports in\n2017. At present, there are 30 international airports in the country, with three in Uttar\nPradesh viz. Kushinagar, Varanasi, and Lucknow international airports.\n \nGovernment of India has also accorded 'in-principle' approval for setting up of five more\ninternational Greenfield Airports at Jewar in Uttar Pradesh, Bhogapuram in Andhra\nPradesh, Dholera and Hirasar in Gujarat, and Navi Mumbai in Maharashtra which are\nat various stages of development.\n \n*****\n74. SHRI RAMESH CHAND BIND\n be pleased to state:-\n (GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD))"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12