����������������� ��������\n�������������������� �������� �������������� ������ �������������������� ���������� ������������ �������� �������� �������������� ���������������� ������������ ��������������inter-\nalia���� ���������������� �������������� ������������������ ������ ������������ �������� �������������� ���������� �������� �������������� �������������� ���������������������� ������\n������������ �������������� ������ ������������������ ������������������������ �������� ����������������������"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |254", "t": "Salary of Anganwadi Workers", "head": "GOVERNEMNT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO 254 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2023 \n \nSALARY OF ANGANWADI WORKERS \n  \n254.  SHRI SANTOSH PANDEY:  \n  \nWill the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the details of the amount paid as salary to the Anganwadi Workers (AWWs) by \nthe Union Government, State-wise;  \n(b) whether there is any State-wise variation in the salary paid to the AWWs;  \n(c) if so, the details thereof, State/UT-wise; and \n(d) the details of the share paid by the Union Government for periodic increase in \nthe salary of AWWs? \n  \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n  \n(a)  to (d): Under the scheme guidelines for 'Mission   Saksham Anganwadi and \nPoshan 2.0', Anganwadi   Workers (AWWs) are honorary workers and are  paid a \nmonthly honorarium as decided by the Government from time to time.  On 01.10.2018, \nGovernment of India had enhanced the honorarium of AWWs at main -AWCs from \n₹3,000/- to ₹ 4,500/- per month; AWWs at mini- AWCs from ₹ 2,250/- to ₹ 3,500/- per \nmonth; AWHs from ₹1,500/ - to ₹2,250/- per month. Performance linked incentive of \n₹250/- per month is paid to AWHs and @ ₹500/ - per month to AWWs. In addition, \nStates/UTs are also paying additional mone tary incentives/ honorarium to these \nfunctionaries from their own resources. \n  \n**** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |255", "t": "Yoga for Treatment of Mental Health Disorders", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF AYUSH\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO-255\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2023\nYOGA FOR TREATMENT OF MENTALHEALTH DISORDERS\n255. SHRI CHANDRA SEKHAR BELLANA:\nWill the Minister of Ayush be pleased to state:\na) whether the government proposes to implement yoga as a mental health practice;\nb) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;\nc) whether any research has been conducted on the effectiveness of yoga in treating\nmental health disorders and if so, the details thereof;\nd) whether the Government has any plan to include yoga as a part of the national mental\nhealth policy; and\ne) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF AYUSH\n(SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL)\n(a) & (b): Ministry of Ayush, at present, does not have any specific proposal to implement\nyoga as a mental health practice. However, Yoga has been used primarily as a stress\nmanagement and lifestyle intervention tool to combat psychosomatic diseases. The ministry\nis popularizing yoga as a therapeutic tool to improve mental health in all its institutions.\nFurther, Central Council of Research in Yoga & Naturopathy (CCRYN) also has 3 centres for\nMind Body Medicine units in AIIMS Rishikesh, AIIMS Raipur and PGI Chandigarh where\nmental health issues are being managed. CCRYN also has a Collaborative research centre\nwith National Institute of Mental Health and Neurosciences (NIMHANS), Bengaluru where\nyoga is being used in the treatment of mental health conditions.\nFurthermore, Yoga OPDs and integrative medicine centres have been set up in major health\ninstitutes across the country to offer yoga therapy services for mental health.\n(c): Studies at NIMHANS have shown excellent results with yoga in improving mental\nhealth conditions.\nCCRYN in collaboration with NIMHANS is working on various projects on mental health\nincluding the followings:\n- Implicit cognition and mirror neuron activity following Yoga in healthy individuals.\n\n- Understanding neuro-hemodynamic correlates of Om chanting: A functional magnetic\nresonance imaging study.\n- Psycho-neuro-endocrinological markers of stress and the response to a yoga-based\nintervention in first degree relatives of schizophrenia patients (FDRS).\n- Effect of yoga on mirror neuron activity among patients with depression: A\nTranscranial Magnetc Stimulation study” revised for originally proposed study with\ntitle“ Role of Yoga in correcting GABA neurotransmitter deficit in moderate to severe\ndepressive patients, a single blind and randomized controlled study.\nFurthermore, there are more than 1188 publications available with a keyword “Yoga for\nMental Health” in Pubmed.\nLink: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=yoga+mental+health\n(d) & (e): At present, there is no such plan in the Ministry to include yoga as a part of the\nnational mental health policy.\n***"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |256", "t": "Recruitment of Women in NDA", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE  \n  \nLOK SABHA  \nSTARRED QUESTION No. 256  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.08.2023  \n  \nNationally Determined Contributions \n  \n*256. SHRI PARBHUBHAI NAGARBHAI V ASA V A:  \n  \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate:  \n  \n(a)  whether the country is on the track to meet Nationally Determined Contributions under \nParis Agreement; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and \n(c)  whether the country will be able to achieve the target of reducing the share of non-fossil \nfuel by forty per cent in power generation by 2030??  \n   \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI BHUPENDER YADA V) \n  \n(a) to (c): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n  \n*****  \n    \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARAS (a) TO (c) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 256 DUE FOR REPL Y ON 07.08.2023 REGARDING \n‘NATIONALLY DETERMINED CONTRIBUTIONS’ RAISED BY SHRI PARBHUBHAI \nNAGARBHAI V ASA V A, HON’BLE MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT  \n  \n(a) to (c) India submitted its first Nationally Determined Contribution (NDC) under the Paris \nAgreement to the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC) in \n2015 and updated its first NDC in August 2022.  India has overachieved its commitments made \nin its NDC (2015) on electric power installed capacity from non-fossil fuel-based energy \nresources. As per the report of Central Electricity Authority, electric power installed capacity \nfrom non-fossil fuel-based energy resources is about 43.60% (183 GW out of total 421 GW) as \non 30th June 2023.   \n \nAs per the updated NDC, India has an enhanced target to reduce emissions intensity of \nits GDP by 45 percent by 2030 from 2005 level and achieve about 50 percent cumulative electric \npower installed capacity from non-fossil fuel-based energy resources by 2030.  The other targets \nare to create an additional carbon sink of 2.5 to 3 billion tonnes of CO2 equivalent through \nadditional forest and tree cover by 2030; propagate sustainable lifestyles through a mass \nmovement of ‘LIFE’ (Lifestyle for Environment) as a key to combating climate change; to \nbetter adapt to climate change; adopt climate friendly and cleaner development path; mobilize \ndomestic, new and additional funds and capacity building.  \n  \nIndia has progressively continued decoupling of economic growth from greenhouse gas \nemissions. India’s emission intensity of gross domestic product (GDP) has reduced by 24 per \ncent between 2005 and 2016. As per the estimates published by the Forest Survey of India, India \nis on the track to achieve additional carbon sink of 2.5 billion to 3.0 billion tones through \nadditional forest and tree cover by 2030, and has achieved carbon sink of 1.97 billion tonnes of \nCO2 by 2019 as compared to base year 2005.   \n*** \n  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |257", "t": "Reimbursement claims of CGHS Beneficiaries", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nDEPARTMENTOF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 257\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21ST JULY,2023\n“REIMBURSEMENT CLAIMS OF CGHS BENEFICIARIES”\n257. SHRI UNMESH BHAIYYASAHEB PATIL:\nDR. HEENA VIJAYKUMAR GA VIT:\nSHRI NATARAJANP. R.:\nDR. SUJAYRADHAKRISHNA VIKHE PATIL:\nPROF. RITABAHUGUNA JOSHI:\nDR. KRISHNA PALSINGH YADA V:\nDR. SHRIKANT EKNATH SHINDE:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has taken note of the difficulties being faced by CGHS\nbeneficiaries due to making upfront payments to the medical institutes and seeking\nindividual reimbursement claims and follow ups from the CGHS;\n(b) if so, the details thereof along with steps taken by the Government for eliminating the\nneed to submit individual reimbursement claims and follow ups for approvals;\n(c) whether the Government has recognized any specific institutes which would provide\nsuch relief of cashless treatment to the beneficiaries, if so, the details thereof;\n(d) the details of the criteria for determining the eligibility of CGHS beneficiaries for such\nrelief at such recognized institutes, if any; and\n(e) whether the Government plans to expand this facility to other medical institutes in the\nnear future and if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATEIN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILYWELFARE\n(DR. BHARATI PRA VINPAWAR)\n(a) to (e) As per Memorandum of Association (MoA) signed between CGHS and empanelled\nHealth Care Organizations (HCOs), in respect of the following categories of beneficiaries,\ntreatment / procedures/services shall be provided on credit.\n Pensioners,\n Ex-Members of Parliament,\n Sitting Members of Parliament\n Freedom Fighters,\n Serving CGHS/DGHS/Ministry of H&FW employees,\n Such other categories of CGHS cardholders as notified by the Government.\n\nNo payment shall be sought from them and the bills should be submitted to the Bill\nClearing Agency / Office of the Additional / Joint Additional Director, CGHS of the concerned\ncity.\nFurther, the Government has also signed MoA with All India Institute of Medical\nSciences, New Delhi, Post Graduate Institute of Medical Education and Research, Chandigarh,\nJawaharlal Institute of Postgraduate Medical Education and Research, Puducherry and All\nIndia Institute of Medical Sciences located in Bhopal, Bhubaneswar, Jodhpur, Patna, Raipur\nand Rishikesh to provide treatment on Cashless basis to CGHS pensioners and other entitled\nclass of beneficiaries like ex-MPs. ex-Governors, former Judges of Supreme Court of India,\nformer Judges of High Courts, Freedom Fighters, etc. No referral from CGHS is mandatory for\navailing treatment at these Medical Institutions.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |258", "t": "Digital Healthcare Mechanism", "head": " \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *258 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.08.2023 \n \nPRESERVATION OF HERITAGE SITES IN BIHAR \n \n \n *258.  SHRI DILESHWAR KAMAIT: \n Will the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state: \n \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF CULTURE, TOURISM AND DEVELOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN REGION \n(SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n \n(a) & (b): A statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n--- \n \n \n \n \n(a) the steps taken by the Government to protect and preserve monuments \nand heritage sites in the State of Bihar; and \n(b) \n \nthe amount of funds allocated, utilised and disbursed for the preservation of monuments \nand heritage sites under the control of Archaeological Survey of India (ASI) in the State \nof Bihar, monument-wise during the last four years? \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n--- \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) & (b) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *258 FOR 07.08.2023 ASKED BY SHRI DILESHWAR \nKAMAIT. \n(a) There are 70 centrally protected monuments/sites under the jurisdiction of \nArchaeological Survey of India (ASI) in the State of Bihar.  ASI takes up \nconservation work of monuments/sites as per requirement and availability of \nresources, while following the National Conservation Policy, 2014. \n(b) The total funds allocated and expenditure incurred in the last four years on \nconservation and preservation of centrally protected monuments in Bihar are given \nat Annexure-I.  \n \n\n \n \nAnnexure-I \nAnnexure Referred to in reply Part (b) of the Lok Sabha Starred Question No.*258 for \n07.08.2023.  \n The Details of funds allocated and expenditure incurred in the last four year on conservation    \nand preservation of centrally protected monuments in Bihar   \n  \n   YEAR 2019-20                                                                                                 (Amount in Rs.) \nSl. \nNo. \nName of the work Allocation Expenditure  \n \n1. Conservation of Newly exposed structure of \nBuddhist stupa at Kesariya, East Champaran. \n5,80,964.00 5,80,964.00 \n2. Conservation of  Excavated remains at Raja Vishal \nKa Garh at Vaishali. \n4,55,184.00 4,55,184.00 \n3. Structural repairs conservation of Monastic cell at \nExcavated remains Vikramshila Mahavihar at \nAntichak, Bhagalpur. \n15,98,133.00 15,98,133.00 \n4. Conservation  of Jarasandh Ka Akhara at Rajgir, \nNalanda. \n4,99,827.00 4,99,827.00 \n5. Debris clearance & restoration of monastery north \neast direction at Antichak, Vikramshila, Bhagalpur. \n44,101.00 44,101.00 \n6. Conservation of Hindu Temple Jain temple and \nvotive stupa complex at Antichak ,Bhagalpur, \n87,793.00 87,793.00 \n7. General maintenance & conservation at Excavated \nremains at Nalanda. \n3,87,871.00 3,87,871.00 \n8. Comprehensive conservation work of Identified \nMonument temple No.3 Excavated remains at \nNalanda. \n22,323.00 22,323.00 \n9. Conservation of ladies compartment of Ancestral \nHouse of Dr. Rajendra Prasad at Zeradei, Siwan. \n41,932.00 41,932.00 \n \n \n \n   YEAR 2020-21        (Amount in Rs.) \nSl. \nNo. \nName of the work Allocation  Expenditure  \n \n1. Conservation of Newly expose structure at Kesariya, \nEast Champaran. \n47,500.00 47,500.00 \n2. Comprehensive conservation work of identified \nmonument Temple no 03 of Excavated remains at \nNalanda \n27,52,996.00 27,52,996.00 \n3. Conservation of Mundeshwari Devi Temple at Paura \nHill, Ramgarh, Kaimur. \n2,620.00 2,620.00 \n4. Conservation of ladies compartment of Ancestral house \nof Dr. Rajendra Pd. at Jiradei. \n4,95,565.00 4,95,565.00 \n5. Conservation of Arogya Vihar at Kumhrar, Patna. 2,601.00 2,601.00 \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \nYEAR 2021-22        (Amount in Rs.) \nSl. \nNo. \nName of the work Allocation Expenditure  \nDuring \n  \n \n1. Comprehensive conservation work of identified \nmonument Temple no 03 of Excavated remains at \nNalanda \n7,31,464.00 7,31,464.00 \n2. Conservation of lower portion of northern side temple \nNo. 13 at Nalanda. \n11,89,235.00 11,89,235.00 \n3. Conservation of  Sarai Mound at Excavated remains at \nNalanda \n12,00,927.00 12,00,927.00 \n4. Conservation of damaged brick masonry & fallen wall of \nMonastery No. 09 at Excavated remains, Nalanda \n7,80,040.00 7,80,040.00 \n5. Conservation of side portion of Temple No. 12 at \nExcavated remains, Nalanda \n7,07,608.00 7,07,608.00 \n6. Conservation of Monastery No. 08 at Nalanda 10,16,445.00 10,16,445.00 \n7. Comprehensive development phase -1  Excavated \nremains at Antichak , Bhagalpur \n3,53,960.00 3,53,960.00 \n8. Conservation of Arogya Vihar at Excavated remains at \nKumhrar, Patna \n6,67,462.00 6,67,462.00 \n9. Conservation of Newly Expose structure of Buddhist \nStupa at Kesariya, East Champaran \n30,39,262.00 30,39,262.00 \n10. Conservation of ancient drainage & development of \npathway Excavated remains at Antichak, Bhagalpur. \n19,11,999.00 19,11,999.00 \n11. Conservation of Temple No. 03 at Nalanda  5,610.00  5,610.00 \n12. Conservation of Temple No. 14 at Nalanda  2,500.00  2,500.00 \n13. Conservation of D"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |259", "t": "Prevalence of Diabetes Cases in Kerala", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nDEPARTMENTOF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 259\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21st JULY,2023\nPREV ALENCEOF DIABETES CASES IN KERALA\n259. SHRI V .K.SREEKANDAN:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether it is a fact that according to the Indian Council of Medical Research and India Diabetes\n(ICMR-INDIAB) study, Kerala figures amongst the three States with the worst overall indicators\nand if so, the details thereof;\n(b) whether Kerala is perhaps one State which is showing up consistently in the red in the nation’s\nmetabolic health report;\n(c) whether it is also true that the State of Kerala is also facing a high prevalence of other non-\ncommunicable diseases and if so, the details thereof;\n(d) whether the Government is considering sending a team of medical experts to oversee the\nsituation there; and\n(e) if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATEIN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\n(PROF. SATYAPALSINGH BAGHEL)\n(a) to (e): As per Indian Council of Medical Research - India Diabetes (ICMR-INDIAB) study, the\nprevalence of diabetes in Kerala is 24%, which is one of the highest figures in the country.\nAccording to the ICMR study Report “India: Health of the Nation's States”, Non-Communicable\nDiseases contributed 74.6% of the total burden of diseases in Kerala. The detailed report is\navailable at the following link-\nhttps://www.healthdata.org/sites/default/files/files/policy_report/2017/India_Health_of_the_Nation\n%27s_States_Report_2017.pdf\nThe Department of Health & Family Welfare, Government of India, provides technical and\nfinancial support to the States/UTs under the National Programme for Prevention and Control of\nNon-Communicable Diseases (NP-NCD), as part of National Health Mission (NHM), based on the\nproposals received from the States/UTs and subject to the resource envelope. The programme\nfocusses on strengthening infrastructure, human resource development, health promotion &\nawareness generation for prevention, early diagnosis, management and referral to an appropriate\nlevel of healthcare facility for treatment of the Non-Communicable Diseases (NCDs). For activities\nupto district level and below, States are given financial assistance under NHM in the ratio of 60:40\n\n(90:10 in case of NE and hilly States). In Kerala, 14 District NCD Clinics, 12 Cardiac Care Units,\n24 District Day Care Centres, and 227 Community Health Center NCD Clinics have been setup.\nA population-based initiative for prevention, control and screening for common NCDs i.e. diabetes,\nhypertension and common cancers has been rolled out in the country under NHM and also as a part\nof Comprehensive Primary Health Care. Under the initiative, persons more than 30 years of age are\ntargeted for their screening. Screening of these common NCDs is an integral part of service delivery\nunder Ayushman Bharat – Health and Wellness Centres.\nPreventive aspect of NCDs is strengthened under Comprehensive Primary Health Care through\nAyushman Bharat Health Wellness Centre scheme, by promotion of wellness activities and targeted\ncommunication at the community level. Other initiatives for increasing public awareness about\nNCDs and for promotion of healthy lifestyle includes observation of International & National\nHealth Days and use of print, electronic and social media for continued community awareness.\nFurthermore, healthy eating is also promoted through FSSAI. Fit India movement is implemented\nby Ministry of Youth Affairs and Sports, and various Yoga related activities are carried out by\nMinistry of AYUSH. In addition, NP-NCD gives financial support under NHM for awareness\ngeneration (IEC) activities for Cardiovascular Diseases to be undertaken by the States/UTs as per\ntheir Programme Implementation Plans (PIPs).\nRegular review meetings are conducted with all States/UTs including Kerala under NP-NCD from\ntime to time. The Common Review Mission is also conducted every year to evaluate the\nprogrammes under NHM including NP-NCD.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |26", "t": "Smart Road Projects", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 26 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON JULY 20, 2023 \n \nSMART ROAD PROJECTS \nNO. 26.  SHRI HIBI EDEN: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN  AFFAIRS be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)  whether Smart Road projects under Smart Cities Mission (SCM) have \nbeen completed in Cochin and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b)  whether Smart Road projects are proposed/ongoing in Cochin and if so, \nthe details thereof along with estimated cost, names of t he roads and \nthe time by which these are likely to be completed; \n \n(c)  whether the Government has assessed the impact of completed Smart \nRoad projects under SCM in Ernakulam district and if so, the details \nthereof; and \n \n(d)  whether the Government has undertaken any measures to ensure the \nsafety and sustainability of  Smart Road projects at Cochin and if so, \nthe details thereof? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n \n(a) to (d) : Cochin Smart Mission Limited (CSML) has undertaken one project \neach in Ernakulam and West Kochi for development of Smart  Roads as \ndetailed below :- \n \n \n \n…2/ - \n \n \n \n \n\n-2- \n \n \ni)   Development of Smart Roads in Ernakulam  :- CSML has awarded the \nproject of “Development of Smart Roads” in Ernakulam at a cost of ₹ 62.90  \ncrore in June, 2019. The project involves development of Durbar Hall Road  \n(0.53 km.), Park Avenue Road (1.47 km.), Chinmaya &  South Railway Station \nRoad (0.50 Km part works), Shanmugham Road (0.89 km.), Abraham  \nMadamakkal Road (1.11 km.) and Banerji Road (0.91 km.) and part works on \nJos Junction to Chittoor road stretch.  This work included upgradation and \naugmentation of carriageway, footpath, pavements, minor bridges, providing \ncycle lanes (shared lanes based on availability of space), road markings, \nbus bay, street landscaping works, construction of ducts for undergrounding \nof utilities, construction of drains  shifting of utilities/undergrounding of \nutilities, upgradation of electric infrastructure, LED street lighting, bus \nshelters (pilot project), signages, and marking of parking spaces based on \navailability of space.  The project has been grounded and the completed \nroads would be handed over to Public Works Department (PWD) after Defect \nLiability Period (DLP) of 3 years is over.  \n \nii)   Development of Smart Roads in West Kochi :- These roads have similar \nfeatures like the Smart R oads in Ernakulam. The project is  being \nimplemented in KB Jacob Road (1.08 km.), Amaravathi Road (1.09 km.),  \nBellar Road & River Road (0.43 km.) and Kalvathy Road (0.64 km.).  \n \nThese projects prioritize safe and well-lit pedestrian facilities. CSML is \nconducting programs to promote active and safe streets/roads for all  \nstakeholders. Under “Smart Cities And Academia Towards Action And  \nResearch - SAAR 2.0”, CSML proposes to associate with I nstitutes to take \nup the impact assessment. Under the different initiatives of MoHUA, CSML  \nhas started campaigns to sensitize citizens  about the importance of safety \nof pedestrians on roads and footpaths. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |260", "t": "Centres for Diseases Control", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 260\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2023\nCENTRES FOR DISEASES CONTROL\n260. SHRI BALAK NATH:\nWill the Minister of HEALTHAND FAMILYWELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government is formulating any scheme for health programmes under the\nCentres for Disease Control in Rajasthan;\n(b) whether the Government has formulated any mechanism to review the programmes under\nNational Centres for Disease Control and if so, the details thereof; and\n(c) the details of the outcome of the latest review to ascertain the efficacy of these projects?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATEIN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILYWELFARE\n(PROF. SATYAPAL SINGH BAGHEL)\n(a) to (c):\nNational Centre for Disease Control (NCDC) under Ministry of Health & Family Welfare supports\nStates/UTs in management of communicable and infectious prone diseases. It works towards\nstrengthening the disease surveillance system, outbreak investigations and provide required\ntechnical support to States/UTs in managing any health emergency. NCDC also provide technical\nguidance & support to states in terms of implementation of national action plan on containment of\nAntimicrobial Resistance (AMR), surveillance of Viral Hepatitis, One Health approach for\nprevention and control of zoonoses, Rabies control, prevention and control of Leptospirosis and\nissues related to Climate Change & Health. All these activities are coordinated across all\nStates/UTs including Rajasthan.\nBesides monitoring through the Output-Outcome Monitoring Framework by NITI Aayog, all\nprogrammes are monitored through regular reviews at senior level in the Ministry.\n****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |261", "t": "Objectives of Poshan Abhiyaan", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 261 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2023 \n \nOBJECTIVES OF POSHAN ABHIYAAN \n \n261     SHRI MAHESH SAHOO: \n           SHRI JAGANNATH SARKAR: \n \nWill the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)    whether the Government has been able to achieve the objectives of Poshan \nAbhiyaan in Odisha and West Bengal, If so, the details thereof and if not, the \nreasons therefor; \n \n(b)    the number of Anganwadi Centres in Odisha and West Bengal wherein Poshan \nscheme is being implemented, district-wise; and \n \n(c)    whether the Government has conducted any study on the results of this scheme \nin Odisha and West Bengal, if so, the details and outcome thereof? \n   \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOMENT \n(SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n  \n(a)    The objective of   POSHAN Abhiyaan is to address challenges of malnutrition \nand contribute to India's human capital development by using effective strategies to \naddress nutritional deficiencies and improve the nutritional & health status of children \nfrom 0-6 years, Adolescent Girls, Pregna nt Women and Lactating Mothers in a time \nbound manner. A total of 43.87 lakh beneficiaries from State of Odisha and 88.34 lakh \nbeneficiaries from West Bengal including pregnant women, lactating mothers, children \nunder 6 years of age and Adolescent girls ar e registered under Mission Poshan 2.0, \nwhich includes Anganwadi Services, Scheme for Adolescent Girls and Poshan \nAbhiyan. \n \nThe estimated number of underweight, malnourished and severely \nmalnourished children under 5 years of age is obtained under National Family Health \nSurvey (NFHS) conducted by the Ministry of Health & Family Welfare. As per the \nrecent report of NFHS-5 (2019-21), the nutrition indicators for children under 5 years \nhave improved as compared with NFHS -4 (2015 -16). Stunting has reduced from \n38.4% to 35.5%, Wasting has reduced from 21.0% to 19.3% and Underweight \nprevalence has reduced from 35.8% to 32.1%.  As per data recorded in Poshan \nTracker, ICT application for Mission Poshan 2.0, close to 7 crore children were \nmeasured in the country in the month of June itself, of which, 7% were SAM and 19% \nunderweight, which is significantly lower than NFHS indicators.  \n \n\n \n \n \nIn the state of Odisha, as per Poshan  Tracker, wasting in the month of June \n2023 was at 3% and underweight at 15% in 29.6 lakh children measured during the \nmonth (compared to 18.1% and 29.7% respectively as per NFHS -5). In the state of \nWest Bengal, as per Poshan Tracker, wasting in the month of June 2023 was at 8% \nand underweight at 14% in 61.91 lakh children measured during the month (compared \nto 20.3% and 32.2% respectively as per NFHS-5). \n \n(b)    The details of district wise number of AWC in Odisha and West Bengal \nregistered in Poshan Tracker, is at Annexure I. \n \n(c)    NITI Aayog was mandated to conduct monitoring and evaluation periodically to \nassess the progress and impact of the programme, nationally. A sectoral evaluation \nof POSHAN Abhiyaan, among other Centrally Sponsored Schemes, was c onducted \nby Development Monitoring & Evaluation Office (DMEO) under NITI Aayog for impact \nevaluation of POSHAN Abhiyaan. The performances of Poshan Abhiyan was found \nSatisfactory in the categories of Relevance, Sustainability and Equity  \n  \n**** \n  \n\nAnnexure I \n \nState District Name Operational AWC \nOdisha Angul 1684 \nOdisha BARGARH 2919 \nOdisha Baleshwar 4220 \nOdisha Bhadrak 2435 \nOdisha Bolangir 2775 \nOdisha Boudh 727 \nOdisha Cuttack 3318 \nOdisha Deogarh 798 \nOdisha Dhenkanal 2220 \nOdisha Gajapati 2436 \nOdisha Ganjam 5161 \nOdisha Jagatsinghpur 1840 \nOdisha Jajapur 2826 \nOdisha Jharsuguda 967 \nOdisha KALAHANDI 2362 \nOdisha KENDRAPARA 2147 \nOdisha KHORDHA 2554 \nOdisha Kandhamal 2197 \nOdisha Keonjhar 3352 \nOdisha Koraput 3399 \nOdisha MALKANGIRI 1324 \nOdisha MAYURBHANJ 5153 \nOdisha Nabarangpur 2331 \nOdisha Nayagarh 1584 \nOdisha Nuapada 1406 \nOdisha Puri 2599 \nOdisha RAYAGADA 2063 \nOdisha SUBARNAPUR 1556 \nOdisha Sambalpur 1886 \nOdisha Sundergarh 3918 \nWest Bengal 24 Paraganas North 10428 \nWest Bengal Alipurduar 3198 \nWest Bengal BANKURA 5739 \nWest Bengal BIRBHUM 5188 \nWest Bengal COOCHBEHAR 5531 \nWest Bengal DARJEELING 3252 \nWest Bengal DINAJPUR DAKSHIN 4154 \nWest Bengal HOOGHLY 6708 \nWest Bengal Howrah 4509 \nWest Bengal JALPAIGURI 3940 \nWest Bengal Jhargram 2478 \nWest Bengal Kalimpong 639 \nWest Bengal Kolkata 1529 \nWest Bengal MALDAH 6399 \nWest Bengal MEDINIPUR WEST 6643 \nWest Bengal MURSHIDABAD 9033 \nWest Bengal NADIA 6934 \nWest Bengal PASCHIM BARDHAMAN 2845 \nWest Bengal PURBA BARDHAMAN 6854 \nWest Bengal PURULIA 4945 \nWest Bengal Purba Medinipur 6343 \nWest Bengal South 24 Parganas 11366 \nWest Bengal Uttar Dinajpur 3787 \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |262", "t": "Research in AYUSH Sector", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF AYUSH\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 262\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21ST JULY,2023\n“RESEARCH IN AYUSH SECTOR”\n† 262. SHRI PARBATBHAISA V ABHAIPATEL:\nSHRIMATI GEETA KORA:\nSHRI PRADEEP KUMAR SINGH:\nWill the Minister of AYUSH be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government proposes to strengthen the evidence based scientific research\nin the field of AYUSH;\n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the efforts being made for the same; and\n(c) the measures taken/proposed to be taken by the Government towards strengthening\nthe research capabilities, educational facilities and evidence based research in the country\nparticularly in Gujarat, Jharkhand and Bihar?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF AYUSH\n(SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL)\n(a) & (b) Ministry of Ayush has a mandate for coordination and promotion of research and\ndevelopment including assistance therefor in Ayush systems of Health Care.\nMinistry has five Research Councils namely Central Council for Research in\nAyurvedic Sciences (CCRAS), Central Council for Research in Yoga and\nNaturopathy (CCRYN), Central Council for Research in Siddha (CCRS), Central\nCouncil for Research in Unani Medicine (CCRUN) and Central Council for\nResearch in Homoeopathy (CCRH) as apex bodies for the formulation,\ncoordination, development and promotion of research on scientific lines in their\nrespective field.\nMinistry of Ayush also runs Ayurgyan Scheme (Central Sector Schemes)\nwhich has research and innovation as component of scheme. This research and\ninnovation component (erstwhile Extra Mural Research Scheme) of Ayurgyan\nscheme was introduced to tap the potential of medical institutes, scientific research\n& development institutions, Universities and organizations for the research needs\nof Ayush sector, with an aim to expand the ambit of research in Ayush systems.\nAll India Institute of Ayurveda (AIIA), New Delhi, National Institute of\nSiddha, Chennai, National Institute of Homoeopathy, Kolkata, National Institute of\nUnani Medicine, Bangalore, National Commission for Indian System of Medicine,\n\nNew Delhi are also contributing towards evidence based scientific research in their\nrespective field.\n(c) Institute of Teaching and Research in Ayurveda (ITRA), Jamnagar is one of the\npremier Teaching and Research centres for Ayurveda in the country. It is the first\ninstitute under Ayush which has been designated as Institute of National\nImportance. This has also been designated as WHO collaborating centre for\ntraditional Medicines (Ayurveda).\nRegional Ayurveda Research Institute (RARI), Ahmedabad and Regional Ayurveda\nResearch Institute (RARI), Patna in the States of Gujarat and Bihar are peripheral\ncentres under CCRAS respectively. The RARI, Ahmedabad is entrusted with\nClinical Research on the management of Skin Disorders through Ayurveda with\nscientific footings. The RARI, Patna has mandate to undertake research on\ninfectious diseases. It also provides research-oriented health care services through\ngeneral OPD and geriatric clinic to cater to the health care services of the people.\nCCRH has established two research units in Jharkhand and Bihar namely\nClinical Research Unit (Homoeopathy), Ranchi and Clinical Verification Unit\n(Homoeopathy), Patna. One Regional Research Unit of CCRUM is also\nfunctioning at Patna. A number of educational institutions/Colleges have also been\nestablished in Gujrat, Jharkhand and Bihar.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |263", "t": "Urea Subsidy Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF PANCHAYATI RAJ \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED DIARY NO. *263 \nANSWERED ON: 08.08.2023 \nPERFORMANCE OF STATES \n \n*263. MS. DEBASREE CHAUDHURI: \n \n   Will the Minister of Panchayati Raj be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of the performance of States with reference to the work done in the major \nprogrammes of the Ministry; \n(b) the factors attributable for the satisfactory performance of State, if any; \n(c) the reasons for unsatisfactory performance of some States in this regard; if any; and  \n(d) the details of steps being undertaken to improve the performance of the States with \nreference to these major programmes of the Ministry?  \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF PANCHAYATI RAJ \n(SHRI GIRIRAJ SINGH) \n \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (d) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.  263 ANSWERED ON 08.08.2023 REGARDING \nPERFORMANCE OF STATES  \n___________________________________________________________________________ \n \n(a) The State-wise performance of the Centrally Sponsored Scheme of revamped Rastriya \nGram Swaraj Abhiyan (RGSA) for capacity building and training of the elected representatives of \nthe Panchayati Raj Institutions (PRIs) and the Central Sector Scheme of Survey of Villages and \nMapping with Improvised Technology in Village Areas (SVAMITVA) for  providing ������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������ are given \nin Annexure-I and Annexure-II respectively.   \n(b) Close and intensive monitoring of the implementation of the scheme through meetings, \nvideo-conferences, regular feedback, dashboard data, demand for timely submission of utilisation \nof funds  released, effective coordination with stakeholders etc. are the factors for satisfactory \nperformance.   \n(c) During the implementation of scheme of SVAMITVA issues like delay in providing \nsufficient number of Chuna Marked villages by States, insufficient trained manpower to carry out \nthe activities, extreme weat her conditions, difficult terrain in some States, delay in drone flying \npermission in areas around airports, international border are some of the factors that affect the \nperformance of the States under the SVAMITVA scheme. \n(d) The States are being closely monitored through meetings, video-conferences, dashboard data \netc. to improve the ir performance for effective implementation of the scheme s and utilisation of \nfunds. A suitable mix of virtual and face to face mode of training has been adopted for various \ntarget groups for capacity building and training of the elected representatives and other \nstakeholders of rural local bodes who come from varied socio -economic and educational \nbackgrounds. States/ UTs have also been suggested to prepare tailored audio -visual training \nmaterials and modules such as animation videos, audios, e -posters etc. for different target groups \nto ensure effectiveness of the trainings.  \n*** \n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |264", "t": "‘Heal by India’ and ‘Heal in India’ Initiatives", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 264 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 8th AUGUST, 2023 \n \nPATENTED PESTICIDE PRODUCTS \n \n*264.  SHRI RAJIV PRATAP RUDY: \n        \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE कृ ͪष एवं ͩकसान कãयाण मंğी \nbe pleased to state:- \n(a) whether it is a fact that the country has optimum share of patented pesticide products; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(c) the details of new pesticide products registered in last five years and the share of patent \nproducts; and \n(d) the steps taken by the Government to promote innovation in agriculture sector? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nकृ ͪष एवं ͩकसान कãयाण मंğी  (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) to (d):   A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 264 DUE FOR REPLY ON 8TH AUGUST, 2023. \n \n(a) to (c): The manufacture, import, storage, sale, distribution, use, etc. of pesticides is \nregulated by the Insecticides Act, 1968 (The Act) and the Insecticides Rules, 1971 (The \nRules) made thereunder. The Registration Committee (RC) constituted under section (5) of \nthe Insecticides Act, 1968 registers the pesticides for their use in agriculture and also for \nnon-agriculture purposes, after evaluating their safety and efficacy. There is no provision \nenshrined in the Act and the Rules regarding patent of pesticides therefore, patent issue is \nnot a consideration before RC for deciding on the registration. \n \nRC has registered 330 Technical and 867 Formulations of pesticides for use in the \ncountry. Out of 330 technical and 867 formulated pesticides, 11 technical and 110 \nformulations are under patent. During the last five years 44 new pesticides have been \nregistered by the RC. Out of these 4 pesticides are under patent. \n(d): Section 38(2) of the Insecticides Act, 1968 provides that the Central Government \nmay exempt any educational, scientific or research organization engaged in carrying out \nexperiments with insecticides from the provisions of the Act. Accordingly, to promote the \ninnovation in agriculture in reference to pesticides sector Government of India has \nexempted following institutes/ agencies which are accredited by the National Accreditation \nBoard for Accredited Laboratories or having Good Laboratory Practices certification with \npesticides in their scope from registration requirements to import pesticides for research \npurposes:  \n Indian Council for Agricultural Research;  \n State Agriculture Universities;  \n Institutions of Department of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare;  \n Department of Science and Technology;  \n Department of Scientific and Industrial Research;  \n And also any other Government or private laboratory or pesticide industry \nrecognised by the Department of Scientific and Industrial Research or the \nDepartment of Science and Technology, Ministry of Science and Technology. \nBesides, Central Government is also encouraging Research in Biopesticide \ndevelopment.  Guidelines on biopesticide's consortia registration have been developed. \nFifteen different types of Biopesticides developed by ICAR / Industry have been approved \nfor the management of pests on various crops. \nFurther, use of Drones for crop protection in India is new and the country is \ngaining experience. The Government has granted an interim approval for use of all the \nregistered pesticides formulation by drones whose safety and efficacy has already been \nevaluated and used in the country on different crops. The Government has also released \nStandard Operating Procedure (SOP) for use of Drone for Crop Protection.  \n*** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |265", "t": "Central Schemes in Odisha", "head": "����������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n��������������������\n��������������������������������������������