ETI PADHAO SCHEME IN GUJARAT \n  \n  \n 363.  SHRI RATANSINH MAGANSINH RATHOD: \n  SHRI PATEL HASMUKHBHAI SOMABHAI: \n  \nWill the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Beti Bachao Beti Padhao scheme is being implemented in Gujarat;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof, district-wise; and  \n(c)  the details of funds  allotted for the said scheme in the State of Gujarat, district -\nwise? \n  \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n (SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n  \n(a) & (b):    Yes, Sir. In the XV Finance Commission period, Beti Bachao Beti Padhao \n(BBBP)  is being implemented, as a component of Sambal Sub scheme under the Mission \nShakti, in all the districts of the country including districts in the State of Gujarat. \n  \n(c)    The funds under BBBP has been allocated in 3 categories  based on the SRB(Sex \nRatio at Birth) status of districts  as per Health Management Information System (HMIS) \ndata of Ministry of Health and Family Welfare for the Financial Year 2020 -2021. The \ndetails of fund allotted under BBBP in the State of Gujarat, district-wise  is at Annexure. \n \n**** \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n\nAnnexure \n \nStatement referred to in reply to Part (c) of Lok Sabha Admitted Question No. 363 \nfor 21.07.2023 regarding Implementation of Beti Bachao Beti Padhao Scheme in \nGujarat raised by SHRI RATANSINH MAGANSINH RATHOD and  SHRI PATEL \nHASMUKHBHAI SOMABHAI \n \n*** \nS No. Name of district SRB 2020-21 as \nper HMIS \nAnnual Amount \nallocated  based on \nSRB 2020-21 \n(Rs in lakhs ) \n1 Sabar Kantha 868 40 \n2 Amreli 877 40 \n3 Ahmadabad 880 40 \n4 Botad 889 40 \n5 Mahesana 893 40 \n6 Anand 897 40 \n7 Rajkot 900 40 \n8 Bhavnagar 907 40 \n9 Surat 907 40 \n10 Junagadh 916 40 \n11 Gir Somnath 916 40 \n12 Mahisagar 919 30 \n13 Panch Mahals 921 30 \n14 Gandhinagar 921 30 \n15 Bharuch 922 30 \n16 Dang 924 30 \n17 Vadodara 925 30 \n18 Valsad 927 30 \n19 Patan 930 30 \n20 Narmada 931 30 \n21 Navsari 934 30 \n22 Chhotaudepur 935 30 \n23 Surendranagar 938 30 \n24 Porbandar 938 30 \n25 Dohad 940 30 \n26 Arvalli 943 30 \n27 Devbhumi Dwarka 943 30 \n28 Tapi 945 30 \n29 Banas Kantha 952 30 \n30 Kachchh 957 20 \n31 Kheda 957 20 \n32 Jamnagar 960 20 \n33 Morbi 978 20 \n\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |364", "t": "Prevalence of Polypharmacy", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nDEPARTMENTOF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.364\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 21st JULY,2023\nPrevalence of Polypharmacy\n364. SHRI P.C. MOHAN:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government is aware of the prevalence of polypharmacy among the elderly\npopulation;\n(b) if so, the details and statistics regarding the same;\n(c) the measures taken/proposed to be taken by the Government to create awareness among\nthe healthcare professionals regarding the risks and challenges associated with\npolypharmacy in the elderly and if so, the details thereof;\n(d) whether the Ministry has conducted/proposed to conduct any studies/research to assess\nthe impact of polypharmacy on the health and well-being of the elderly population and\nif so, the details thereof in this regard; and\n(e) whether the Government has plans to monitor and evaluate the effectiveness of\ninterventions implemented to reduce polypharmacy in the elderly population and if so,\nthe details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATEIN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY\nWELFARE\n(PROF. S.P. SINGH BAGHEL)\n(a) to (e)\nPolypharmacy is prevalent in India. A systematic review published in Frontiers in\npharmacology in 2021 found that 49% of older Indian adults (aged 60 years and above) were\nexperiencing polypharmacy. Polypharmacy refers to the concomitant use of 5 or more\nmedications.\nThe training modules for Medical Officers and CHO for Elderly Care, developed by National\nHealth System Resource Centre under this Ministry, are comprised of the assessment tool on\n\npolypharmacy by the elderly patients which include the awareness on adverse effects\nassociated with polypharmacy.\nIndian Council of Medical Research has mandate to conduct Research/Study to assess the\nimpact of Polypharmacy etc. in Geriatric Population.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |365", "t": "AIIMS, Kozhikode", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nDEPARTMENTOF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO-365\nTO BE ANSWERED ON- 21/07/2023\nAIIMS, KOZHIKODE\n365. SHRI M.K. RAGHA V AN:\nWill the Minister of HEALTHAND FAMILYWELFARE be pleased to state:-\n(a) the details of the present status of All India Institute of Medical Sciences (AIIMS) being\nconsidered at Kinalur at Kozhikode in Kerala and if so, the details thereof;\n(b) whether the Government has received any communication from the State Government\nregarding the completion of land acquisition for setting up of AIIMS at Kozhikode Kerala and if\nso, the details thereof;\n(c) whether the Government has sanctioned any additional funds for development of various\nhospitals at Kerala including at Kozhikode and if so, the details thereof;\n(d) the details of the funds utilized and allocated under National Health Mission in Kerala\nincluding Kozhikode;\n(e) whether any funds granted for Health department of Kerala has been found lapsed and if so,\nthe details of funds granted and utilized by the State since 2019;\n(f) whether the Government proposes to provide more funds to the State to revamp health\ninfrastructure and if so, the details thereof; and\n(g) whether the Government is aware of the fact that rising cost of medical treatments in the\ncountry varies from hospital to hospital and if so, the necessary steps being taken to ensure a\nuniform cost of treatment across the country for various diseases?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATEIN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY\nWELFARE\n(DR. BHARATI PRA VINPAWAR)\n(a) to (b) Under Pradhan Mantri Swasthya Suraksha Yojana (PMSSY) Scheme AIIMS are set up\nin different parts of the country, keeping in view the gaps in availability of tertiary health care\nfacilities, in a phased manner. Till date, 22 AIIMS have been sanctioned under the Scheme.\nGovernment of Kerala has identified & proposed four sites at following locations for the\nestablishment of new AIIMS in the state:. Kinaloor in Kozhikode district, Kattakada Taluk in\n\nThiruvananthapuram district, Kottayam district and Ernakulam district.The proposal has not been\napproved in the current phase of PMSSY .\n(c) to (f) Government of India has taken upgradation of the following Government Medical\nColleges in the State of Kerala under PMSSY Scheme by way of construction of Super\nSpeciality Blocks as per details below-\nSl. Name of Govt. Medical College\nCentral Share (Rs. in Crore)\nApproved Released\n1 Thiruvananthapuram Medical College 100 96.26\n2 Government Medical College, Kozhikode 120 90.90\n3 TD Medical College, Alappuzha 120 99.53\n4\nSree Chitra Tirunal Institute of Medical\nSciences & Technology,Trivandrum 230 180.94\nTotal fund released 570 467.63\nDetails of Central Release and Expenditure of National Health Mission for the State of Kerala\nfrom the FY 2019-20 to FY 2022-23 is as follows:\n(Rs in crore)\nName\nof the\nState\nFY 2019-20 FY 2020-21 FY 2021-22 FY 2022-23\nCentra\nl\nReleas\ne\nExpend\niture\nCentral\nReleas\ne\nExpendit\nure\nCentral\nRelease\nExpendit\nure\nCentral\nRelease\nExpendit\nure\nKerala 836.14 1023.9\n4\n788.22 1457.75 771.47 1230.96 1036.76 1592.96\nNote:\n1. The above releases relate to Central Govt. Grants & do not include State share\ncontribution.\n2. Expenditure includes expenditure against Central Release, State Release & unspent\nbalances at the beginning of the year. Expenditure is as per available Financial\nManagement Reports submitted by the State.\nGovernment of India has implemented release of funds through Treasury Single Account (TSA)\nfor all Central sector and Centrally Sponsored Schemes (CSS) wherein unutilized assignments\nwill lapse to the Government at the close of the financial year and will not be available for\nexpenditure in the next financial year.\n\nUnder the National Health Mission (NHM), this Ministry provides financial and technical\nsupport to the States/ UTs to strengthen their healthcare systems based on the requirements\nprojected by them in their Programme Implementation Plans (PIPs).\n(g) The Government of India has also enacted the Clinical Establishments (Registration and\nRegulation) Act, 2010 (CE Act, 2010) and notified Clinical Establishments (Central Government)\nRules, 2012 thereunder to provide for registration and regulation of Government (except those of\nArmed Forces) as well as private clinical establishments belonging to recognized systems of\nmedicine. The States/Union Territories (UTs) which have adopted CE Act, 2010, are primarily\nresponsible for regulating their hospitals including private hospitals as per provisions of the Act\nand rules thereunder to ensure provision of affordable and quality healthcare to patients. As per\nthe Act, the clinical establishments are required to fulfill the conditions of minimum standards of\nfacilities and services, minimum requirement of personnel, maintenance of records & reports and\nother conditions including ensuring compliance to Standard Treatment Guidelines (STGs) issued\nby the Central/State Governm"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |366", "t": "Schemes for Improvement in Judicial System", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LAW & JUSTICE \nDEPARTMENT OF JUSTICE \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 366 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FRIDAY, THE 21ST JULY, 2023 \n \nSchemes for Improvement in Judicial System \n \n†366. SHRI SATYADEV PACHAURI: \n \nWill the Minister of LAW AND JUSTICE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of schemes started by the Government to improve judicial system \nduring the last three years, year-wise;  \n(b) the details of targets set and achievements made under each of the said \nschemes during the said period;  \n(c) the details of proposa ls received from the State of Uttar Pradesh and approved \nby the Union Government in this regard; and  \n(d) the details of funds allocated and utilized for the implementation of these \nschemes during each of the last three years and the current year, scheme and State-\nwise? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE \nMINISTRY OF LAW AND JUSTICE; MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \nMINISTRY OF PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS; AND MINISTER OF \nSTATE IN THE MINISTRY OF CULTURE \n(SHRI ARJUN RAM MEGHWAL) \n \n \n(a) to (d) : The Department of Justice is implementing several schemes like \nCentrally Sponsored Scheme (CSS) for Judicial Infrastructure, Gram Nyayalayas, \nFast Track Special Courts and  Central Sector Scheme of e Courts for \nimprovement in Judicial System but all these schemes are more than 3years old. \n\n During the period of last 3 years, Department of Justice has launched a \nscheme on Access to Justice titled “Designing Innovative Solutions for Holistic \nAccess to Justice in India”, for a period of five years (2021 -2026), with a total \nbudget of Rs 250 Crore. The scheme aims to provide citizen -centric delivery of \nlegal services with use of technology. It has three components which includes \nTele- Law: Mainstreaming Legal Aid to the Grassroots; Nyaya Bandhu (Pro \nBono L egal Services) and Pan India legal literacy and legal awaren ess \nprogramme. The Tele -Law aims to strengthen pre -litigation advice and \nconsultation, between the citizens and Panel Lawyers. The Tele -Law service is \navailable across 2.5 lakh Gram Panchayats acr oss the country at the Common \nService Centers. It is also available via Tele -Law Citizen Mobile APP (available \non Android & iOS platform) and UMANG portal. As on 30th June,2023 the Tele-\nLaw has enabled advice to 46 lakh beneficiaries.  \nThe Nyaya Bandhu (Pr o Bono Legal Services) program provides to create a \ndecentralized pan India dispensation framework to deliver Pro Bono legal \nservices. It aims to connect registered Pro Bono lawyers with registered \nbeneficiaries through Nyaya Bandhu Mobile APP (available o n Android & iOS \nplatform) and UMANG portal. As on 30th June,2023 10,241 Lawyers have \nenrolled on the Nyaya Bandhuprogramme. Pro Bono Clubs has been started under \n69 Law schools, across the country to instill the young legal minds with Pro Bono \nlegal services.  \nIn addition, to ensure legal empowerment of citizens, Department of Justice is \nimplementing Pan India legal literacy and legal awareness programme, through \n\nvarious State agencies and National Law school’s etc. Approx. 4 Lakh \nparticipants has benefitte d through various initiatives of legal literacy and legal \nawareness programmes being implemented under it (both online and physical \nmode). \nEspecially in the State of Uttar Pradesh, Tele -Law service is operational \nacross 19,560 Common Service Centers and has enabled advice to 8 lakh \nbeneficiaries in 75 districts, till 30th June 2023. 649 Lawyers have joined the \nNyaya Bandhu Programme and 6 Law  colleges have constituted Pro Bono Clubs \ntill 30th June 2023. Moreover, DoJ is implementing training and awareness \nprogramme on issues related to gender -based violence in six districts of Uttar \nPradesh which includes Prayagraj, Mathura, Varanasi, Pratapga rh, Aligarh and \nBulandshahr. The project is being implemented by National Law University, \nDelhi. Awareness programmes have been conducted on various topics like \nProtection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005 ; Prevention of Sexual \nHarassment at Workpl ace Act, 2013 ; Prohibition of Child Marriage Act, 200 6; \nCrimes like female foeticide, child sexual offe nces, dowry death, cruelty \netc.Since 2021, 860+ participants including villagers, law enforcing agencies, \ncollege and school students have been covered under the various legal awareness \nprogrammes.  \n Under DISHA SCHEME, during financial year 2021-2022, 2022-2023 and \n2023-2024, funds amounting to Rs. 87 crore, Rs. 40 crore  and Rs. 48.15 crore \nwere sanctioned, out of which Rs. 87 crore, Rs. 39.96 crore and Rs. 47.14 crore \nwere utilized in respective year. \n\n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |367", "t": "Setting up of Medical Colleges", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nDEPARTMENTOF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †367\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 21ST JULY,2023\nSETTING UP OF MEDICAL COLLEGES\n†367. SHRI OMPRAKASH BHUPALSINH ALIAS PAWANRAJENIMBALKAR:\nSHRIMATI SANGEETA AZAD:\nSHRI RAMESH CHANDRA MAJHI:\nSHRI SANJAYJADHA V:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has selected/identified the places for setting up of new medical\ncolleges in the country and if so, the details thereof, State/ UT-wise along with the criteria\nfor setting up new medical colleges;\n(b) the number of medical colleges functional in the country and the number of medical\ncolleges sanctioned and established during the last five years, the current year, particularly\nin Maharashtra and Uttar Pradesh, district-wise and seat-wise;\n(c) whether the academic sessions have commenced in these medical colleges, if so, the details\nthereof, college-wise and if not, the time by which they are likely to be functional;\n(d) whether the Government has any plan for setting up of new medical colleges in Azamgarh,\nNabarangpur Parliamentary Constituencies in Uttar Pradesh, backward areas of Parbhani\nand in each district of the tribal areas in Osmanabad in Government and private sectors; and\n(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATEIN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY\nWELFARE\n(DR. BHARATI PRA VINPAWAR)\n(a) to (e): The Government has increased number of medical colleges and subsequently\nincreased MBBS seats. There is an increase of 82% in medical colleges from 387 before 2014 to\n704 as of now. Further, there is an increase of 110% in MBBS seats from 51,348 before 2014 to\n\n1,07,948 as of now, there is also an increase of 117% in PG seats from 31,185 before 2014 to\n67,802 as of now.\nFurther, the Ministry of Health & Family Welfare administers a Centrally Sponsored\nScheme (CSS) for ‘Establishment of new medical colleges attached with existing district/referral\nhospitals’ with preference to underserved areas and aspirational districts, where there is no\nexisting Government or private medical college with fund sharing between the Centre and State\nGovernments in the ratio of 90:10 for North Eastern and Special Category States and 60:40 for\nothers. As per the Scheme guidelines, the planning, execution and commissioning of the projects\nis to be done by the State Government. Under the Scheme, a total of 157 medical colleges have\nbeen approved in three phases, of which 107 are functional.\nDuring the last five years, 101 medical colleges have been approved under the Scheme,\nof which 55 are functional. In the State of Maharashtra, 01 medical college has been approved\nduring the last five years, which is functional. In the State of Uttar Pradesh, 22 medical colleges\nhave been approved during the last five years, of which 08 are functional. The district-wise\ndetails of medical colleges approved under the Scheme including those in Maharashtra & Uttar\nPradesh are at Annexure.\nIn addition, support has also been provided to the States of Maharashtra and Uttar\nPradesh under the Pradhan Mantri Swasthya Suraksha Yojana (PMSSY) for upgradation of 06\nGovernment medical colleges by setting up of Super Specialty blocks in Maharashtra at Mumbai,\nNagpur, Chhatrapati Sambhajinagar, Latur, Akola & Yavatmal and 11 Government medical\ncolleges in Uttar Pradesh at Lucknow, Varanasi, Aligarh, Jhansi, Gorakhpur, Prayagraj, Meerut,\nAgra & Kanpur. Further, AIIMS at Nagpur, Rae Bareli & Gorakhpur have also been established.\n*****\n\nAnnexure\nDetails of 157 Medical Colleges approved under Centrally Sponsored Scheme for\n‘Establishment of new Medical Colleges attached with existing District/Referral Hospitals’\nS.No State/UT No. Districts Approval\nDate\nFunctional\n(as on 30/06/2023)\n1. A & N Islands 1. Port Blair 29.12.2014 Yes\n2. Arunachal Pradesh 2. Naharlagun 10.03.2015 Yes\n3. Andhra Pradesh\n3. Piduguralla 19.02.2020 No\n4. Paderu 19.02.2020 No\n5. Machilipatnam 19.02.2020 Yes\n4. Assam\n6. Dhubri 27.11.2015 Yes\n7. Nagaon 27.11.2015 Yes\n8. North Lakhimpur 27.11.2015 Yes\n9. Diphu 16.08.2016 Yes\n10. Kokrajhar 19.02.2020 Yes\n5. Bihar\n11. Purnia 27.11.2015 No\n12. Saran (Chhapara) 27.11.2015 No\n13. Samastipur 31.01.2019 No\n14. Sitamarhi 25.09.2019 No\n15. Jhanjharpur 25.09.2019 No\n16. Siwan 21.09.2020 No\n17. Buxar 25.09.2019 No\n18. Jamui 25.09.2019 No\n6. Chhattisgarh\n19. Rajnandgaon 10.03.2015 Yes\n20. Sarguja 10.03.2015 Yes\n21. Korba 19.02.2020 Yes\n22. Mahasamund 19.02.2020 Yes\n23. Kanker 19.02.2020 Yes\n7. Gujarat\n24. Narmada 13.01.2020 Yes\n25. Navsari 13.01.2020 Yes\n26. Panchmahal 28.07.2020 Yes\n27. Porbandar 13.01.2020 Yes\n28. Morbi 26.11.2020 Yes\n8. Himachal Pradesh\n29. Chamba 27.11.2015 Yes\n30. Hamirpur 10.03.2015 Yes\n31. Nahan (Sirmour) 10.0.2015 Yes\n9. Haryana 32. Bhiwani 17.10.2016 No\n10. Jharkhand\n33. Dumka 14.03.2016 Yes\n34. Hazaribagh 14.03.2016 Yes\n35. Palamu (Daltonganj) 14.03.2016 Yes\n36. K"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |368", "t": "AYUSH Hospitals and Pharmacies in Jharkhand", "head": "1\nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF AYUSH\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.368\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21ST JULY,2023\nAYUSH HOSPITALS AND PHARMACIES IN JHARKHAND\n368. SHRI JAYANTSINHA:\nWill the Minister of AYUSH be pleased to state:\n(a) the number of AYUSH hospitals and pharmacies in Jharkhand, district-wise;\n(b) the details of the registered/licensed AYUSH practitioners in Jharkhand, district-wise;\n(c) whether the Government has any plan to enhance accessibility to AYUSH treatment facilities\nin Jharkhand, particularly in Hazaribagh and Ramgarh districts; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF AYUSH\n(SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL)\n(a) The number of Ayush hospitals in Jharkhand, district-wise is furnished at Annexure-I. However, as\nreported by State Government of Jharkhand, no Government pharmacy is available in Jharkhand.\n(b) As reported by the State Government of Jharkhand, the district-wise registered/licensed Ayush\npractitioners in Jharkhand is furnished at Annexure-II.\n(c) & (d) Public Health being a State subject, enhancement of accessibility to Ayush treatment facilities\nanywhere in Jharkhand including Hazaribagh and Ramgarh districts, comes under the purview of State\nGovernment of Jharkhand. However, under Centrally Sponsored Scheme of the National Ayush Mission\n(NAM), there is a provision of financial assistance to States/UTs including Jharkhand for promotion\nand development of Ayush system to enhance accessibility to Ayush treatment facilities. Accordingly,\nState Government may avail financial assistance by submitting suitable proposal through State Annual\nAction Plan (SAAP) as per the provision of NAM guideline. NAM inter-alia makes provisions for the\nfollowing activities:-\n(i) AyushHealth & WellnessCentres\n\n2\n(ii) Co-location of Ayush facilities at Primary Health Centres (PHCs), Community Health Centres\n(CHCs) and District Hospitals (DHs)\n(iii) Upgradation of existing standalone Government AyushHospitals\n(iv) Upgradation of existing Government/ Panchayat/ Government aided Ayush\nDispensaries/Construction of building for existing Ayush Dispensary (Rented/dilapidated\naccommodation)/ Construction of building to establish new Ayush Dispensary\n(v) Setting up of upto 50/30/10 bedded integrated Ayush Hospitals\n(vi) Supply of essential drugs to Government Ayush Hospitals, Government Dispensaries and\nGovernment/Government aided Teaching Institutional Ayush Hospitals\n(vii) Ayush Public Health Programmes\n(viii) Infrastructural development of Ayush Under-Graduate Institutions and Ayush Post-Graduate\nInstitutions\n(ix) Establishment of new Ayush Colleges in the States where availability of Ayush teaching\ninstitutions is inadequate in the Government Sector.\n\n3\nAnnexure-I\nStatus of district-wise Ayush hospitals in Jharkhand\nS. No. Name of District Number of Ayush hospital\n1 Bokaro 1\n2 Chatra 1\n3 Deoghar 1\n4 Dhanbad 1\n5 Dumka 1\n6 Garhwa 1\n7 Giridih 1\n8 Godda 1\n9 Gumla 1\n10 Hazaribagh 1\n11 Jamtara 1\n12 Khunti 1\n13 Koderma 1\n14 Latehar 1\n15 Lohardaga 1\n16 Pakur 1\n17 Palamu 1\n18 West Singhbhum 1\n19 East Singhbhum 1\n20 Ramgarh 1\n21 Ranchi 1\n22 Sahebganj 1\n23 Saraikela 1\n24 Simdega 1\nTotal 24\n\n4\nAnnexure-II\nStatus of district-wise registered/licensed Ayush practitioners\nS. No. Name of District Homeopath\ny Ayurveda Unani Total\n1 Bokaro 46 17 2 65\n2 Chatra 15 8 0 23\n3 Deoghar 34 2 0 36\n4 Dhanbad 86 12 4 102\n5 Dumka 15 5 0 20\n6 Garhwa 81 21 6 108\n7 Giridih 41 6 0 47\n8 Godda 63 4 1 68\n9 Gumla 15 14 2 31\n10 Hazaribagh 27 15 7 49\n11 Jamtara 27 2 0 29\n12 Khunti 4 2 0 6\n13 Koderma 14 6 3 23\n14 Latehar 10 6 0 16\n15 Lohardaga 13 4 0 17\n16 Pakur 11 2 0 13\n17 Palamu 66 29 11 106\n18 East Singhbhum 173 18 9 200\n19 Ramgarh 16 14 0 30\n20 Ranchi 104 107 21 232\n21 Sahebganj 15 4 0 19\n22 Saraikela 53 10 0 63\n23 Simdega 1 2 0 3\nTotal 930 310 66 1306"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |369", "t": "Combat Issue of Air Pollution", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO-369\nANSWERED ON- 21/07/2023\nCOMBAT ISSUE OF AIR POLLUTION\n369. DR. T. SUMATHY (A) THAMIZHACHI THANGAPANDIAN\n        SHRI SHYAM SINGH YADAV\nWill the Minister of  EXTERNAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state :-\n(a) whether there are existing partnerships with neighboring countries to combat the issue of\nAir Pollution in South Asia, a global hotspot for the most polluted cities in the world;\n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, whether there are any plans to execute any such\nagreement;\n(c) whether there are any signed MoUs with neighboring countries to install air pollution\nmonitors at critical points to generate data and adopt cost-effective solutions to tackle the\nissue of air pollution based on airshed approach; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\n \n(DR. RAJKUMAR RANJAN SINGH)\n(a to d) In India’s neighborhood, at the regional level, SAARC member states have signed the\n“Convention on Cooperation in Environment” in 2010. Similarly, BIMSTEC member states\nhave identified  “Environment  and  Climate  Change”  as  one  of  the  priority  areas  of\ncooperation and a draft Plan of Action for cooperation in Environment and Climate Change is\npresently under discussion among the member states. \nAt the bilateral level, India identifies cooperation activities with neighbouring countries based\non mutual consultations, mutual requirements and mutual benefits.  India has signed an\numbrella “MoU on Cooperation in the Areas of Environment” with Bhutan in June 2021 that\nenvisages long term cooperation in environmental protection and management of natural\nresources including prevention of Air Pollution. No specific cooperation agreement to combat\nair pollution have been entered into with other countries in India’s neighborhood.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |37", "t": "Budget for Minority Development", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO.37\nANSWERED ON 21.07.2023\nMIGRATION TO ECR COUNTRIES\n*37. SHRI K. MURALEEDHARAN\n        DR. A. CHELLAKUMAR\nWill the Minister of EXTERNAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state:\n(a) total number of people migrated to ECR (Emigration Check Required) countries through\nillegal agents in the last three years;\n(b) the status of the recently launched eMigrate Portal for the ECR passport holders who have\nmigrated to ECR countries; and\n(c) the measures taken/proposed to be taken by the Government to prevent illicit agencies from\nengaging in the recruitment of citizens for ECR category countries?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\n[DR. SUBRAMANYAM JAISHANKAR]\n(a) to (c) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n*****\n1\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (A) TO (C) IN RESPECT OF\nLOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 37 FOR REPLY ON 21.07.2022 REGARDING\n“MIGRATION TO ECR COUNTRIES” \n(a) The Ministry maintains record of migrant workers who migrate to 18 Emigration Check\nRequired (ECR) countries through e-Migrate portal. The Ministry keeps receiving inputs about\ninstances of Indian nationals holding ECR passports traveling through unlawful channels. On the\nbasis of complaints related to such illegal recruitment activities, the concerned state police\nauthorities are alerted to take appropriate action. Details of complaints received and forwarded\nto state police for action and investigation, during last four years are as under:\nYear No. of\ncomplaints\nCases referred to state\ngovernments for action\nProsecution\nsanctions sought\nProsecution\nsanctions issued\n2020 166 166 7 7\n2021 139 139 7 7\n2022 297 297 11 11\n2023 (upto \nJune 2023)\n47 47 05 05\nSo far 2548 illegal agents have been listed on the e-Migrate portal. Ministry has also put\nsome Foreign Employers in Pre-Approval Category (PAC) list based on complaints related to\nharassment, illegal recruitment, non-payment of salary etc.  On the basis of such complaints\nreceived from Indian migrant workers who are duped by illegal agents / employers abroad\nconcerned Indian Missions/Posts take up the matter with local authorities concerned to provide\nnecessary assistance and repatriate them to India on need basis. \n(b) The eMigrate portal is fully operational since its launch in year 2015 and provides for\nregistration of Recruitment Agents (RAs), Foreign Employers (FEs) and issue of Emigration\nClearance (EC) to prospective emigrants.  It is a comprehensive online system developed to\nfacilitate safe and legal migration of Indian workers to 18 ECR countries. The initiative was\nconceptualized  to  make  the  emigration  process  transparent  and  also  to  address  various\ncomplaints.  At present, approximately 1000 ECs are processed daily by the 14 Protector of\nEmigrants offices across the country. Since its inception in 2015, a total of 40,03,592 ECs have\nbeen issued through the eMigrate portal till June 2023. There are around 2000 active RAs and\naround 2 lakh FEs registered in the portal. \n(c) The Government accords highest priority to the safety, security and well-being of Indian\nnationals abroad. Ministry has been making constant efforts to educate migrant workers about\nthe perils of illegal channels. Following the implementation of the eMigrate system in 2015.\nECR  emigrants  are  recommended  to  seek  overseas  employment  exclusively  through  legal\nchannels,  such  as  the  eMigrate  portal.  Indian  Missions  abroad  also  play  a  crucial  role  in\nproactively sensitising Indian job seekers through issuing Advisories and Press Releases about\nramifications of fake job offers by fraudulent and illegal channels.\nThis Ministry has also launched the 'Surakshit Jaaye Prasikshit Jaaye - Go Safe, Go\nTrained' campaign in 2018 to raise awareness and facilitate safe and legal migration. This motto\nhas been publicized through awareness generation workshops and Pre-Departure Orientation &\nTraining (PDOT) imparted to the prospective migrants since its launch in March 2017. The\nprogram is presently being popularized through 33 centers spread across the country. The goal is\nthat  well-informed  migrant  workers  are   able  to seamlessly  integrate  and  have a  safe  and\nproductive stay abroad. Further, to underline the importance of safe, legal, orderly and skilled\n2\n\nmigration, a commemorative postal stamp on ‘Surakshit Jayein, Prasikshit Jayein’ was released\nby the Prime Minister of India on 09.01.2023 during the PBD 2023.\nThe Ministry holds regular inter-ministerial consultations with BoI, MHA and outreach\nevents  with  State  Governments  to  find  actionable  agenda  to  curb  the  activities  of  illegal\nrecruitment agents and to create awareness on safe and legal migration.  In recent years, GOI has\nsignificantly reinforced the support and welfare framework to empower ECR passport holders\nemigrating to 18 ECR countries.  The Indian Community Welfare Fund, the MADAD portal, e-\nMigrate system, Pravasi Bharatiy"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |370", "t": "Ayurvedic Hospitals in Madhya Pradesh", "head": "1\nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF AYUSH\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.370\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21ST JULY,2023\nAYURVEDICHOSPITALS IN MADHYAPRADESH\n†370. SHRI GYANESHWARPATIL:\nSHRI VIVEK NARAYANSHEJWALKAR:\nWill the Minister of AYUSH be pleased to state:\n(a) the number of Ayurvedic Hospitals/AYUSH Health Centres functioning in Madhya Pradesh,\nparticularly in Khandwa Parliamentary Constituency;\n(b) whether the Government has any special scheme for their expansion and if so, the details\nthereof;\n(c) whether the Government has any plans to establish new AYUSH hospitals/Health Centres in\nevery district of Madhya Pradesh, particularly in Khandwa Parliamentary Constituency, Gwalior\nand Shivpuri districts; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof along with the steps taken/proposed to be taken by the Government\nin this regard?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF AYUSH\n(SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL)\n(a) The number of Ayurvedic hospitals and Ayush Health & Wellness Centres (AHWCs)\nfunctioning in Madhya Pradesh, district-wise including Khandwa district is furnished at\nAnnexure-I.\n(b) Under Centrally Sponsored Scheme of the National Ayush Mission (NAM), there is provision\nof financial assistance to States/UTs including Madhya Pradesh for operationalization of AHWCs\nby upgrading Ayush dispensaries and sub health centres and setting up of upto 50/30/10 bedded\nintegrated Ayush hospitals.\n(c) & (d) Public Health being a State subject, establishment of new Ayush hospitals/Health\nCentres in any district of Madhya Pradesh, comes under the purview of State Government of\nMadhya Pradesh. However, under NAM, there is a provision of financial assistance to States/UTs\nfor setting up of 50/30/10 bedded Integrated Ayush hospitals and operationalization of AHWCs\nby upgrading Ayush dispensaries and sub health centres. Accordingly, State Government may\navail financial assistance by submitting suitable proposal through State Annual Action Plan\n(SAAP) as per the provision of NAM guidelines. Further, as per the proposals received from the\nState Government of Madhya Pradesh, the status of approved integrated Ayush Hospitals and\nAHWCs is furnished as Annexure-II.\n\n2\nAnnexure-I\nDistrict-wise status of functional Ayurvedic hospitals and Ayush Health & Wellness\nCentres (AHWCs) in Madhya Pradesh\n(i) The number of functional Ayurvedic hospitals:\nS. No. Name of the District Ayurveda\n1 Bhopal 10\n2 Sehore -\n3 Raisen -\n4 Rajgarh -\n5 Vidisha -\n6 Betul 2\n7 Hoshangabad 1\n8 Harda -\n9 Indore 3\n10 Dharwad 1\n11 Alirajpur -\n12 Jhabua 1\n13 Khargone 1\n14 Barwani -\n15 Khandwa -\n16 Burhanpur 1\n17 Jabalpur 2\n18 Katni -\n19 Chhidwara 1\n20 Seoni 1\n21 Mandla 1\n22 Balaghat 1\n23 Narsinghpur -\n24 Dindori -\n25 Gwalior 1\n26 Shivpuri 1\n27 Guna -\n28 Ashoknagar -\n29 Datia -\n30 Sheopur -\n31 Morena 1\n32 Bhind -\n33 Sagar 1\n34 Damoh 1\n35 Panna -\n36 Chhatarpur 1\n37 Tikamgarh -\n38 Ujjain 1\n39 Shajapur -\n40 Mandsaur 2\n41 Ratlam 2\n42 Dewas -\n43 Neemuch -\n\n3\n44 Agar Malwa -\n45 Rewa 1\n46 Singrauli -\n47 Sidhi 1\n48 Satna 1\n49 Umaria -\n50 Shahdol 1\n51 Anuppur -\n41\n\n4\n(ii) The number of functional Ayush Health & Wellness Centres (AHWCs):\nS. No. Name of District Functional AHWCs\n1 Agar-Malwa 3\n2 Alirajpur 5\n3 Anuppur 5\n4 Ashoknagar 8\n5 Balaghat 46\n6 Barwani 8\n7 Betul 12\n8 Bhind 16\n9 Bhopal 3\n10 Burhanpur 6\n11 Chhatarpur 18\n12 Chhindwara 12\n13 Damoh 12\n14 Datia 10\n15 Dewas 15\n16 Dhar 9\n17 Dindori 5\n18 Guna 12\n19 Gwalior 7\n20 Harda 4\n21 Hoshangabad 9\n22 Indore 9\n23 Jabalpur 13\n24 Jhabua 5\n25 Katni 15\n26 Khandwa 13\n27 Khargaon 11\n28 Mandla 8\n29 Mandsaur 11\n30 Morena 17\n31 Narsinghpur 11\n32 Neemuch 6\n33 Panna 11\n34 Raisen 7\n35 Rajgarh 9\n36 Ratlam 7\n37 Rewa 25\n38 Sagar 19\n39 Satna 22\n40 Sehore 14\n41 Seoni 11\n42 Shahdol 6\n\n5\n43 Shajapur 5\n44 Sheopur 3\n45 Shivpuri 12\n46 Sidhi 14\n47 Singrauli 7\n48 Tikamgarh 12\n49 Ujjain 16\n50 Umaria 9\n51 Vidisha 9\nTotal 562\n\n6\nAnnexure-II\n(i) The status of approved integrated Ayush hospitals\nS. No. Location of the integrated AyushHospital\n1. Bhopal\n2. Narsinghpur\n3. Indore\n4. Amarkantak\n5. Mandleshwar\n6. Balaghat\n7. Sehore\n8. Guna\n9. Panna\n10. Bhind\n(ii) The status of approved AHWCs\nSr.No. Name of District No. of AHWCs approved\n1 Ashoknagar 11\n2 Alirajpur 8\n3 Anuppur 7\n4 Agar malwa 5\n5 Balaghat 56\n6 Betul 16\n7 Bhopal 4\n8 Barwani 10\n9 Burhanpur 10\n10 Chhatarpur 21\n11 Chhindwara 18\n12 Bhind 20\n13 Datia 12\n14 Damoh 15\n15 Dhar 17\n16 Dindori 7\n17 Dewas 19\n18 Gwalior 10\n19 Guna 14\n20 Harda 6\n21 Hoshangabad 13\n22 Indore 15\n23 Jhabua 8\n24 Jabalpur 19\n25 Katni 18\n26 Khandwa 16\n27 Khargon 12\n28 Mandla 10\n29 Mandsaur 23\n30 Morena 21\n\n7\n31 Narsinghpur 15\n32 Nimuch 9\n33 Panna 15\n34 Ratlam 12\n35 Rajgarh 11\n36 Raisen 15\n37 Rewa 29\n38 Sidhi 17\n39 Sagar 28\n40 Singroli 9\n41 Satna 28\n42 Sehore 21\n43 Shajapur 6\n44 Seoni 13\n45 Sheopur 6\n46 Shivpuri 16\n47 Shahdol 9\n48 Tikamgarh 17\n49 Ujjain 22\n50 Umaria 9\n51 Vidisha 14\nTotal 762"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |371", "t": "Scheme for Cultivation of Medicinal Plants and Herbs", "head": "1\nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF AYUSH\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 371\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21/07/2023\n“SCHEME FOR CULTIV ATIONOF MEDICINAL PLANTS AND HERBS”\n371. DR. JAYANTAKUMAR ROY:\nSHRI VINOD KUMAR SONKAR:\nSMT. SANGEETA KUMARI SINGH DEO:\nWill the Minister of AYUSH be pleased to state:\na) whether the Government proposes to implement any scheme for the cultivation of natural\nherbs and medicinal plants in the country and if so, the details thereof;\nb) the details of the funds allocated for the projects under the scheme during the last three years\nand the current year, year-wise and State/UT-wise;\nc) whether the Government provides funds for development of infrastructure, research,\ndevelopment and training facilities in the AYUSH system of medicines;\nd) if so, the details thereof; and\ne) the other steps taken/proposed to be taken by the Government in this regard?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF AYUSH\n(SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL)\n(a) No, Sir. However, Ministry of Ayush, Government of India had implemented Centrally\nSponsored Scheme of National Ayush Mission (NAM) to promote the cultivation of medicinal plants\nfrom the financial year 2015-16 to 2020-21. As per the scheme guidelines, the support was provided\nfor:\n(i) Cultivation of prioritized medicinal plants on farmer’s land.\n(ii) Establishment of nurseries with backward linkages for raising and supply of quality\nplanting material.\n(iii) Post-harvest management with forwarding linkages.\n(iv) Primary processing, marketing infrastructure etc.\nPresently, National Medicinal Plants Board, Ministry of Ayush, Government of India is\nimplementing Central Sector Scheme on “Conservation, Development and Sustainable Management\nof Medicinal Plants” wherein following activities are supported:\n(i) In-situ conservation / Ex-situ conservation\n\n2\n(ii) Livelihood linkages with Joint Forest Management Committees (JFMCs) / Panchayats /\nVan Panchayats / Biodiversity Management Committees (BMCs) / Self Help Groups\n(SHGs).\n(iii) IEC activities like Training / workshops / Seminars/ Conferences etc.\n(iv) Research & Development.\n(v) Promotion, marketing and trade of medicinal plants produce.\n(vi) Establishment of nurseries\n(vii) Forward and backward linkage in supply chain of medicinal plants (Integrated component)\nAdditionally, Under ICAR there is one Institute named as ICAR-Directorate of Medicinal\nand Aromatic Plant Research (ICAR-DMAPR) located at Boriavi, Anand,\nGujarat which undertakes research on basic, applied and adaptive aspect on medicinal and aromatic\nplants. It has undertaken management of Plant Genetic Resources (PGR) as a prioritized activity\nwith the effort for collection, conservation, evaluation and documentation of germplasm of\nmedicinal and aromatic plants alongwith development of Good Agricultural Practices (GAP) of\nmedicinal plants brought under cultivation and which are of high medicinal and economic value.\nFurther, DMAPR maintains several germplasm/accessions of 20 species of MAPs. Besides, large\nnumber of accessions of 54 medicinal and aromatic plants are also maintained at different ICAR-\nAICRP centres. Presently DMAPR maintains 2313 germplasm accessions of 20 species of MAPs\nand 331 accessions of Betelvine. In addition, 5060 number of accessions of 54 medicinal and\naromatic plants are maintained at 26 ICAR-All India Coordinated Research Projects (ICAR-AICRP)\ncentres at different Agro-ecological Zones of the country. Multi-location evaluation trials conducted\nunder the AICRP on Medicinal and Aromatic Plants have resulted in the identification and release of\ntwenty five new improved varieties of medicinal plants of fourteen species and seven varieties of\naromatic plants of six species.\n(b) The details of State-Wise fund allocated from the financial year 2015-16 to 2020-21 for\nmedicinal plants component under National Ayush Mission Scheme (NAM) is at Annexure-I.\n(c) to (e) Ministry of Ayush is implementing the Centrally Sponsored Scheme of National\nAYUSH Mission (NAM) through State/UT Governments for the overall development and promotion\nof Ayush systems of medicine by providing financial assistance to them through different activities\nas per the provision of NAM guideline in accordance to the proposal received in their State Annual\nAction Plans (SAAPs). Under NAM, there are provisions for financial assistance for infrastructure\nstrengthening under following activities : -\n(i) Ayush Health & Wellness Centres.\n(ii) Co-location of Ayush facilities at Primary Health Centres (PHCs),\nCommunity Health Centres (CHCs) and District Hospitals (DHs).\n(iii) Upgradation of existing standalone Government Ayush Hospitals.\n(iv) Upgradation of existing Government/Panchayat/Government aided Ayush\nDispensaries/ Construction of building for existing Ayush Dispensary (Rented/\n\n3\ndilapidated accommodation)/ Construction of building to establish new Ayush\nDispensary in the area where there are no Ayush facilities available.\n(v) Setting up of upto 10/30/50 bedded integrated Ayush Hospitals.\n(v"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |372", "t": "Effect of UCC on other Laws", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LAW AND JUSTICE \nLEGISLATIVE DEPARTMENT \n \n \nLOK     S A B H A \n \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 372 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FRIDAY, THE 21st JULY, 2023. \n \nEffect of UCC on other Laws \n \n \n372. SHRI VINAYAK RAUT: \n PROF.  SOUGATA RAY: \n SHRI ARVIND GANPAT SAWANT: \n SHRI RAKESH SINGH. \n  \n  \n Will the Minister of LAW AND JUSTICE be pleased to state : \n \n(a) whether Uniform Civil Code (UCC) is being drafted to be implemented in the \ncountry and if so, the details and the time -frame thereof along with the stand of the \nGovernment thereon;  \n(b)  whether various religious groups have been  taken into confidence regardin g its \nimplementation, if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(c) whether adoption of cultures, beliefs and  traditions of tribal community is a \nchallenging factor for UCC and may cause difficulties in its implementation; \n(d)   if so, the details thereof along with the measures proposed to keep them out of the \nambit of UCC; \n(e)  whether the Government has analysed the  likely effect of implementation of UCC \non other laws and if so, the details thereof and the steps proposed  to be taken by the \nGovernment to address such situations; and \n(f) whether  the UCC is likely to be a single code  for everyone like Cr.PC and IPC and \nif so, the details thereof? \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \nA N S W E R \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE MINISTRY OF LAW AND JUSTICE; \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS; AND MINISTER OF \nSTATE IN THE MINISTRY OF CULTURE \n(SHRI ARJUN RAM MEGHWAL) \n \n(a) to (f) : The Government had  requested the 21 st  Law Commission of India to \nundertake examination of various issues relating to uniform civil code and to \nmake recommendations thereon. The term of the 21 st Law Commission ended on \n31.8.2018 and the 22nd Law Commission took up the reference on uniform civil \ncode for consideration and solicited views and ideas of the public at large and \nrecognized organizations through a public notice dated 14.6.2023.   Stake holders \nare at liberty to submit their comments to Law Commission till the 28 th of  July, \n2023. \n                                                          **** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |373", "t": "Radical Changes in Healthcare System", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nDEPARTMENTOF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.373\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21ST JULY,2023\nRADICAL CHANGES IN HEALTHCARE SYSTEM\n373: SHRIMATI RANJANBEN DHANANJAYBHATT:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government is considering to bring radical changes in the healthcare\nsystem and if so, the details thereof; and\n(b) whether the Government has taken/proposes to take any steps in this regard, if so, the\ndetails thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATEIN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY\nWELFARE\n(PROF. SATYAPAL SINGH BAGHEL)\n(a) to (b) : The National Health Policy 2017 envisages to provide universal access to good\nquality health care services through increasing access, increasing affordability by lowering the\ncost of healthcare delivery and equity. The goal of the policy is to attain the highest possible\nlevel of health and wellbeing for all at all ages through a preventive and promotive health care\norientation in all developmental policies and universal access to good quality health care services\nwithout anyone having to face financial hardship as a consequence.\nThe Government has launched four mission mode projects, namely PM-Ayushman Bharat Health\nInfrastructure Mission (PM-ABHIM), Ayushman Bharat Health & Wellness Centres (ABHWCs),\nPradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana (PMJAY) and Ayushman Bharat Digital Mission (ABDM).\nOther notable initiatives are National Health Mission (NHM), setting up of new AIIMS and\nupgrading of Government medical colleges under the Pradhan Mantri Swasthya Suraksha Yojna\n(PMSSY), support to State/UTs for setting up of new medical colleges as well as for increasing\nUG and PG medical seats , provision of free drugs and free diagnostic facilities at Primary\nHealth Centres (PHC)/ Community Health Centres (CHC) and district hospital level, substantial\nincrease in availability of government ambulances etc. The Fifteenth Finance Commission has\nalso recommended health grants aggregating to Rs. 70,051 crore to local government for\naddressing the gaps in primary health infrastructure in rural and urban areas.\nBesides, quality generic medicines are made available at affordable prices to all under\nPradhan Mantri Bhartiya Janaushadhi Pariyojana (PMBJP) in collaboration with the State\nGovernments. Affordable Medicines and Reliable Implants for Treatment (AMRIT) Pharmacy\nstores have been set up in some hospitals/institutions.\n***"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |374", "t": "Cases of Heat Wave Deaths", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 374\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2023\nCASES OF HEAT WA VEDEATHS\n374. SHRI RAMALINGAM S.:\nWill the Minister of HEALTHAND FAMILYWELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has taken any steps or remedial measures to prevent heat wave\ndeaths in the country, particularly in Tamil Nadu;\n(b) if so, the details thereof, State/UT-wise;\n(c) whether the Government has any clear data on the deaths reported due to heat wave across\nthe country during the last 10 years, particularly in the State of Tamil Nadu and if so, the\ndetails thereof;\n(d) whether the Government has any policy/data/action plan for reducing the heat wave deaths in\nthe country; and\n(e) if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATEIN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILYWELFARE\n(PROF. SATYAPAL SINGH BAGHEL)\n(a) to (e):\nUnion Health Ministry has launched National Programme on Climate Change & Human Health\n(NPCCHH) in 2019 with an objective to create awareness, strengthen capacity of healthcare system\nand health preparedness & response, and synergy/partnership with other missions to mitigate the\nimpact of climate change on human health.\nUnder National Programme on Climate Change and Human Health (NPCCHH), Union Health\nMinistry has taken several initiatives focused on providing technical support to states to address\nheat wave related issues. These include:\n\n1. The State officials has been advised to disseminate messages to increase general\nawareness through various IEC activities and conduct sensitization workshops on heat’s\nhealth impact and its prevention.\n2. To strengthen the public healthcare system, NPCCHH conducts capacity building\ntrainings/workshops of State/District level programme officers, health care professionals\nand health workers on tracking and managing of heat-related health issues.\n3. Multiple advisories on health sector preparedness, including do’s and don’ts are also\nshared with states.\n4. \"National Action Plan on Heat Related Illnesses\" has been released in July, 2021 and also\navailable on the website of Union Ministry of Health & Family Welfare.\n5. States are further advised to prepare their state-level heat health sector plan with focus on\nawareness building, health system capacity upgradation and strengthening of health\ninfrastructure to treat heat related illness and reduce related deaths. Till date, 28 States\nhave completed their heat-health action plans including State Government of Tamil Nadu.\n6. India Meteorological Department (IMD) issues heatwave alerts (early warning) and these\nare further shared with State Nodal Officers-Climate Change to ensure health facility\npreparedness and follow up.\n7. To ensure adequate preparedness at State/UT level ,Union Health Ministry has released\nGuidelines on Strengthening Health System Preparedness for Heat-Related Illnesses (HRI)\nincluding preparedness, control and management in April, 2023.\nDeaths as reported by States/UTs due to heat wave is at Annexure.\n******\n\nAnnexure\nState/UTs-wise list of deaths due to heat wave\nStates/UTs 2015 2016 2017 2018 2019 2020 2021 2022\n2023\n(Till\n30.06.2023)\nAndhra Pradesh 1422 723 236 8 29 0 0 1 4\nArunachal Pradesh -- -- -- -- NR 1 NR 0 0\nBihar -- 0 0 2 121 NR NR 0 8\nChhattisgarh -- 1 0 0 0 0 NR NR 0\nDelhi -- -- 0 0 0 NR 0 0 1\nGoa -- -- -- -- 0 NR NR 0 0\nGujarat 17 7 0 3 8 NR NR 0 35\nHaryana 0 0 0 0 0 NR NR NR 0\nHimachal Pradesh -- -- -- -- 0 NR NR NR 9\nJammu & Kashmir\n(Jammu Division) -- -- -- --\n0 1 NR\n0 0\nJammu & Kashmir\n(KashmirDivision)\n0 2 NR\nJharkhand -- 4 0 0 0 NR NR NR 0\nKarnataka -- 0 2 0 0 NR 0 NR 1\nKerala -- -- -- -- 1 0 0 0 120\nMadhya Pradesh -- 0 3 1 2 NR NR NR 10\nMaharashtra -- 7 9 2 6 0 NR 27 14\nOdisha 60 36 17 7 0 NR 0 3 7\nPunjab 0 0 0 0 0 NR NR 0 0\nRajasthan -- 9 9 1 3 0 NR NR 0\nTamil Nadu 0 0 0 0 1 NR 0 0 12\nTelangana 541 324 108 0 42 NR NR 2 20\nUttar Pradesh -- 0 0 0 2 NR 0 NR 12\nUttarakhand -- -- -- -- 0 NR NR 0 0\nWest Bengal -- 0 0 1 NR NR NR NR 11"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |375", "t": "Mortality due to Rabies", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 375\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2023\nMORTALITYDUE TO RABIES\n375. ADV .DEAN KURIAKOSE:\nWill the Minister of HEALTHAND FAMILYWELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) the details of the fatalities due to Rabies infection caused by dog bite and if so, the details\nthereof, State/UT-wise;\n(b) the steps taken/proposed to be taken to improve the availability of anti-rabies\nvaccines/medicines in the Government hospitals in Kerala;\n(c) the steps taken/proposed to be taken to control/curb stray dogs in Kerala;\n(d) whether the Government is likely to empower the local self-government to cull stray dogs\nas a means to control attack by them; and\n(e) if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATEIN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILYWELFARE\n(PROF. SATYAPAL SINGH BAGHEL)\n(a) to (e):\nUnion Health Ministry has launched the National Rabies Control Programme (NRCP) for prevention and\ncontrol of Rabies. To effectively control rabies, ‘National Action Plan For Dog Mediated Rabies\nElimination (NAPRE) from India by 2030’ has been launched by Ministry of Health & Family Welfare\nalongwith Ministry of Fisheries Animal Husbandry & Dairying, Government of India. As reported by\nStates/UTs in IDSP , State/UT-wise details of the fatalities due to rabies infection is at Annexure I.\nAs per the information provided by the State Government of Kerala, Vaccines are procured through Kerala\nMedical Services Corporation (KMSCL) through open tender process . The stock position of vaccines and\nimmunoglobulins are being monitored on a daily basis at district and state levels so that available vaccines\nare distributed judiciously. If necessary, rabies biologicals are also mobilised from neighbouring states\n\nsuch as Tamilnadu. In cases of emergency, institutions can also purchase vaccines/immunoglobulins\ndirectly from the open market using local purchase mechanisms.\nAs informed by Govt of Kerala several initiatives have been taken to control stray dogs in Kerala\nincluding :\ni. Government has initiated concerted efforts involving all stakeholders including the key\ndepartments of Local Self Governance Institute(LSGI) , Animal Husbandry and Health.\nii. Intersectoral meetings have been convened by the respective Ministers with decisions to undertake\nanimal birth control activities, mass dog vaccination, ensuring infrastructure & anti- rabies\nimmunologicals in hospitals and intense IEC activities.\niii. IEC activities have been intensified in print, audio-visual and social media regarding need for\nwound washing & post-exposure prophylaxis after animal bites, responsible pet ownership, pet\nvaccination and adequate waste management.\niv. Animal Birth Control (ABC) activities have been intensified by the LSGI department with the help\nof Animal Husbandry department. Guidelines have been issued for undertaking the ABC activities\nwith LSGI project funds and includes training of dog-catchers and setting up of infrastructure.\nv. Mass dog vaccination (pet dogs & stray dogs) have been ongoing during the months of September,\nOctober and November 2022 and mass dog vaccination drive are conducted every year during the\nmonth of September.\nvi. Capacity buildings of health-care workers are being conducted on an ongoing basis in all districts\nregarding first aid, wound care, categorization of wounds, post exposure prophylaxis and\nadministration of intradermal vaccine & immunoglobulins.\nvii. Rabies has been included as a Notifiable Disease under the provisions of the Kerala Public Health\nOrdinance 2022.\nviii.Joint Steering Committee for preparation of State Action Plan for Elimination of Dog Mediated\nRabies (SAPRE) has been constituted under the Chairmanship of Chief Secretary and the first\nmeeting was held on 16th November, 2022.\nAccording to State Government of Kerala, culling of stray dogs as a means to control dog bites are not\nallowed as per existing rules\n*****\n\nAnnexure I\nState/UT-wise details of the fatalities due to Rabies infection\nState/UTs 2022\nAndhra Pradesh 29\nArunachal Pradesh 1\nAssam 12\nBihar 21\nChandigarh 0\nChhattisgarh 2\nDadar Nagar Haveli & Daman Diu 0\nDelhi 48\nGoa 1\nGujarat 6\nHaryana 0\nHimachal Pradesh 1\nJammu 4\nJharkhand 4\nKarnataka 29\nKashmir 1\nKerala 27\nMadhya Pradesh 4\nMaharashtra 29\nManipur 2\nMeghalaya 0\nMizoram 0\nNagaland 0\nPuducherry 0\nOdisha NA\nPunjab 2\nRajasthan 0\nSikkim 0\nTamilnadu 20\nTelangana 21\nTripura 0\nUT Ladakh 0\nUttar Pradesh 5\nUttarakhand 0\nWest Bengal 38"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |376", "t": "Production of Generic Drugs", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF PHARMACEUTICALS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 376 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21st July, 2023 \n  \nProduction of Generic Drugs  \n \n†376. SHRI VINOD L. CHAVDA:  \n \nWill the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the details of the share of India in global trade of generic drugs;  \n(b) the quantum and value of generic drugs produced during the last three years and the current \nyear along with the value and quantity of such p roducts which have been exported and \nimported, category-wise and year-wise;  \n(c) the respective share of generic and nongeneric drugs in total sale of drugs in India including \nthose sold in Government hospitals; and \n(d) the steps taken/proposed to be taken by the Government to promote the use of generic drugs \nincluding the mandatory provision for physicians to provide prescription of generic drugs and \nthe outcome of these steps?  \n \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \n (SHRI BHAGWANTH KHUBA)   \n  \n(a) to (c):  Indian Pharmaceuticals Industry is 3rd largest by volume and 13th largest by value in \nthe world. It is one of the largest supplier of generic medicines with  about 20% share in the \nglobal supply and produces more tha n 60,000 generic drugs across 60 therapeutic categories. \nThe data pertaining to turnover, export and import of the Pharmaceutical sector during the last \nthree years is as under:  \nRs. in crore \nFinancial Year Turnover Export Imports \n2019-2020 2,89,998 1,40,537 40,139 \n2020-2021 3,28,054 1,74,064 46,808 \n2021-2022 3,44,125 1,74,955 60,060 \n \nData pertaining specifically to generic drugs is, however, not maintained.   \n \n(d): In order to provide quality generic medicines at affordable prices to all citizens, especially \nthe poor and the deprived ones, Pradhan Mantri Bhartiya Janaushadhi Pariyojana  (PMBJP) \nhas been launched by the Department of Pharmaceuticals wherein about 9,512 dedicated outlets \nknown as Pradhan Mantri Bhartiya Janaushadhi Kendras  (PMBJKs) have been opened till \n30.06.2023. \n \nFurther, Clause 1.5 of Indian Medical Council (Professional Conduct, Etiquette and \nEthics) Regulations, 2002 prescribes that every physician should prescribe drugs with generic \n\nnames legibly and preferably in capital letters and shall ensure that there is a rational \nprescription and use of drug. Registered Medical Practitioners have been directed by the \nMinistry of Health & Family Welfare from time to time to comply with the aforesaid \nprovisions. States have also been advised by the Ministry of Health & Family Welfare to ensure \nprescription of generic drugs and conduct regular prescription audits in public health facilities. \n \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |377", "t": "Medical Colleges recognized by Medical Council of India", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nDEPARTMENTOF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †377\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 21ST JULY,2023\nMEDICAL COLLEGES RECOGNIZED BY MEDICAL COUNCIL OF INDIA\n†377. SHRI RAMCHARAN BOHRA:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) the number and name of medical colleges recognized by the Medical Council of India (MCI)\nin the country during the last three years and the current year;\n(b) the funds allocated by the Government to such recognized medical colleges by MCI for\nproviding facilities, college-wise;\n(c) whether the Government has made any assessment regarding the reforms and the progress\nmade in the construction of buildings, purchase of medical equipments, teaching facilities\netc.; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATEIN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY\nWELFARE\n(DR. BHARATI PRA VINPAWAR)\n(a) to (d): The Government has increased number of medical colleges and subsequently\nincreased MBBS seats. There is an increase of 82% in Medical Colleges from 387 before 2014 to\n704 as of now. Further, there is an increase of 110% in MBBS seats from 51,348 before 2014 to\n107,948 as of now, there is also an increase of 117% in PG seats from 31,185 before 2014 to\n67,802 as of now.\nAs informed by National Medical Commission (NMC) (erstwhile Medical Council of\nIndia (MCI)), a total of 183 Medical College have been recognised by the MCI/ NMC under the\nIndian Medical Council Act, 1956/ National Medical Commission Act, 2019 during the last three\nyears and current year. States/ UT-wise details are at Annexure.\n\nUnder CSS for strengthening/ upgradation of existing State Government/ Central\nGovernment Medical Colleges to increase the number of MBBS (UG) and PG seats, under which\nsupport has been provided for increase of 4677 MBBS seats in 77 colleges with an approved cost\nof Rs. 5612.25 Cr, 4058 PG seats in phase-I in 72 colleges with an approved cost of 1498.43 Cr\nand 3957 PG seats in phase -II in 62 colleges with an approved cost of Rs. 4461.44 Cr.\nUnder Upgradation of Government Medical Colleges by construction of Super-Specialty\nBlocks of Pradhan Mantri Swasthya Suraksha Yojana (PMSSY) Scheme a total of 75 projects\nhave been approved, of which 62 projects are complete. This Ministry monitors the progress of\nthe projects through various review meetings held with the States/ UTs.\n\nAnnexure\nDetails of Medical Colleges recognized during the last three years\nS\nNo.\nState/UT\nMedical Colleges recognized\n2020 2021 2022 2023\n1 Andhra Pradesh 2 5 0 4\n2 Assam 0 0 0 0\n3 Andaman & Nicobar 1 0 0 1\n4 Arunachal Pradesh 0 0 0 1\n5 Bihar 2 0 1 0\n6 Chhattisgarh 2 4 0 3\n7 Delhi 0 1 1 0\n8 Gujarat 2 4 0 5\n9 Haryana 2 3 1 4\n10 Himachal Pradesh 0 1 0 4\n11 Karnataka 3 6 3 6\n12 Kerala 0 3 2 2\n13 Madhya Pradesh 1 2 0 5\n14 Maharashtra 3 5 6 2\n15 Manipur 0 0 1 0\n16 Mizoram 0 0 0 1\n17 Odisha 0 1 0 3\n18 Punjab 0 1 0 0\n19 Rajasthan 1 4 0 7\n20 Tamil Nadu 1 4 4 2\n21 Telangana 0 6 0 6\n22 Tripura 0 0 1 0\n23 Uttar Pradesh 6 11 2 10\n24 Uttarakhand 0 1 0 1\n25 West Bengal 0 4 1 1"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |378", "t": "Consumption of AUS-40 and AUS-32", "head": "Government of India \nMinistry of Chemicals & Fertilizers \nDepartment of Chemicals & Petrochemicals \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTON NO- 378 \nANSWERED ON -21/07/2023 \n  \nCONSUMPTION OF AUS-40 and AUS-32 \n  \n378. SHRI KESINENI SRINIVAS \n  \nWill the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state :-  \n \n(a) the details of the quantity of annual consumption of AUS-40 and AUS-32; \n(b) whether the Government maintains any list of the procurement source of AUS-40 and AUS-\n32; \n(c) if so, the details thereof; \n(d) whether the Government maintains any list of the procurement source of imported AUS-40 \nand AUS-32; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof?         \n \n \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \n \n(SHRI BHAGWANTH KHUBA) \n  \n(a-e) The Chemicals and Petrochemicals is a de-licensed and deregulated sector in \ngeneral. Department of Chemicals and Petrochemicals monitors the production and \nconsumption of only the limited number of chemicals and petrochemicals; as such the  data \nabout the consumption and procurement sources of domestic and imported AUS32 and AUS40 \nare not maintained. \n  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |379", "t": "Centrally Sponsored Schemes", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.379 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2023 \n \nCENTRALLY SPONSORED SCHEMES \n \n379.  DR. SUKANTA MAJUMDAR: \n \nWill the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) the details of Centrally Sponsored Schemes and Central Sector Schemes being \nimplemented by the Ministry in the State of West Bengal during the last four years \nand the current year, year-wise, scheme-wise and district-wise including North and \nSouth Dinajpur districts;  \n(b) the details of funds allocated, sanctioned, released and utilized for each of the \nabove schemes during the last four years and the current year, year-wise, scheme-\nwise and district-wise;  \n(c) the details of physical targets set and achiev ed while implementing the above \nschemes during the last five years and the current year, year -wise, scheme-wise \nand district-wise;  \n(d) whether the Ministry has found any shortcomings while implementing the above \nschemes and if so, the details thereof including the steps taken by the Government \nto check those shortcomings;  \n(e) whether there is time and cost overrun in any of the above schemes; and  \n(f) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \n MINISTER OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) & (b) For better implementation and efficient monitoring, all Centrally Sponsored \nSchemes of the Ministry being implemented in the country, including West Bengal, have \nbeen clubbed into three verticals, viz. (1) Saksham Anganwadi & Poshan 2.0 \nfor improving nutrition indicators in the country (2) Mission Shakti, for safety, protection \nand empowerment of women; and (3) Mission Vatsalya, for protection and welfare of \nchildren. The details of these schemes are as under: \n  \n(i) Saksham Anganwadi & Poshan 2.0:  This programme has been reorganized into 3 \nprimary verticals: (i) Nutrition Support for POSHAN and for Adolescent Girls (ii) Early \nChildhood Care and Education [3 -6 years] and (iii) Anganwadi Infrastructure including \nmodern, upgraded Saksham Anganwadis.  \n  \n(ii) Mission Shakti: Mission Shakti comprises of two sub -schemes ‘Sambal’ and \n‘Samarthya’ for safety and security of women and empowerment of women respectively. \nThe existing schemes of One Stop Centres (OSC), Women Helplines (181-WHL) and  \n\n \n \n \n \nBeti Bachao Beti Padhao (BBBP) have been made part of Sambal sub - scheme; while \nthe existing schemes of Pradhan Mantri Matru Vandana Yojana (PMMVY), Ujjwala, \nSwadhar Greh and Working Women Hostel and National Creche Scheme have been \nsubsumed in ‘Samarthya’. \n  \n(iii) Mission Vatsalya: Under Mission Vatsalya, the Child Protection Scheme(CPS) has \nbeen strengthened for better outreach and protection for children in need and care in a \nmission mode with the objective to: (i)Support and sustain Children in difficult \ncircumstances. (ii) Develop context -based solutions for holistic development of children \nfrom varied backgrounds. (iii) Provide scope for green field projects for encouraging \ninnovative solutions. (iv) Cement convergent action by gap funding, if required.  \n  \nOnly under 3 schemes of Mission Shakti, funds were being directly released to districts \nof all the States/UTs viz. One Stop Centre (OSC), Universalization of Women Helplines \n(WHL) and Beti Bachao Beti Padhao (BBBP) till FY 2022-23. However, BBBP & WHL are \nnot being implemented by State Government of West Bengal. District -wise details of \nfunds allocated/ released and utilized under the OSC scheme during the last four years \nis annexed at Annexure-I. In the rest of the schemes, the funds are being released to \nStates/UTs. Details of funds allocated/ released and utilized under the schemes during \nthe last four years and the current year is annexed at Annexure-II. \n  \n(c)  The physical targets and achievements in West Bengal under schemes of the \nMinistry are annexed at Annexure-III.  \n  \n(d), (e) & (f) PMMVY is being implemented in the State of West Bengal since 2017 - 18. \nHowever, it had come to the notice that the Scheme was being implemented in the State \nwith a different nomenclature i.e. 'Bangla Matri Prakalpa'. The State government later on \nagreed and ensured that in future the scheme would be implemented strictly as per the \nguidelines of PMMVY. On receipt of the written assurance from the State, the release of \nCentral share of funds to the State Government of West Bengal has been resumed from \nMarch, 2022. \n  \nUnder all the schemes of the Ministry, Utilisation Certificate/Statement of Expenditure are \nnot being received from the State. Regular follow up meetings have been conducted with \nthe State at all levels to report the utilisation of funds. \n \nAll the schemes of the Ministry were evaluated by NITI Aayog and their suggestions were \nincorporated while reorganising the schemes of the Ministry into 3 Missions. \n  \n*****  \n  \n\n \nAnnexure-I \n  \nDistrict-wise details of funds allocated/ released and util"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |38", "t": "Credit Linked Capital Subsidy Scheme", "head": "��\n��\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n����\n��\n������\n��\n����������������\n��\n����������\n��\n����������\n��\n������������\n��\n����������\n��\n��\n��\n������������������������������������������������������\n��\n��������\n��\n��\n��\n��\n��\n��������\n��\n��\n��\n����������������������������\n��\n����������\n��\n������\n��\n����\n��\n����������\n��\n��\n��\n������������������\n��\n��������������\n��\n��������\n��\n����\n��\n��\n��\n��������\n��\n��\n��\n����������\n��\n����������\n��\n������\n��\n����\n��\n����������\n��\n��\n��\n����������\n��\n������������\n��\n������\n��\n����\n��\n������������\n��\n��\n��\n��������������������\n��\n��������\n��\n����\n��\n��\n��\n��������\n��\n��\n��\n������������������������\n��\n����������\n��\n������\n��\n��\n��\n����������\n��\n��\n��\n����������\n��\n����������\n��\n������\n��\n����\n��\n����������\n��\n��\n��\n������\n��\n��������\n��\n����\n��\n��\n��\n��������\n��\n����\n��\n��������������\n��\n������������\n��\n��������\n��\n����\n��\n������������\n��\n����\n��\n��������������\n��\n����������\n��\n������\n��\n����\n��\n����������\n��\n����\n��\n��������������������������������\n��\n����������\n��\n������\n��\n��\n��\n����������\n��\n����\n��\n����������������������������������\n��\n����������\n��\n������\n��\n��\n��\n����������\n��\n����\n��\n������������������\n��\n����������\n��\n������\n��\n����\n��\n����������\n��\n����\n��\n������������������\n��\n������������\n��\n������\n��\n����\n��\n������������\n��\n����\n��\n������������\n��\n����������\n��\n������\n��\n����\n��\n����������\n��\n����\n��\n������������\n��\n��������\n��\n��\n��\n��\n��\n��������\n��\n����\n��\n����������������������\n��\n������\n��\n��\n��\n��\n��\n������\n��\n����\n��\n����������������������������\n��\n������������\n��\n������\n��\n����\n��\n������������\n��\n����\n��\n����������������������\n��\n������������\n��\n��������\n��\n������\n��\n������������\n��\n����\n��\n��������������\n��\n��������\n��\n����\n��\n��\n��\n��������\n��\n����\n��\n������������������\n��\n��������\n��\n��\n��\n��\n��\n��������\n��\n����\n��\n��������������\n��\n��������\n��\n��\n��\n��\n��\n��������\n��\n����\n��\n����������������\n��\n��������\n��\n��\n��\n��\n��\n��������\n��\n����\n��\n������������\n��\n����������\n��\n������\n��\n����\n��\n����������\n��\n����\n��\n��������������������\n��\n��������\n��\n����\n��\n��\n��\n��������\n��\n����\n��\n������������\n��\n����������\n��\n������\n��\n����\n��\n����������\n��\n����\n��\n������������������\n��\n������������\n��\n������\n��\n����\n��\n������������\n��\n����\n��\n������������\n��\n��������\n��\n��\n��\n��\n��\n��������\n��\n����\n��\n��������������������\n��\n������������\n��\n��������\n��\n����\n��\n������������\n��\n����\n��\n������������������\n��\n����������\n��\n������\n��\n����\n��\n����������\n��\n����\n��\n��������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������\n��\n��������\n��\n����\n��\n��\n��\n��������\n��\n����\n��\n��������������\n��\n��������\n��\n����\n��\n��\n��\n��������\n��\n����\n��\n��������������������������\n��\n������������\n��\n��������\n��\n����\n��\n������������\n��\n����\n��\n����������������������\n��\n����������\n��\n������\n��\n��\n��\n����������\n��\n����\n��\n����������������������\n��\n����������\n��\n������\n��\n����\n��\n����������\n��\n��\n��\n����������\n��\n��������������\n��\n����������\n��\n������\n��\n��������������\n��\n��\n��\n��"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |380", "t": "Filling up Vacancies in Medical Colleges", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nDEPARTMENTOF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †380\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 21ST JULY,2023\nFILLING UP V ACANCIESIN MEDICAL COLLEGES\n†380. SHRIMATI GOMATI SAI:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether any steps have been taken/proposed to be taken by the Government to fill up the\nvacant posts in large number of medical colleges across the country;\n(b) if so, the details thereof, State/UT-wise particularly in Chhattisgarh;\n(c) if not, the reasons therefor; and\n(d) the steps taken/proposed to be taken to resolve the problem of shortage of equipments, lack\nof infrastructure and basic facilities in the medical colleges of the country including\nChhattisgarh?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATEIN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY\nWELFARE\n(DR. BHARATI PRA VINPAWAR)\n(a) to (d): As informed by the National Medical Commission (NMC), the following steps have\nbeen initiated to help filling up of various vacancies in medical colleges in the Country including\nChhattisgarh:\ni. The qualification awarded by the Diplomate of National Board (DNB) through National\nBoard of Examinations (NBE) has been recognized for appointment as faculty to take\ncare of shortage of faculty.\nii. Enhancement of age limit for appointment/ extension/ re-employment against posts of\nteachers/ Dean/ Principal/ Director in medical colleges upto 70 years.\n\niii. The ratio of teachers to students has been rationalized to increase the number of\npostgraduate medical seats in the country.\niv. By amending the regulations, it has been made mandatory for all medical colleges to\nstart PG courses within 3 years from the date of their MBBS recognition/ continuation of\nrecognition.\nThe Ministry of Health and Family Welfare administers a Centrally Sponsored Scheme\n(CSS) for establishment of new Medical Colleges attached with existing district/ referral\nhospitals’ with preference to underserved areas and aspirational districts where there is no\nexisting Government or private medical college with fund sharing between Center and State\nGovernments in the Ratio of 90:10 for North Eastern special category states and 60:40 for others.\nUnder the scheme, 157 medical colleges have been approved in the three phases, including 05\nmedical colleges in Chhattisgarh.\nUnder CSS for strengthening/ upgradation of existing State Government/ Central\nGovernment Medical Colleges to increase the number of MBBS (UG) and PG seats, under\nwhich support has been provided for increase of 4677 MBBS seats in 77 colleges with an\napproved cost of Rs. 5612.25 Cr, 4058 PG seats in phase-I in 72 colleges with an approved cost\nof 1498.43 Cr and 3957 PG seats in phase -II in 62 colleges with an approved cost of Rs.\n4461.44 Cr. Under this scheme support has been provided to the state of Chhattisgarh for\nincrease of 150 MBBS seats in 03 colleges at an approved cost of Rs. 180 Cr and increase of 79\nPG seats in 01 college at an approved cost of Rs. 32.73 Cr.\nUnder upgradation of Government Medical Colleges by construction of Super-Specialty\nBlocks of Pradhan Mantri Swasthya Suraksha Yojana (PMSSY) Scheme, a total of 75 projects\nhave been approved including 02 Government Medical Colleges at Bilaspur and Jagdalpur in\nChhattisgarh. Further, establishment of 22 new AIIMS including AIIMS at Raipur, Chhattisgarh\nhas also been approved.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |381", "t": "Screening of Common Cancer under NPCDCS", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 381\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21st JULY,2023\nSCREENING OF COMMON CANCER UNDER NPCDCS\n381. SHRI SRIDHAR KOTAGIRI:\nWill the MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether it is a fact that screening of common cancer under National Programme for Prevention\nand Control of Cancer, Diabetes, Cardiovascular Diseases, and Stroke (NPCDCS) is\nvolunteer-based and if so, the details thereof;\n(b) whether it is also a fact that because of this NPCDCS lacks the follow-up strategy for further\ninvestigation and treatment and if so, the details thereof;\n(c) whether the Government is planning to sponsor a National Screening Program (NCD) for\ncancer in the country and if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;\n(d) whether it is also a fact that NPCDCS only covers three common cancers (oral, cervical and\nbreast) and if so, the details thereof; and\n(e) whether the Government is considering the inclusion of other prevalent cancers under the\nNPCDCS and if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATEIN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\n(Prof. SATYAPALSINGH BAGHEL)\n(a) to (e): The Department of Health & Family Welfare, Government of India, provides technical and\nfinancial support to the States/UTs under the National Programme for Prevention and Control of Non\nCommunicable Diseases (NP-NCD) erstwhile National Programme for Prevention and Control of\nCancer, Diabetes, Cardiovascular Diseases and Stroke (NPCDCS), as part of National Health Mission\n(NHM), based on the proposals received from the States/UTs and subject to the resource envelope.\nCancer is an integral part of NP-NCD. The programme focusses on strengthening infrastructure,\nhuman resource development, health promotion & awareness generation for prevention, early\n\ndiagnosis, management, follow up and referral to an appropriate level of healthcare facility for\ntreatment of the Non-Communicable Diseases (NCDs), including Cancer. Under NP-NCD, 724\nDistrict NCD Clinics, 326 District Day Care Centre, and 6110 Community Health Center NCD Clinics\nhave been set up.\nA population-based initiative for prevention, control and screening for common NCDs i.e. diabetes,\nhypertension and common cancers has been rolled out in the country under NHM and also as a part of\nComprehensive Primary Health Care. Under the initiative, persons more than 30 years of age are\ntargeted for their screening for the three common cancers i.e oral, breast and cervical. Screening of\nthese common cancers is an integral part of service delivery under Ayushman Bharat – Health and\nWellness Centres. Preventive aspect of Cancer is strengthened under Comprehensive Primary Health\nCare through Ayushman Bharat Health Wellness Centre scheme, by promotion of wellness activities\nand targeted communication at the community level.\nCancer is diagnosed and treated at various levels in the health care facilities. The treatment in\nGovernment Hospitals is either free or highly subsidized for the poor and needy. The Central\nGovernment implements Strengthening of Tertiary Cancer Care Centres Facilities Scheme in order to\nenhance the facilities for tertiary care of cancer. 19 State Cancer Institutes (SCIs) and 20 Tertiary Care\nCancer Centres (TCCCs) have been approved under the said scheme. 17 Institutes are functional so far.\n***"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |382", "t": "Out of Pocket Expenditure", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nDEPARTMENTOF HEALTH & FAMILYWELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 382\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21ST July, 2023\nOUT OF POCKET EXPENDITURE\n382: SHRI POCHA BRAHMANANDA REDDY:\nSHRI M.K. RAGHA V AN:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) the details of the health expenditure as a percentage of GDP during each of the last three\nyears;\n(b) whether Out-of-Pocket Expenditure (OOPE) on health in the country is one of the highest\nacross the world and if so, the details thereof along with the necessary steps taken by the\nGovernment to bring down OOPE on health in the country;\n(c) whether the Government proposes to increase the spending on health in order to meet its\ntargets and if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;\n(d) whether irregularity in the treatment costs across hospitals is causing a meteoric rise in\nOOPE in the country and if so, the details thereof; and\n(e) The present status of AIIMS, Kozhikode along with total amount of funds provided for\nhealth infrastructure development of Kerala?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATEIN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH\nAND FAMILYWELFARE\n(PROF. SATYAPALSINGH BAGHEL)\n(a) to ( d) : As per the National Health Accounts (NHA) Estimates for India, the Total Health\nExpenditure (THE) as percentage of GDP and Out-of-Pocket Expenditure (OOPE) on health\nas percentage of THE for the years 2017-18 - 2019-20 are as under:\nIndicator 2017-18 2018-19 2019-20\nTotal Health Expenditure as percentage of\nGDP (in %age)\n3.3 3.2 3.3\nOOPE as %age of THE 48.8 48.2 47.1\nThere is declining trend in the OOPE in the country.\nNational Health Policy (NHP), 2017 envisages raising government health expenditure to\n2.5% of GDP in a time bound manner. Further, NHP 2017, also envisages that States should\n\nincrease their health spending to atleast 8% of their budget and two thirds of such expenditure\nshould be on primary care. The budget allocation for Department of Health & Family Welfare\n(DoHFW) has increased by 82% from Rs. 47,353 crore in 2017-18 (BE) to Rs. 86,175 crore\nin 2023-24 (BE). Government is making continuous efforts to increase allocation in health\nbudget.\nThe Government has launched four mission mode projects, namely PM-Ayushman Bharat\nHealth Infrastructure Mission (PM-ABHIM), Ayushman Bharat Health & Wellness Centres\n(ABHWCs), Pradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana (PMJAY) and Ayushman Bharat Digital\nMission (ABDM). Other notable initiatives are National Health Mission (NHM), setting up of\nnew AIIMS and upgrading of Government medical colleges under the Pradhan Mantri\nSwasthya Suraksha Yojna (PMSSY), support to State/UTs for setting up of new medical\ncolleges as well as for increasing UG and PG medical seats , provision of free drugs and free\ndiagnostic facilities at Primary Health Centres (PHC)/ Community Health Centres (CHC) and\ndistrict hospital level, substantial increase in availability of government ambulances etc. The\nFifteenth Finance Commission has also recommended health grants aggregating to Rs.\n70,051 crore to local government for addressing the gaps in primary health infrastructure in\nrural and urban areas.\nThe Clinical Establishments (Registration and Regulation) Act, 2010 has been enacted by the\nCentral Government to provide for the registration and regulation of all clinical\nestablishments in the country with a view to prescribe the minimum standards of facilities\nand services provided by them.\n( e) : Setting up of AIIMS in Kerala has not been sanctioned, however, Government of India\nhas taken upgradation of the following Government Medical Colleges in the State of Kerala\nin Phase-I, III and V of PMSSY ,details of which is as follows:\nSl.\nName of Govt. Medical\nCollege Phase\nApproved Project Cost (Rs. in Crore) Central\nShare\nReleased\n(Rs. in\nCrore)\nCentral\nShare\nState /\nAdministrative\nMinistry Share Total\n1 Thiruvananthapuram\nMedical College I 100 20 120 96.26\n2 Government Medical\nCollege, Kozhikode III 120 30 150 90.90\n3 TD Medical College,\nAlappuzha III 120 30 150 99.53\n4\nSree Chitra Tirunal\nInstitute of Medical\nSciences & Technology,\nTrivandrum V(A) 120\n110\n(Administrative\nMinistry Share) 230 180.94\n***"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |383", "t": "Mental Health Services", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nDEPARTMENTOF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 383\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21ST JULY,2023\nMENTALHEALTH SERVICES\n383. SHRI KURUV AGORANTLA MADHA V:\nWill the Minister of HEALTHAND FAMILYWELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether a separate allocation of budget is made by the Government for development of\nMental Health Services;\n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the funds allocated and spent on development of\nMental Health Services in the country during the last two years; and\n(c) if not, the reasons therefor?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATEIN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILYWELFARE\n(PROF. SATYAPAL SINGH BAGHEL)\n(a) to (c) Yes, the budget allocated for mental health services under the Ministry, including\nallocation for National Mental Health Programme (NMHP), District Mental Health Programme\n(DMHP), National Institute of Mental Health and Neuro Sciences (NIMHANS), Bengaluru,\nLokopriya Gopinath Bordoloi Regional Institute of Mental Health (LGBRIMH), Tezpur, Assam\nand Central Institute of Psychiatry (CIP), Ranchi, for the last two years is as follows:\nSr. No. Year Total Allocation for Mental Health (Rs. in Crore)\n1 2021-22 846.17\n2 2022-23 949.74\nThe Government has also launched a “National Tele Mental Health Programme” on 10th October,\n2022, to further improve access to quality mental health counselling and care services in the\ncountry. For the year 2022-23, a total of Rs. 120.98 crore was allocated for National Tele-Mental\nHealth Programme (NTMHP).\n****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |384", "t": "Upgradation of AYUSH Colleges in Kerala", "head": "1\nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF AYUSH\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.384\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21ST JULY,2023\nUPGRADATION OF AYUSH COLLEGES IN KERALA\n384. SHRI KODIKUNNIL SURESH:\nWill the Minister of AYUSH be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has any plan to upgrade Ayurveda Panchakarma College in\nThiruvananthapuram district and Government Homeo Medical College in Kurichy of Kottayam\ndistrict for augmenting infrastructure to facilitate advanced AYUSH treatments with research\nwork;\n(b) if so, the details thereof;\n(c) whether the Government has allocated any funds in this regard during the last three years and\nthe current year; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF AYUSH\n(SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL)\n(a) to (d) Public Health being a State subject, upgradation of Ayurveda Panchakarma College in\nThiruvananthapuram district and Government Homeo Medical College in Kurichy of Kottayam district\nfor augmenting infrastructure to facilitate advanced AYUSH treatments with research work comes\nunder the purview of State Government of Kerala. However, under Centrally Sponsored Scheme of the\nNational Ayush Mission (NAM), there is a provision of financial assistance to States/UTs for\ninfrastructural development of Ayush Under-Graduate Institutions and Ayush Post-Graduate\nInstitutions. Accordingly, State Government may avail financial assistance by submitting suitable\nproposal through State Annual Action Plan (SAAP) as per the provision of NAM guideline. Further, no\nsuch proposals have been received in Ministry of Ayush through SAAPs during the last three years and\nthe current year.\n****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |385", "t": "Infant Mortality", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nDEPARTMENTOF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 385\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21st July 2023\nINFANT MORTALITYRATE\n385: SHRIMATI RAKSHA NIKHIL KHADSE:\nWill the MINISTER of HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) The number of births per day;\n(b) The number of newborn deaths per day along with the reasons therefor;\n(c) Whether the Government has formulated any working committee to address this issue\nto understand and minimize such cases in the country; and\n(d) If so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATEIN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY\nWELFARE\n(DR. BHARATI PRA VINPAWAR)\n(a) to (d):\nAs per latest Sample Registration Survey Report of Registrar General of India, Birth Rate is\n19.5 per 1000 population and Neonatal Mortality Rate is 20 per 1000 Live Births in 2020 at\nNational level.\nAs per Cause of Death Statistics 2017-19 released by Office of the Registrar General &\nCensus Commissioner, India; major causes of new-borns deaths in India are Prematurity &\nlow birth weight (45.5%), Birth asphyxia & birth trauma (15.1%), Neonatal Pneumonia\n(12.6%), Other non-communicable diseases (8.0%), Sepsis (5.2%), Congenital anomalies\n(4.9%), Ill-defined or cause unknown (4.5%), Diarrhoeal diseases (2.1%), Fever of unknown\norigin (1.1%), Injuries (0.6%), and All Other Remaining Causes (0.4%).\nMinistry of Health and Family Welfare issued Guidelines on Child Death Review in 2014 for\nformulation of various committees to review Child Deaths at State, District and Facility Level\nto take corrective measures based on causes of child deaths.\nIn order to bring down newborn mortality all across the country, the Ministry of Health and\nFamily Welfare (MoHFW) supports all States/UTs in implementation of Reproductive,\nMaternal, New-born, Child, Adolescent health and Nutrition (RMNCAH+N) strategy under\nNational Health Mission (NHM) based on the Annual Program Implementation Plan (APIP)\nsubmitted by States/ UTs.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |386", "t": "Tobacco Consumption", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nDEPARTMENTOF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 386\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21ST JULY 2023\nTOBACCO CONSUMPTION\n386. SHRI RAMDAS C. TADAS:\nSHRI FEROZE V ARUNGANDHI:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether tobacco consumption is one of the major risk factors for all kinds of non-\ncommunicable diseases;\n(b) if so, the details of the funds allocated and utilized on tobacco control since 2018,\nState/UT-wise, year-wise;\n(c) whether the Government proposes to strengthen the Cigarettes and other Tobacco\nProducts (Prohibition of Advertisement and Regulation of Trade and Commerce, Production,\nSupply and Distribution) Act, 2003 to control tobacco consumption;\n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;\n(e) whether it is a fact that the sale of tobacco products to minors/by minors is punishable\nunder the Juvenile Justice Act and if so, the details of the number of such cases registered so\nfar across the country; and\n(f) whether the Government has any plan to prevent the young generation from tobacco\naddiction and if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATEIN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILYWELFARE\n(PROF. S.P. SINGH BAGHEL)\n(a) & (b): Tobacco is a known risk factor for cardiovascular diseases, diabetes, and chronic\nobstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) as well as for infectious respiratory diseases such as\npneumonia and tuberculosis (TB). The State/UT wise State Programme Implementation Plan\n(SPIP) Approvals and Expenditure under National Tobacco Control Programme (NTCP)\nunder National Health Mission (NHM) from F.Y .2018-19 to 2022-23 is at Annexure.\n(c) & (d): The draft Cigarettes and Other Tobacco Products (Prohibition of Advertisement\nand Regulation of Trade and Commerce, Production, Supply and Distribution) (Amendment)\nBill, 2020, was hosted in public domain for pre-legislative consultation.\n\n(e) & (f): As per information received from Ministry of Women and Child Development,\nMinistry of Women and Child Development administers the Juvenile Justice (Care and\nProtection of Children) (JJ) Act, 2015 (as amended in 2021) has a separate chapter to provide\nstringent penalty to be imposed on whoever gives intoxicating liquor or narcotic drug or\npsychotropic substance to a child. No such central data is maintained under the Juvenile\nJustice (Care and Protection of Children) Act, 2015.\nFurther, various steps have been taken by the Government to protect school going\nchildren against the harmful effects of tobacco products. As per Section 6 (a) of Cigarettes\nand Other Tobacco Products (Prohibition of Advertisement and Regulation of Trade and\nCommerce, Production, Supply and Distribution) Act, 2003 (COTPA, 2003) read with\nCigarettes and Other Tobacco Products (Prohibition of Advertisement and Regulation of\nTrade and Commerce, Production, Supply and Distribution) Amendment Rules, 2011, there is\nprohibition on sale of tobacco products to and by persons below the age of 18 years and as\nper Section 6 (b) of COTPA, 2003, there is prohibition on sale of tobacco products in an area\nwithin a radius of one hundred yards of any educational institution.\nPublic Health is a State subject and the primary responsibility for enforcement of the\nAct, lies with the State Governments for effective implementation of the provisions of\nCOTPA, 2003. The Ministry of Health & Family Welfare has issued the “Guidelines for\nTobacco Free Educational Institution (Revised)” for effective implementation of Section-6 of\nCOTPA, 2003. States are taking effective measures with Education departments for\nimplementation of these Guidelines. Regular and sustained awareness is carried out about the\nadverse effects of tobacco usage on health through various anti-tobacco campaigns. For the\nyear 2022-23, a total of 2,05,655 challans were issued by States/UTs for violations reported\nunder Sections 6(a) & (b) of COTPA, 2003.\nMinistry of Health & Family Welfare has launched a 60 days Tobacco Free Youth\nCampaign on this World No Tobacco Day on 31st May, 2023 which is implemented by all\nStates and UTs to create intensive awareness on harmful effects of tobacco use, particularly\namongst the youth.\n********\n\nAnnexure for LS US PQ 386 for 21.07.2023\nState/UT wise SPIP Approvals and Expenditure under National Tobacco Control Programme (NTCP) under NHM from F.Y. 2018-19 to 2022-23\nS.\nN\no.\nName of the\nState/UTs\n2018-19 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 2022-23\nSPIP\nApproval\nExpendit\nure\nSPIP\nApproval Expenditure SPIP\nApproval Expenditure SPIP\nApproval Expenditure SPIP\nApproval\nExpendit\nure\n1\nAndaman and\nNicobar\nIslands\n- - 7.08 3.00 16.33 2.36 23.75 1.41 26.00 2.68\n2 Andhra\nPradesh 37.38 34.32 63.38 53.75 67.98 67.98 244.23 226.33 380.16 279.90\n3 Arunachal\nPradesh 89.76 159.42 255.80 - 414.00 69.66 149.86 90.87 500.75 17.31\n4 Assam 456.27 178.12 324.45 201.37 246.27 74.55 321.96 174.88 329.11 201.82\n5 Bihar 390.20 57.51 304.97 72.43 188.61 20.33 220"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |387", "t": "One Stop Centres in Andhra Pradesh", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n  \nLOK SABHA \n UN-STARRED QUESTION NO. 387 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2023 \n  \nONE STOP CENTRES IN ANDHRA PRADESH \n  \n387.   SHRI MAGUNTA SREENIVASULU REDDY:  \n \nWill the Minister of Women and Child Development be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) the details of the One Stop Centres currently operational in the State of Andhra \nPradesh, districtwise;  \n(b) the details of funds allocated towards such centres during the last three years, \nyear-wise; and  \n(c) the details of services provided to women in the said centres?  \n  \n  \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n  \n  \n(a) & (b): The One Stop Centre (OSC) scheme implemented by the Government since \n1st April, 2015 is now a component of sub -scheme 'Sambal' under umbrella scheme \nfor safety, security and empowerment of women named 'Mission Shakti'. So far, a total \nof 26 One Stop Centres have been approved for 26 Districts of Andhra Pradesh, out \nof which 13  OSCs are functional. The district -wise details of functional OSCs and \nfunds released to these centres during the last three years are at Annexure-I.  \n  \n(c): OSCs are set up with the objective to provide a range of integrated services under \none roof includ ing police facilitation, medical aid, legal aid and legal counselling, \npsycho-social counselling and temporary shelter to women affected by violence or \ndistress. \n  \n**** \n  \n\nANNEXURE-I \n  \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) & (b) OF THE LOK SABHA \nUN-STARRED QUESTION NO.387 FOR 21.07.2023 ASKED BY SHRI MAGUNTA \nSREENIVASULU REDDY REGARDING ‘ONE STOP CENTRES IN ANDHRA \nPRADESH’  \n  \nDistrict wise details of functional and non -functional OSCs and funds released \nto these centres during the last three years : \nSl. \nNo. \nDistricts Function \nStatus \n2020-21 2021-22 2022-23 \n1 Ananthapuram Yes 1500450 1500450 0 \n2 Chittoor-A Yes 1250375 1500450 0 \n3 Kakinda formerly East Godavari Yes 1850375 1500450 0 \n4 Guntur Yes 1500450 1500450 0 \n5 Dr. YSR Kadapa Yes 1250375 1500450 0 \n6 Kurnool Yes 1850375 1500450 0 \n7 SPSR Nellore Yes 1250375 1500450 0 \n8 Prakasm Yes 1850375 3325450 1326739 \n9 Srikakulam Yes 2100450 1500450 0 \n10 NTR formerly Krishna Yes 1850375 1500450 2385588 \n11 Visakhapatnam Yes 1500450 1500450 2813774 \n12 Vizianagaram Yes 1500450 1500450 769554 \n13 Eluru formerly West Godavari Yes 1250375 1500450 0 \n14 Tirupathi formerly Chittor-B* No 0 3625911* 0 \nTotal 20505250 24956761 7295655 \n*Funds released for recurring expenditure and construction activities. \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |388", "t": "Indian Community Welfare Fund", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO-388\nANSWERED ON- 21/07/2023\nINDIAN COMMUNITY WELFARE FUND \n 388. ADV . A.M. ARIFF\nWill the Minister of EXTERNAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state :-\n(a) the details of balance amount available in Indian Community Welfare Fund (ICWF) in each of\nthe Indian Embassy/High Commission abroad as on 30.06.2023, country-wise;\n(b) whether the Government has utilised the amount available with ICWF for helping Indians\ninvolved  in  legal  disputes  during  the  Financial  Year  2019-23,  if  so,  the  details  thereof,\nEmbassy/High Commission-wise; and\n(c) whether the Government has utilized the amount available with ICWF for bringing back the\nmortal remains of Indians who had expired during their stay abroad during the said period and if so,\nthe details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\n(SHRI V . MURALEEDHARAN)\n(a) The details of balance amount available in Indian Community Welfare Fund (ICWF) in each of\nthe  Indian  Embassy/High  Commission  abroad  as  on  30.06.2023,  country-wise  is  given  in\nAnnexure I.\n(b & c) Missions / Posts abroad are authorized to incur expenditure on different welfare activities\non  actual  need  basis  within  prescribed  ceilings.  Expenditure  beyond  prescribed  ceilings  on  a\nparticular welfare activity can also be incurred, if required, with prior approval of the Ministry. The\nMission wise details of expenditure incurred from Indian Community Welfare Fund (ICWF)  from\n2019 till June 2023, for providing legal assistance to  Indian nationals and expenditure incurred on\ntransportation of mortal remains from the country of death to India are given in Annexure II.\n*****\n1\n\nAnnexure – I\nSl. No. Name of the country\nAvailable balance in ICWF as on \n30.06.2023\n1 Algeria  21,15,838.00₹\n2 Angola  16,33,922.00₹\n3 Argentina  2,33,248.00₹\n4 Armenia  12,88,712.00₹\n5 Australia  27,78,20,712.65₹\n6 Austria  1,18,66,729.00₹\n7 Azerbaijan  2,27,317.00₹\n8 Bahrain  14,13,95,933.49₹\n9 Bangladesh  43,69,056.00₹\n10 Belarus  62,60,300.00₹\n11 Belgium  6,39,57,086.15₹\n12 Bhutan  33,54,884.00₹\n13 Botswana  1,07,96,754.00₹\n14 Brazil  55,34,443.00₹\n15 Brunei darussalam  41,64,848.00₹\n16 Bulgaria  29,28,691.00₹\n17 Burkina Faso  2,48,784.00₹\n18 Cambodia  23,01,297.00₹\n19 Cameroon  7,83,032.00₹\n20 Canada  36,38,10,132.00₹\n21 Chile  18,41,281.00₹\n22 China  22,06,34,417.49₹\n23 Colombia  16,85,765.00₹\n24 Cote d'Ivoire  57,79,171.60₹\n25 Croatia  18,14,240.00₹\n26 Cuba  1,16,848.31₹\n27 Cyprus  16,33,989.00₹\n28 Czech Republic  48,34,309.00₹\n29 Democratic Republic of the Congo  98,41,597.00₹\n30 Denmark  3,11,04,991.00₹\n31 Djibouti  11,10,563.71₹\n32 Egypt  42,95,051.76₹\n2\n\n33 Equatorial Guinea  45,619.00₹\n34 Eritrea  50,473.00₹\n35 Ethiopia  1,32,68,457.00₹\n36 Fiji  6,86,192.51₹\n37 Finland  93,10,998.00₹\n38 France  13,82,81,141.00₹\n39 Germany  17,49,05,416.00₹\n40 Ghana  21,25,641.00₹\n41 Greece  1,30,22,913.00₹\n42 Guatemala  10,13,034.00₹\n43 Guinea  2,82,159.00₹\n44 Guyana  5,55,715.45₹\n45 Hungary  24,17,886.00₹\n46 Iceland  5,00,498.00₹\n47 Indonesia  3,41,69,389.30₹\n48 Iran  11,34,392.00₹\n49 Iraq  5,20,17,600.00₹\n50 Ireland  2,23,47,906.00₹\n51 Israel  5,20,