\n \n0.00\n \n0.19\n \n0.09\n \nJHARKHAND\n \n1.53\n \n2.55\n \n4.28\n \n5.12\n \n1.17\n \nJ&K\n \n0.09\n \n0.10\n \n0.26\n \n0.27\n \n0.22\n \nKARNATAKA\n \n0.59\n \n0.41\n \n1.38\n \n1.47\n \n0.14\n \nKERALA\n \n4.65\n \n4.82\n \n5.20\n \n5.09\n \n4.97\n \nMADHYA\n \nPR\nADESH\n \n13.95\n \n17.40\n \n24.97\n \n30.70\n \n30.93\n \nMAHARASHTRA\n \n5.80\n \n11.67\n \n12.72\n \n12.27\n \n12.37\n \nODISHA\n \n44.48\n \n47.98\n \n"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |670", "t": "Suicide Cases and Guidelines for NEET Exam", "head": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.670 \nANSWERED ON 24/07/2023 \n \nSUICIDE CASES AND GUIDELINES FOR NEET EXAM \n \n670. DR. T.R. PAARIVENDHAR: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware of the fact that large number of students who are pursuing \ncoaching for NEET from various coaching centres across the country are committing suicide in the \nrecent past; \n(b) whether the Government has any data abo ut the number of students who committed suicide for \nthe last 03 years; \n(c) if so, the details of the action taken by the Government in this regard; \n(d) whether the Government has any proposal to frame a standard guideline to run coaching centres/ \ninstitutions across the country to save the students from forced suicide; \n(e) if so, the details thereof; and \n(f) if not, the reasons therefor? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n(DR. SUBHAS SARKAR) \n \n(a) to (c)      Education being a concurrent list subject, Educational Institutions are managed by both \nCentral and State Government. Majority of the Educational Institutions in the country falls in the \npurview of the State / UT Government. The number of suicide cases of students of all these \ninstitutions and various coaching centres including coaching centres for NEET  across the country are \nnot maintained. However, as per the Accidental Deaths & Suicide in India (ADSI), 2021, Report of \nNational Crime Record Bureau (NCRB), there are various causes of suicides like professional / \nCareer problems, sense of isolation, abuse, violence, family problems, mental disorders etc.  \n(d) to (f)   National Education Policy (NEP) 2020 provides for counselling systems for handling \nstress and emotional adjustments in I nstitutions. It also provisions for opportunities for students \nparticipation in sports, culture/arts clubs, eco -clubs, activity clubs, community service projects, etc. \nTo address the issue of mental health and well -being during and after COVID,  University Grants \nCommission (UGC) had issued advisories to Higher Educational Institutions (HEIs) on \n05.04.2020.UGC has also issued Guidelines for Promotion of Physical Fitness, Sports, Student’s \nHealth, Welfare, Psychological and Emotional Well - being at HEIs.  I n January 2023, University \nGrants Commission (UGC), has circulated the National Suicide Prevention Strategy formulated by \nthe Ministry of Health and Family Welfare. The Ministry has undertaken various steps such as peer \nassisted learning, introduction of tec hnical education in regional languages for students in order to \n\n2 \n \nease the academic stress. The Government of India initiative, named MANODARPAN, covers a \nwide range of activities to provide psychological support to students, teachers and families for mental  \nand emotional well-being during the COVID outbreak and beyond. The Ministry has also advised the \ninstitutions to make the system more robust that would include prevention, detection and remedial \nmeasures for addressing possible cause of suicides. \nThe high er educational institutions take various steps such as conducting workshops/ seminars on \nHappiness and Wellness, regular sessions on Yoga, induction programs, extracurricular activities \nincluding sports and cultural activities, assigning of one faculty adv iser for group of students to \nsupport with their academics and monitor their progress, and appointment of Student Counselors for \noverall personality development and de-stressing students.  \n*** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |671", "t": "Protection of Interests of Employees through Outsourcing Agencies", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 671 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 24.07.2023 \n \nPROTECTION OF INTERESTS OF EMPLOYEES THROUGH \nOUTSOURCING AGENCIES \n \n†671. SHRI SATYADEV PACHAURI: \n  \n Will the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)whether the Government has formulated any scheme to monitor \nthe entire process related to protection of interests of employees \nrecruited through outsourcing;  \n(b)if so, the details thereof; and \n(c)if not, the details of the steps taken by the Government to \nsafeguard the interests of employees from outsourcing Agencies? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) to (c): The Government posts are filled up in accordance with the \nrecruitment rules. However, keeping in view the administrative \nexigency of work and unavoidable circumstances, outsourcing for \nservices through contract is also sometimes resorted to.  \n \n  There are detailed procedures laid down for procurement \nof such non -consulting or outsourced services, including through e -\nprocurement, in the “General Financial Rules, 2017 (GFR 2017), and \nthe “Manual for Procurement of Consultancy & Other Services, 2017”. \n \n  Statutory and contractual obligations to be complied with \nin respect of the outsourced employees engaged for provision of \nservices to Government are governed by the applicable Labour Laws \nsuch as Contract Labour (Regulation and Abolition) Act, 1970, \nMinimum Wages Act, 1948, Payment of Wages Act, 1936, Payment of \nBonus Act, 1965, Payment of Gratuity Act, 1972, Building and Other \nConstruction Workers (Regulation of Employment and Conditions of \nService)  Act, 1996,   Equal   Remuneration   Act, 1976,   Inter-State  \nContd..2/- \n\n:: 2 :: \nMigrant Workmen (Regulation of Employment and Conditions of \nService) Act, 1979, Child and Adolescent Labour (P&R) Act, 1986 and \nMaternity Benefit Act, 1961 etc.   \nProtection of the interests of contractors’ employees \nunder these laws, is enforced by the Government through the Office \nof Chief Labour Commissioner (Central) [CLC(C)] for Central Sphere. \nThe CLC(C) carries out inspections for the purpose, so as to regulate \nthe employment of contract labour, payment of minimum wages, \nwelfare, etc of employees engaged by the contractors for providing \nservice. \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |672", "t": "Relocation of Cheetah", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 672 \n TO BE ANSWERED ON 24.07.2023  \n \nRelocation of Cheetah \n \n672. SHRI SURESH KUMAR KASHYAP:  \nSHRI THIRUNAVUKKARASAR SU:  \nSHRIMATI MALA ROY:  \nDR. RAMAPATI RAM TRIPATHI:  \nSHRI MANICKAM TAGORE B.:  \nSHRI SANGAM LAL GUPTA:  \nPROF. SOUGATA RAY:  \nSHRI P.P. CHAUDHARY:  \nSHRI RAVNEET SINGH BITTU:  \nSHRI ANURAG SHARMA:  \nSHRI PRATAP CHANDRA SARANGI: \n \nWill the MINISTER OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate:  \n(a) whether it is a fact that among the cheetahs that were translocated from South Africa and \nNamibia to Kuno National Park (KNP) of Madhya Pradesh, some of them have died and \nif so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor;  \n(b) the total cost of import of 70 Cheetahs;  \n(c) whether the Government has any proposal to relocate them to some other places in the \ncountry and if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;  \n(d) whether the Government has consulted of any wild life expert to free them at Kuno forest \nand if so, the details thereof;  \n(e) the method for keeping track of the health and physical condition of the translocated \ncheetahs; \n(f) whether the Government has formed a new committee to keep watch on the Cheetah \nproject having International and national experts in it;  \n(g) the measures taken to ensure their safety and prevent any further cheetah deaths in this \nregard; and  \n(h) whether the Government proposes to translocate more cheetahs and other similar animals \nto the country in the near future and if so, the details in this regard? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE \nCHANGE  \n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n(a) As reported by the State, the details of Cheetah deaths are given below: \n  \nSl. \nNo. \nName of Cheetah Date of \ndeath \nReason for death \n1. Sasha 27.03.2023 Chronic renal failure \n2. Uday  23.04.2023 Cardiopulmonary failure \n\n3. Daksha 09.05.2023 Traumatic Shock \n4. Tejas 11.07.2023 As per the Preliminary Necropsy Report, \nthe reasons of death is Traumatic Shock \n5. Suraj 14.07.2023 As per the Preliminary Necropsy Report, \nthe reasons of death is Traumatic Shock \n \n3 Indian born cubs of nearly 2 months of age died on 23.05.2023 due to heat stroke. \n \n(b) As per the Action Plan for Introduction of cheetah in India, for the first phase (5 \nyears), the estimated cost of the project is Rs. 91.65 crore.   \n \n(c) A detailed scientific action plan has been prepared as per IUCN Guidelines for \nintroduction of Cheetah in India.  As per the Action Plan, Kuno National Park, \nNauradehi wildlife sanctuary and Gandhi Sagar wildlife sanctuary in Madhya Pradesh  \nand Shahgarh Bulge, Bhainsrorgarh wildlife sanctuary and enclosure in Mukundara \nHills Tiger Reserve in Rajasthan are the potential sites identified for cheetah \nintroduction.  Cheetah introduction in these sites depends upon the continued \navailability of cheetahs from African countries for sourcing as well as the status of \nhabitat, prey base and protection mechanism on the ground. \n \n(d) Yes Sir.  The introduction of cheetahs in India is based on the best available \nconservation science and a detailed scientific action plan prepared following the \nexpertise of leading Cheetah experts including biologists, ecologists, conservationists \nand managers. \n \n(e) A dedicated team of forest officials, cheetah experts and veterinarians has been undertaking protection as well as monitoring and well-being of cheetahs on 24x7 basis. \n \n(f) Yes Sir.  A Cheetah Project Steering Committee has been constituted on 25th May, \n2023 and the terms of reference of the Steering Committee is to review progress, \nmonitor and advice on the Cheetah introduction in consultation with international \ncheetah experts as and when required. \n \n(g) To ensure conservation and protection of Cheetahs in India, actions are taken as per \nscientific action plan which has been prepared following the expertise of leading \nCheetah experts including biologists, ecologists, conservationists and managers. \n \n(h) As per the Action Plan for introduction of Cheetah, every year depending upon the \navailability of animals and status of introduced Cheetahs, 12-14 individuals are \nproposed to be brought from South Africa and Namibia or other African countries \nduring the next five years. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |673", "t": "Apprenticeship Fair", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUN-STARRED QUESTION NO: †673 \nANSWERED ON 24-07-2023 \n  \nAPPRENTICESHIP FAIR \n  \n†673.  SHRI SHANKAR LALWANI:               DR. BHARATIBEN DHIRUBHAI SHIYAL: \n                                                 \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether the Government organizes apprenticeship fair to increase the employment \nopportunities for youths; \n(b) if so, the details of such fairs conducted during the last three years, State-wise; and \n(c) the number of youths who have been given apprenticeship certificates through various \nmediums, State-wise? \n  \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND \nENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \n(SHRI RAJEEV CHANDRASEKHAR) \n \n(a) to (b): Yes Sir. Pradhan Mantri National Apprenticeship Mela (PMNAM) is a monthly event in \nwhich establishments / industries and candidates actively participate. It provides awareness to \nthe youth on various apprenticeship opportunities existing across the participating \nestablishments / industries and also allows the establishments to engage candidates as \napprentices. Initially, Ministry of Skill Development & Entrepreneurship (MSDE) organized \nApprenticeship Melas on 04.10.2021 and 21.04.2022.  \n \nBased on the encouraging response that these Melas received, States/UTs were advised \nto conduct PMNAM. From June 2022 onwards, PMNAM is organized on every 2nd Monday in \none-third of the total number of districts in the State with the flexibility for choosing the \ndistrict/location and the day of Mela based on local conditions/festivals etc so that all districts \nare covered once in a quarter and four times in a year. Smaller States/UTs can conduct \nPMNAM once in three months. Since, launch of PMNAM in June 2022 and upto March 2023, \nthe event has witnessed dynamic participation of establishments/companies and candidates. \nThe State/UT-wise list of number of PMNAM organized and contracts generated is at Annexure-\nI.  \n \n      The thrust given by the Ministry towards apprenticeship training through reforms \n(facilitation and process simplifications) and public outreach to create awareness about \napprenticeship training among the students and the establishments through workshops, \nPMNAM, engagement with establishments / employers are making positive impact. All these \nhave led to increase in the number of apprentices engaged from 2.96 lakh in 2020-2021 to 7.4 \nlakh in 2022-23. Currently, the number of apprentices engaged stands at 6.40 lakh. \nAn Impact Evaluation Study on National  Apprenticeship Promotion Scheme \n(NAPS)  through National Productivity Council (NPC) has major findings with respect to \n\nemployment post apprenticeship training viz. (i) employment rate among pass-outs was 64% \nwithin 6 months and 74% within 12 months after completion and (ii) 68% strongly felt that \nprogram made them more employable compared to a non-certificate holder.  \n(c) Broadly, the apprenticeship trades are classified as ‘Designated’ Trade and ‘Optional’ \nTrade. The duration of these trades varies from 6 months to 2 years. For the category, \nDesignated Trade, the National Apprenticeship Certificate (NAC) is awarded to those who pass \nthe All India Trade Tests (AITT), conducted by Directorate General of Training (DGT). To \nfacilitate apprentices who have completed the training to take the examination at the earliest, \nAITT has been made quarterly. This was previously conducted twice a year.  \n \nFor Optional Trades, the apprenticeship certificates are awarded to the youth based on \nthe assessment done by the Sector Skill Councils (SSCs). In addition to this, the Regional \nBoards under Ministry of Education grant ‘Certificate of Proficiency’ to the apprentices. The \nnumber of youths given apprenticeships certificates State-wise is at Annexure-II. \n  \n  \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \n  \nFor UN-STARRED QUESTION NO: 673 TO BE ANSWERED ON 24-07-2023 \n  \nThe State/UT-wise list of number of PMNAM organized and contracts generated \n  \nState PMNAM Locations Contract Generated* \n1. Andaman & Nicobar -  29 \n2. Andhra Pradesh 84  7,309 \n3. Arunachal Pradesh 13  10 \n4. Assam 58  4,660 \n5. Bihar 91  2,615 \n6. Chandigarh 2  453 \n7. Chhattisgarh 72  2,810 \n8. Delhi 23  11,785 \n9. Goa 2  5,587 \n10. Gujarat 236  35,071 \n11. Haryana 57  24,813 \n12. Himachal Pradesh 30  3,554 \n13. Jammu & Kashmir 52  3,667 \n14. Jharkhand 65  4,411 \n15. Karnataka 70  21,835 \n16. Kerala 44  4,789 \n17. Ladakh -  - \n18. Lakshadweep 1  1,045 \n19. Madhya Pradesh 104  20,389 \n20. Maharashtra 109  77,895 \n21. Manipur -  8 \n22. Meghalaya 4  115 \n23. Mizoram 3  1 \n24. Nagaland -  1,186 \n25. Odisha 89  3,516 \n26. Puducherry 5  1,459 \n27. Punjab 71  6,082 \n28. Rajasthan 95  7,179 \n29. Sikkim 6  4,285 \n30. Tamil Nadu 110  32,148 \n31. Telangana 71  13,175 \n32. The Dadra Nagar Haveli and \nDaman & Diu \n6 1,766 \n33. Tripura 11  215 \n34. Uttar Pradesh 216  25,973 \n35. Uttarakhand 1"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |674", "t": "Features of eShram Portal", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 674 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 24.07.2023 \n \nFEATURES OF ESHRAM PORTAL \n \n674.  DR. KALANIDHI VEERASWAMY: \n  SHRI KRIPANATH MALLAH:  \nSHRIMATI JASKAUR MEENA:  \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate:  \n \n(a)the salient features of the eShram Portal;  \n(b)the details of the number of unorganized workers registered under \nthis portal across the country including Assam, Tamil Nadu and \nRajasthan;  \n(c)the details of the benefits being provided to the unorganized \nworkers through this portal across the country including Assam, \nTamil Nadu and Rajasthan;  \n(d)the details of the target fixed in the country;  \n(e)the details of the achievements made so far along with its \nresponse within the country; and \n(f)whether the Government has made available new features in \neShram Portal to enhance utility of the portal and facilitate ease \nof registration for unorganised workers and if so, the details \nthereof? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n(a) to (f): The Ministry of Labour and Employment has launched \neShram portal on 26.08.2021 for registration and creation of a \nComprehensive National Database of Unorganized Workers. It allows \nan unorganised worker to register himself or herself on the portal on \nself-declaration basis, under 400 occupations in 30 broad occupation \nsectors.  \nThe details of targets fixed and number of unorganized \nworkers registered/ achievements under the eShram portal across \nthe country including Assam, Tamil Nadu and Rajasthan are at \nAnnexure. \nContd..2/- \n\n:: 2 :: \n \nSalient features and new devel opment on eShram portal \nand the benefits extended to the unorganised workers through \neShram portal are as follows:  \n \n(i) The registration in the portal is fully Aadhaar verified and Aadhaar \nseeded. Any unorganised worker can register himself or herself on \nthe portal on a self-declaration basis.  \n \n(ii)  Provision has been added in eShram to capture family details of \nmigrant workers. Provision has been added in eShram to share \nconstruction workers’ data with States/UTs to facilitate their \nregistration in resp ective Building and other Construction Workers \n(BoCW) boards. \n  \n(iii) eShram has been integrated with National Career Service (NCS) \nPortal. An unorganised worker can register on NCS using his/ her \nUniversal Account Number (UAN) and search for suitable job \nopportunities. An option/ link has also been provided to registrant on \neShram portal to seamlessly register on NCS. \n \n(iv) eShram is also integrated with Pradhan Mantri Shram -yogi \nMaandhan (PM-SYM). PM-SYM is a pension scheme for unorganised \nworkers who are aged between 18-40 years. Using Universal Account \nNumber (eShram), any unorganised worker can easily register on \nMaandhan portal. \n \n(v)  To provide skill enhancement and apprenticeship opportunities to \nunorganised worker, eShram has been integrated with Skill India \nDigital portal of Ministry of Skill Development and Entrepreneurship. \n \n(vi)  eShram registrant, who is registered on NCS can also avail digital \ntrainings through DigiSaksham programme. \n \n *                                                       ****** \n  \n\nAnnexure \nANNEXURE REFERRED IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (f) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 674 FOR 24.07.2023 REGARDING “FEATURES OF ESHRAM PORTAL” \nRAISED BY DR. KALANIDHI VEERASWAMY: SHRI KRIPNATH MALLAH: SHRIMATI \nJASKAUR MEENA. \nSN State Target Total Registration  \n(As on 16th July 2023) \nTarget \nAchieved \n1 Andaman and Nicobar Islands          1,16,770                               29,132  24.95% \n2 Andhra Pradesh     1,50,92,950                          79,94,843  52.97% \n3 Arunachal Pradesh          4,39,728                            1,42,416  32.39% \n4 Assam        99,69,971                          69,78,738  70.00% \n5 Bihar     3,49,43,979                        2,86,30,249  81.93% \n6 Chandigarh          3,24,372                            1,74,562  53.82% \n7 Chhattisgarh        82,42,144                          83,21,764  100.97% \n8 Delhi        52,39,058                          32,64,306  62.31% \n9 Goa          4,44,150                               63,243  14.24% \n10 Gujarat     1,78,84,271                        1,13,36,534  63.39% \n11 Haryana        78,97,313                          52,70,748  66.74% \n12 Himachal Pradesh        20,86,547                          19,29,705  92.48% \n13 Jammu and Kashmir        38,09,769                          34,03,237  89.33% \n14 Jharkhand     1,08,06,305                          92,05,615  85.19% \n15 Karnataka     1,89,17,552                          76,27,655  40.32% \n16 Kerala        99,95,844                          59,11,462  59.14% \n17 Ladakh            80,926                               30,858  38.13% \n18 Lakshadweep             20,491                                2,455  11.98% \n19 Madhya Pradesh     2,39,00,510                   "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |675", "t": "Green Credits", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 675 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 24.07.2023 \n \nGreen Credits \n \n675. SHRI BHOLA SINGH: \nSHRI VINOD KUMAR SONKAR: \nSHRI P.C. MOHAN: \nSHRIMATI SANGEETA KUMARI SINGH DEO: \nSHRI RAJA AMARESHWARA NAIK: \nDR. SUKANTA MAJUMDAR: \nSHRI RAJVEER SINGH (RAJU BHAIYA): \nSHRI SUSHIL KUMAR SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)  whether a draft for Green Credit Programme (GCP) 2023 has been proposed, if so, its key \ndetails; \n(b)  whether the Green Credit System will include a wider range of tradable environmental \nobligations than traditional carbon credit systems, if so, the scope and trading \nmechanisms of these obligations; \n(c)  whether the Indian Council of Forestry Research and Education (ICFRE) will manage the \nGCP, and if so, the details of plan for developing, implementation procedures and \nguidelines; \n(d)  whether the GCP is designed to incentivize multiple ecosystem services beyond just \ncarbon reductions, and if so, the mechanisms to quantify these services; \n(e)  whether concerns over potential Greenwashing in the green credits/market have been \nraised and if so, the Government’s response and planned mitigation measures; \n(f)  whether a robust methodology and demand generation strategies for Green Credit has \nbeen formulated to ensure market stability and if so, the details thereof; \n(g)  whether the Government has identified sectors including increasing the green cover \nthrough tree plantation across the country, promoting water conservation, water \nharvesting and water use efficiency/savings etc. for the implementation of the Green \nCredit programme and if so, the details thereof; and \n(h)  the steps being taken to create a mass movement around environmental positive actions \nand realise the vision of “Mission LiFE” to achieve net zero goal of 2070? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE \nCHANGE  \n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n\n \n \n \n(a) to (h). Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change has notified ‘Draft Green \nCredit Programme Implementation Rules 2023’ on 26.06.2023 for public consultation. \nThe draft notification is enclosed at Annexure - I. Final notification will be issued after \nconsidering the comments received from stakeholders. \n \nThe draft notification envisages following: \n \ni. Green Credit Programme (GCP) is proposed as a market-based mechanism for \nincentivizing environment friendly actions. This will also promote LiFE \nmovement which aims at promoting mindful utilisation of resources.  \nii. Sectors identified for the GCP include - (i) tree plantation, (ii) water conservation, \n(iii) sustainable agriculture, (iv) waste management, (v) air pollution reduction, \n(vi) mangrove conservation and restoration, (vii) Ecomark and (viii) sustainable \ninfrastructure. A phased approach for implementation of the GCP will be adopted. \niii. ICFRE is designated as GCP Administrator and will be responsible for \nimplementation of the GCP including its management, monitoring and operation. \nThis inter-alia will entail development of methodologies, guidelines and \nprocedures for generation of green credits from each identified activity. \niv. Thresholds and benchmarks will be developed for each Green Credit activity for \ngenerating and issuance of Green Credits. Environmental outcome achievable by \nany Green credit activity will be based on equivalence of resource requirement, \nparity of scale, scope, size and other relevant parameters. Digital processes will be \nused for self-assessment of eligible green credit activities, registration of \nactivities, issuance of green credits, monitoring of performance and other relevant \nprocesses. \nv. Rule 12 of the draft notification contains provisions for demand generation. \n \n***** \n \n  \n\n4039 GI/2023 (1) \nरजजस्ट्री सं. डी.एल.- 33004/99 REGD. No. D. L.-33004/99 \n \n \n \nxxxGIDHxxx \nxxxGIDExxx \nऄसाधारण  \nEXTRAORDINARY \nभाग II—खण् ड 3—ईप-खण् ड (ii)  \nPART II—Section 3—Sub-section (ii) \nप्राजधकार  से प्रकाजित \nPUBLISHED BY AUTHORITY \nपयाावरण , वन और जलवायु पररवतान मंत्रालय  \nऄजधसूचना  \nनइ ददल् ली, 26 जून , 2023 \nका.अ.  2779(ऄ).—भारत सरकार ने जलवायु पररवतान से जनपटने , संरक्षण एवं संयम की परंपराओं और मूल् यों \nपर अधाररत स्ट् वस्ट् थ एवं सतत जीवन -िैली का प्रचार -प्रसार करने हेतु पयाावरण संबंधी कायाकलापों में वृजि करने क े  जलए , \nतथा सतत और पयाावरण क े  ऄनुक ू ल जवकास क े  जलए जमीनी स्ट् त र पर एक जन -अंदोलन क े  रूप में ‘लाआफ ’- ‘पयाावरण क े \nजलए जीवन -िैली’ की िुरूअत की ;  \n और , ‘जमिन लाआफ ’, जजसका ईद्देश् य पयाावरण क े  ऄनुक ू ल प्रथाओं को प्रो्‍ सा हन प्रदान करने हेतु \nईपभोक् ता/समसमुदाय को ‍ यवहार संबंधी पररवतानों की ददिा में प्रेररत करक े  सतत जीवन -िैजलयों को प्रो्‍ साजहत करना है  \nआसको बढावा देने हेतु एक पररवताना्‍ म क बाजार -अधारर त तंत्र की अवश् यकता है ;  \n और , आसे सक्षम बनाने हेतु, एक प्रजतस्ट् पधी  बाजार -अधाररत दृजिकोण को बढावा देने क े  जलए राष्‍ट री य"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |676", "t": "Proposals received for Development of Tourism in Unexplored Areas", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.676 \nANSWERED ON 24.07.2023 \n \nPROPOSALS RECEIVED FOR DEVELOPMENT OF \nTOURISM IN UNEXPLORED AREAS \n \n676.  SHRI SELVAM G.:  \nDR. SUBHASH RAMRAO BHAMRE:  \nSHRIMATI MANJULATA MANDAL:  \nDR. DNV SENTHILKUMAR S.:  \nSHRI KULDEEP RAI SHARMA:  \nSHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE:  \nSHRI C.N. ANNADURAI:  \nSHRI DHANUSH M. KUMAR:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the challenges faced by the Government while boosting inflow of \nforeign tourists in the country during the last three years;  \n(b)  whether Government has identified the potential of unexplored \ntourist places and if so, the steps taken by the Government to \ndevelop such unexplored tourist places;  \n(c)  the details of the ongoing schemes for the development and \npromotion of under developed tourist places particularly in Tamil \nNadu, Maharashtra, Odisha, Andaman and Nicobar Islands;  \n(d)  whether the Government has received proposals from various \nStates/UTs for the development and promotion of t ourism in \nunexplored areas and if so, the details thereof and the response of \nthe Government thereto;  \n(e)  the reasons for not being able to develop tourism in the countr y \nthough having lot of potential; and  \n(f)  whether the Government proposes to increa se allocation of funds \nfor tourism infrastructure, promotion, publicity of tourist \ndestinations and products in the domestic and international \nmarkets and if so, the details thereof? \n \n\nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM                (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) to (f): Tourism sector was adversely affected  by Covid 19 pandemic \nwhich caused an unprecedented disruption to tourism promotion with a \nmassive fall of international demand due to widespread lockdowns and \ntravel restrictions put in place by countries in  order to contain the \nspread of the virus.  The international travel dropped by 74.9% in 2020, \nthe worst year on record for tourism.  The tourism sector is  gradually \nrecovering but the recovery measures face challenges for boosting \ninflow of foreign tourists  partly due to global economic slowdown . This \nhas impacted the demand for tourism services. \n \nThe identification, development and promotion of tourism is primarily \nthe responsibility of the concerned State Governments/Union Territory \nAdministrations for tap ping the potential of the tourism destinations \nwithin their States/UTs. However, the Ministry  of Tourism provides \nfinancial assistance to State Governments/Union Territory \nAdministrations under the Swadesh Darshan, Pilgrimage Rejuvenation \nand Spiritual Heritage Augmentation Drive (PRASHAD) and Assistance to \nCentral Agencies Schemes for the develop ment of tourism relate d \ninfrastructure in the country as well as for organizing fairs/festivals & \ntourism related events under Domestic Promotion & Publicity inclu ding \nHospitality (DPPH) Scheme for the development & promotion of tourism \nin the country.  \n \nDetails of projects sanctioned under the Swadesh Darshan and \nPRASHAD Schemes to different States/UTs including Tamil Nadu, \nMaharashtra, Odisha and Andaman and Nicob ar Islands  is given at \nAnnexure-I. \n \nReceiving proposals from the State Governm ents/UT Adminis trations/ \nCentral Agencies for financial assistance for tourism projects is a \ncontinuous process. The proposals received are examined with \nreference to the prescri bed guidelines and financial assistance is \nextended for such projects subject to fulfilment of the stipulated \nconditions, operational viability and availability of funds. \n \nFor the Financial Year 2023-24 an allocation of Rs.2400 crores has been \ngiven to the  Ministry of Tourism. Out of this an amount  \nRs.1,939.22 crore is allocated for i nfrastructure development and an \namount of  Rs.242.00 crore is for promotion and publicity, Rs. 105.00 \ncrore for training and Rs.5.27 crore for Safe Tourist Destination for \nWomen has been allocated. \n*******\n\nANNEXURE I \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (f) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO.676 ANSWERED ON \n24.07.2023 REGARDING PROPOSALS RECEIVED FOR DEVELOPMENT OF TOURISM IN UNEXPLORED AREAS \n \nState wise list of projects sanctioned under Swadesh Darshan and PRASHAD Scheme.  \n(Rs. in crore) \nS. \nNo State/UTs \nSWADESH DARSHAN PRASHAD TOTAL \nNo. of \nProjects \nAmt. \nSanctioned \nAmount \nreleased \nNo. of \nProjects \nAmt. \nSanctioned \nAmount \nreleased \nAmt. \nSanctioned \nAmount \nreleased \n1 Andhra Pradesh 3 141.53 141.53 3 124.89 70.85 266.42 212.38 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 2 146.49 139.16 1 37.88 7.34 184.37 146.5 \n3 Assam 2 185.66 176.36 1 29.8 29.8 215.46 206.16 \n4 Bihar 5 262.73 248.06 2 45.81 36.14 308.54 284.2 \n5 Chhattisgarh 1 96.10 94.23 1 43.33 24.33 139.43 118.56 \n6 Goa 2 197.00 192.11 0 0 0 197 192.11 \n7 Gujarat 3 176.22 165.74 5 205.53 113.48 381.75 279.22 \n8 Haryana 1 70.91 77.88 1 49.52 28.77 120.43 106.65 \n9 Himachal Pradesh 1 68.34 64.55 0 0 0 68.34 64.55 \n10 Jammu & Kashmir 6 519.59 440.01 1 40.46 34"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |677", "t": "Scheme of Siddharthnagar Villages", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO- 677 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON- 24/07/2023 \n  \nSCHEME OF SIDDHARTHNAGAR VILLAGES \n  \n677. SHRI JAGDAMBIKA PAL:      \n \nWill the Minister of TRIBAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) whether the Government has included any villages of Siddharthnagar under the Scheme; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the progress made so far in these villages;  \n(c) the details of the amount sanctioned by the Government under the programme during the last \nfive years in Uttar Pradesh; and \n(d) whether the Government proposes to any alternative of the scheme; if so, the details thereof \nand if not, the reasons therefor? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TRIBAL AFFAIRS  \n(SMT. RENUKA SINGH SARUTA) \n  \n(a) to (c): Government has revamped the earlier Scheme of ‘Special Central Assistance to Tribal \nSub-Scheme (SCA to TSS) with nomenclature ‘Pradhan Mantri Adi Adarsh Gram Yojna \n(PMAAGY)’, for implementation during 2021-22 to 2025-26, which aims at integrated \ndevelopment of  villages with significant tribal population. It is envisaged to cover 36,428 villages \nhaving at least 50% tribal population and 500 STs across States / UTs with notified STs. This \nincludes 183 villages in Uttar Pradesh. Since there is a criteria of 50% tribal population and 500 \nSTs in a village for selection under the scheme of PMAAGY, villages of Siddharthanagar are not \ncovered under the scheme.  The District-wise details of the villages selected under the scheme of \nPMAAGY in State of Uttar Pradesh is under: \n \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n\nS.No. Name of District No. of Villages \n1.   \nBahraich 3 \n2.   \nBalrampur 16 \n3.   \nBijnor 1 \n4.   \nKheri 32 \n5.   \nLalitpur 2 \n6.   \nMirzapur 1 \n7.   \nShrawasti 2 \n8.   \nSonbhadra 126 \nTotal 183 \n  \nMinistry of Tribal Affairs had provided financial assistance to Government of Uttar Pradesh under \nthe erstwhile scheme of Special Central Assistance to Tribal Sub-Scheme (SCA to TSS) till 2020-\n21 against the activities related to education, health, sanitation, water supply, livelihood, skill \ndevelopment etc. The details of the funds released to the Government of Uttar Pradesh during last \nfive years under the scheme of ‘SCA to TSS’ / ‘PMAAGY’ is as under: \nRs. in lakh) \nName of State Name of Scheme Year Fund Released \n  Scheme of Special Central Assistance to \nTribal Sub-Scheme (SCA to TSS) \n2017-18 458.35 \nUttar Pradesh 2018-19 0.00 \n2019-20 779.91 \n2020-21 508.83 \n  \nUnder PMAAGY funds could not be released to Uttar Pradesh for FY 2021-22 and 2022-23 \nbecause of pending Utilization Certificates and unspent balance available with states. \n  \n(d): Since, the Government has only recently revamped the earlier Scheme of ‘Special Central \nAssistance to Tribal Sub-Scheme (SCA to TSS) into ‘Pradhan Mantri Adi Adarsh Gram Yojna \n(PMAAGY)’, there is no proposal for considering alternative of the scheme. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |678", "t": "e-Invoicing", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF REVENUE \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 678 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY, JULY 24, 2023/SRAVANA 2, 1945 (SAKA) \n \nE-INVOICING \n678. Shri Shrirang Appa Barne:  \nShri Prataprao Jadhav:  \nShri Dhairyasheel Sambhajirao Mane:  \nShri Bidyut Baran Mahato:  \nShri Sanjay Sadashivrao Mandlik:  \nShri Sudheer Gupta:  \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state:- \n(a) the details of present criteria followed by the Government for e-invoicing; \n(b) whether the Governme nt on the recommendations of GST council has widened the \nrequirement for e-invoicing or real-time reporting of business-to-business sales;  \n(c) if so, the details thereof; \n(d) the manner in which this new system is going to plug revenue leakages on account  of input \ntax credit frauds; \n(e) the total amount of additional revenue likely to be generated by the Government by \nintroducing the e-invoicing during the current fiscal year; and \n(f) the other steps taken/being taken by the Government to improve tax compl iance in the \ncountry? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FINANCE  \n \n(SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY) \n \n(a) As per the present provisions, a registered person, whose annual turnover exceeds Rs 10 crore \nin any of the preceding financial year from 2017 -18 onwards, is required to issue e -invoices in \nrespect of the supply of goods or services or both made to a registered person (B2B), SEZs, or \nfor exports and deemed exports.  \n\n(b) and (c) Electronic invoicing system (e -invoice) has been introduced in a phased manner as \nper the recommendations made by the GST Council. E -invoicing was made mandatory for \nsupply of goods or services or both made to a registered person (B2B), SEZs, or for exports and \ndeemed exports in respect of registered person s with turnover exceeding ₹500 c rore with effect \nfrom 01.10.2020. This threshold of turnover has subseq uently been revised to Rs. 100 c rore \nw.e.f. 01.01.2021, to Rs. 50 crore w.e.f. 01.04.2021, to Rs. 20 crore w.e.f. 01.04.2022 and to Rs. \n10 crore w.e.f. 01.10.2022. This threshold is further being reduced to Rs 5 crore with effect from \n01.08.2023. \n(d) E-invoice helps in easing the process of filing returns and also helps in ‘invoice matching’ \nand assists in the seamles s flow of input tax credit across the supply chain, thereby reducing \ncases of frauds and other malpractices under GST regime. \n(e) No such estimates of additional revenue likely to be generated specifically on account of e -\ninvoicing can be made. \n(f) Some of the major initiatives / policy measures / reforms undertaken/ being undertaken by \nGovernment, on the recommendations of the GST Council, to improve tax compliance in the \ncountry are as follows: \n(i) To bring discipline in return filing and to promote timely fi ling of GSTR -3B returns, \nrestriction has been made on filing of FORM GSTR -1, if taxpayer fails to furnish the \nFORM GSTR-3B return for preceding tax period.  \n(ii) Provision has also been made to make filing of FORM GSTR -1 mandatory before filing \nof FORM GSTR -3B for a tax period and also for mandating sequential filing of FORM \nGSTR-1.  \n(iii)Amendments have been made to limit the input tax credit availment to the extent the \ndetails of such supply have been furnished by the supplier in his FORM GSTR -1 and \nwhich has been made available to the taxpayer in his FORM GSTR-2B.  \n(iv) To encourage self -discipline and self -compliance by the taxpayers, provision has been \nmade for system -based intimation of the difference in liability in FORM GSTR -1 and \nFORM GSTR-3B for a tax period, to the taxpayer on the common portal, for enabling the \ntaxpayer to either pay the differential liability or explain the difference.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |679", "t": "National Book Trust", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 679 \nANSWERED ON 24.07.2023 \n \nNational Book Trust \n \n679.  SHRI D.K. SURESH: \n SHRI NALIN KUMAR KATEEL: \n SHRIMATI SUMALATHA AMBAREESH: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether it is tru e that National Book Trust (NBT)  is promoting and publishing books in \nvarious Indian Languages and if so, the number of books published in each Indian language \nduring the last three years, year-wise and language-wise; \n(b) whether the Government fe els that there is need to expand the function s of National Book \nTrust (NBT) to cover more people of all Indian languages, if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government has taken any steps to further expand the functions of NBT to cover \nmore people of all age groups in Indian languages and if so, the details thereof; \n(d) whether the Government is aware that there are huge number of posts in NBT lying vacant for \nthe last many years and if so, the details of the posts sanctioned and vacant in NBT, language-\nwise; and \n(e) whether the Government has taken any steps to fill up of the vacant posts to ensure better \nfunctioning of the NBT and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n \n( DR. SUBHAS SARKAR ) \n \n(a) Yes, Sir. National Book Trust (NBT), India is an autonomous organization under \nMinistry of Education, Government of India, established in 1957 to publish and promote \nbooks in India and create a culture of reading.  The detail of number of the books \npublished in each Indian language during the last three years, year -wise and language -\nwise are provided at Annexure-I.  \n \n(b) NBT has a regular publishing programme in 14 Indian languages which includes                       \nAsamiya, Bangla, Bodo, Gujarati, Hindi, Kannada, Kashmiri, Malayalam, Manipuri, \nMaithili, Marathi, Nepali, Odia, Punjabi, Sanskrit, Sindhi, T amil, Telugu, Urdu and \nEnglish. NBT has been continuously making efforts to publish in more Indian languages \nincluding Tribal languages. In this context 36 languages have been added including Ao \nNaga, Asuri, Bhojpuri, Bhili, Bhatri, Biho, Birajia, Bhumij, Dhubri, Garhwali, Garo, \nGondi, Ho, Halbi, Haryanavi, Himachali, Khadia, Khasi, Kokborok, Konkani, Kuduk, \nKumauni, Lepcha, Bhutia, Malto, Malavi, Mising, Mundari, Limboo, Mizo, Newari, \nNimari, Mag ahi, Rajasthani, Tibetan, Santali, among others.   As part  of NEP \nimplementation Plan, NBT has also started publishing bilingual editions of its books and \nthe same have been published in Hindi-English, English-Telugu, English-Tamil, English-\nPunjabi, English-Marathi and English-Urdu. \n  \n\n(c)     Yes, Sir. The steps taken are provided at Annexure –II. \n  \n \n(d) The details of the posts sanctioned and vacant in National Book Trust, India is as under: \n \nSanctioned \nposts \n  Filled \n  posts \nVacant \nposts \n   275 + 15*     153+13*   122+02* \n  \n* language-wise posts in editorial section \n  \n \n(e) The Revision of the Recruitment rules to fill up the vacant post is a continuous process. \n*** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n\nANNEXURE-I \n \nANNEXURE AS REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF THE LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 679 FOR ANSWER ON 24.07.2023 REGARDING \nNATIONAL BOOK TRUST ASKED BY SHRI D.K. SURESH, SHRI NALIN KUMAR \nKATEEL AND SHRIMATI SUMALATHA AMBAREESH, HON’BLE MP. \n \nS.no. Language Books Published in the \nyear 2019-20 \nBooks Published in \nthe year 2020-21 \nBooks Published in \nthe year 2021-22 \n1.  Asamiya 13 82 80 \n2.  Asuri 3 - 3 \n3.  Bangla 144 44 171 \n4.  Bhatri - - 3 \n5.  Bhojpuri - 1 3 \n6.  Bhumij 2 - 3 \n7.  Bhutia 1 - - \n8.  Biho 3 - 3 \n9.  Bilingual - 20 172 \n10.  Birajia 3 - 2 \n11.  Bodo - 35 6 \n12.  English 440 673 699 \n13.  Garo - - 6 \n14.  Gondi - - 3 \n15.  Gujarati   275 88 5 \n16.  Halbi - - 1 \n17.  Haryanvi - 28  \n18.  Himachali - - 1 \n19.  Hindi 756 854 1235 \n20.  Ho 7 - 3 \n21.  Kannada 15 73 67 \n22.  Kashmiri -  1 - \n23.  Khadiya 6 - 5 \n24.  Khasi 2 - 6 \n25.  Kokborok 10 - - \n26.  Konkani 1 - - \n27.  Kudukh 6 - 6 \n28.  Magahi - - 2 \n29.  Maithili - 1 4 \n30.  Malayalam 53 14 33 \n31.  Malto - - 2 \n32.  Manipuri - 15 - \n33.  Marathi 56 61 44 \n34.  Mato 2 - - \n35.  Mizo 8 1 - \n36.  Mundari 7 - 6 \n37.  Nepali - - 24 \n38.  Odia   136 202 181 \n39.  Punjabi   22 61 90 \n40.  Sanskrit 6 5 1 \n41.  Santali - - 5 \n42.  Spanish 12 - - \n43.  Tamil 10 40 92 \n44.  Telugu 9 82 27 \n45.  Urdu 5 28 9 \n Total 2013 2409 3003 \n \n \n  \n\nANEXURE-II \n \nANNEXURE AS REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (c) OF THE LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 679 FOR ANSWER ON 24.07.2023 REGARDING \nNATIONAL BOOK TRUST ASKED BY SHRI D.K. SURESH, SHRI NALIN KUMAR \nKATEEL AND SHRIMATI SUMALATHA AMBAREESH, HON’BLE MP. \n \n(a) Books in Tribal Languages \nWith an objective to promote all Major, Minor and Tribal languages of India, National Book \nTrust (NBT), India has been bringing out books "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |68", "t": "Water Storage Infrastructure", "head": "O.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nDEPARTMENT OF ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nLOK SABHA \nADMITTED STARRED QUESTION NO. *68 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25th July, 2023 \n \nRASHTRIYA KAMDHENU AAYOG \n \n*68 . MS LOCKET CHATTERJEE  \n        SHRIMATI NAVNEET RAVI RANA \n \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nमत्स्यपालन, पशुपालन और डेयरी मंत्री \n be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the aims and objectives of establishing Rashtriya Kamdhenu Aayog (RKA) and \nthe achievements made so far since its inception; \n \n(b) the details of the works done for conservation and enhancement of indigenous \nbreeds of cows in the country under the Rashtriya Gokul Mission during the last three \nyears, State-wise; \n \n(c) the number of Gokul Grams sanctioned in West  Bengal, Madhya Pradesh, \nMaharashtra particularly in Amravati and Bihar under the said mission; \n \n(d) whether the Government proposes to increase the number of Gokul Grams \nthereunder and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(e) the steps taken by the Government for the  protection and development of the \nlivestock population as well as for the conservation of indigenous breeds in the country? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n(Shri Parshottam Rupala) \n(a) to (e):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *68 RASHTRIYA KAMDHENU AAYOG TO BE \nANSWERED ON 25th July 2023.  \n \n(a) The Rashtriya Kamdhenu Aayog  (RKA) was constituted vide Resolution No. 3 -\n260/2019-AHT (RGM) dated 21st February, 2019 as an Advisory body and as an \nintegral part of Rashtriya Gokul Mission (RGM). \n \nRKA was constituted for the purpose of conservation, protection and development of \ncows and their progeny. It was assigned to develop policy frame work and shape \nguidelines aimed at formulat ing policy to provide direction to the implementation of \nschemes related to cattle genetic up-gradation of genetic resources. \n \nThe members of the RKA vis ited Gaushala, Stray Cattle homes etc in the country for \nconsultative meetings. Also conducted several meetings with Hon’ble Governor, AH \nMinisters, Universities, Gau sewa Aayogs of States etc. and discussed on related \nissues. The members of RKA visited ac ademic institutions and universities and had \ninteraction about the cows and its development with regard to scientific, economical, \nenvironmental, spiritual aspects. \n \n(b) The details of work done for conservation  and  enhancement of Indigenous breed of \ncows in the country under Rashtriya Gokul Mission (RGM) state-wise during last 3 years \nis at Annexure-I. \n \n(c) & (d) Following is the details of the Gokul Grams sanctioned u nder Rashtriya Gokul \nMission in the State of Bihar, Madhya Pradesh, Maharashtra and West Bengal:   \n \nState  No of Gokul Gram \nsanctioned \nLocation of Gokul Gram \nBihar 1 Dumraon,  Buxar \nMadhya Pradesh 1 Ratona, Sagar \nMaharashtra 2 Pohara, Amravati \nTathawade, Pune \nWest Bengal - No proposal was received from the State \n \nAs per the revised and realigned guidelines of Rashtriya Gokul Mission for 2021-2022 to \n2025-2026, the component has been discontinued under the scheme.  However, \nGovernment of India is undertaking establishment of breed multiplication farms \npreferably of indigenous breeds and under the component 50% capital subsidy is \navailable for entrepreneurs  (Individual / Self Help Groups/Farmer Cooperative \norganizations / Farmer Producer organizations /Joint Liability Groups and section 8 \ncompanies). \n  \n\n(e) Following major steps have been undertaken by Government of India for the \ndevelopment and conservation of indigenous breeds: \n \n(i) Nationwide AI porogramme  for extension of Artificial Insemination coverage  \n \n(ii) Progeny testing and pedigree selection for product ion of high genetic merit bulls  \nof indigenous breeds.  \n \n(iii) Establishment of Breed Multiplication Farms preferably of indigenous breeds  \n \n(iv) Promotion of In-vitro Fertilization (IVF) technology for faster genetic up gradation \nof indigenous breeds  \n \n(v) Implementation of genomic selection using genomic chip.  \n \n(vi) Sex sorted semen production for indigenous breeds  \n \n(vii) Funds have been released to the States for establishment of 16 Gokul and 2  \nNational Kamdhenu Breeding Centres. \n\nAnnexure-I \nState-wise achievements made under Rashtriya Gokul Mission for Development and conservation of \nIndigenous breeds \nSN Name of \nState \nPedigree \nSelection \nProgramm\ne \nProgeny \nTesting \nNo. of \nGokul \nGrams   \nNo. of \nNational \nKamdhe\nnu \nBreeding \nCenter \nIVF \nLabs \nNationwide AI programme (NAIP) \nAnimal \nCovered \nAI done Farmers \nbenefitted \n1 Andhra \nPradesh \n-  1 1 1 2 4122110 5842406 2244607 \n2 Arunachal \nPradesh \n-    1   - 3187 3332 1527 \n3 Assam  -       - 1014449 1159717 869352 \n4 Bihar  -   1   2 2130026 2483141 1691520 \n5 Chhattisgarh  -   1   1 1349355 1566516 863260 \n6 Goa "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |680", "t": "Cases registered under GST Invoice Frauds", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF REVENUE \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 680 \nTo be answered on Monday, July 24, 2023/Sravana 2, 1945 (Saka) \n \n“CASES REGISTERED UNDER GST INVOICE FRAUDS” \n \n680. SHRI ADALA PRABHAKARA REDDY: \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state:  \n(a) the total number of cases registered under GST invoice frauds during the last five \nyears, State/UT-wise; \n(b) the total number of persons arrested under the same; \n(c) the total amount of frauds detected and recovered under the same; and \n(d) the measures undertaken/being undertaken by the Government to curb such GST \ninvoice related frauds? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN MINISTRY OF FINANCE \n(SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY) \n \n(a), (b) and (c): The total number of cases registered by Central GST under GST \ninvoice frauds during the period 2018 -19 to 2022 -23, State/UT wise, alongwith the \namount detected, recovered and arrests made is as under:  \n State/UT \n \nNo. of \ncases \nDetection         \n(Rs. in \nCrore) \nRealization     \n(Rs. in Crore) \n \nNo. of \npersons \narrested \nAndaman & Nicobar \nIslands 0 0 0 0 \nAndhra Pradesh 490 667 386 9 \nArunachal Pradesh 10 7 1 0 \nAssam 250 519 129 4 \nBihar 185 698 25 0 \nChandigarh 58 60 18 2 \nChhattisgarh 591 1892 354 27 \nDaman & Diu and \nDadra Nagar Haveli 53 118 18 3 \nDelhi 754 14901 780 139 \nGoa 39 13 4 0 \nGujarat 4486 11949 1027 153 \nHaryana 3454 12488 833 94 \n \n  \n\nHimachal Pradesh 98 340 21 0 \nJammu & Kashmir 42 84 41 0 \nJharkhand 315 1196 51 4 \nKarnataka 1076 4847 536 25 \nKerala 202 529 34 9 \nLadakh 0 0 0 0 \nLakshadweep 0 0 0 0 \nMadhya Pradesh 458 978 202 16 \nMaharashtra 5021 30013 2215 272 \nManipur 19 15 7 0 \nMeghalaya 30 53 11 0 \nMizoram 3 1 1 0 \nNagaland 10 16 0 0 \nOdisha 670 2357 108 17 \nPuducherry 22 28 2 0 \nPunjab 221 1621 140 57 \nRajasthan 1151 5249 487 81 \nSikkim 30 10 6 0 \nTamil Nadu 2556 3894 362 90 \nTelangana 1113 3275 728 61 \nTripura 71 98 5 0 \nUttar Pradesh 758 9471 1131 91 \nUttarakhand 189 2202 215 44 \nWest Bengal 1829 5867 465 41 \nGrand Total 26254 115457 10346 1239 \n(d) Details of the measures taken by the Government to prevent GST invoice related \nfrauds are as under: \n \n1. Using robust data analytics and artificial intelligence to identify and track \nrisky taxpayers and detect tax evasion; \n2. Carrying out special all India drive to weed out fake/bogus registrations from \n16.05.2023 to 15.07.2023; \n3. Insertion of sub -rule (4A) in rule 8 of CGST Rules, 2017 to provide for risk \nbased biometric-based Aadhaar authentication of registration applicants; \n4. Amendment in rule 9 of CGST Rules, 2017 to provide for physical \nverification in high risk cases, even when Aadhaar has been authenticated; \n5. Amendment in rule 10 A of CGST Rules, 2017 to provide for requirem ent of \nbank account to be furnished as a part of registration process to be in the name of the \nregistered persons and obtained on PAN of the registered person and also linked with \nAadhaar in case of proprietorship firm. Also, the GST Council in its 50 th meeting has \nrecommended further amendment in rule 10A to provide that the details of bank \naccount will be required to be furnished within 30 days of grant of registration or \nbefore filing of GSTR-1, whichever is earlier; \n\n6. Restriction on availment of ITC to  invoices and debit notes furnished by the \nsupplier in their statement of outward supplies; \n7. Sharing of data with partner law enforcement agencies for more targeted \ninterventions; \n8. Reduction in threshold limit for issue of e -invoice for B2B transactions from \nRs. 10 crore to Rs. 5 crore, w.e.f. 01.08.2023; \n9. Restriction on generation of e-way bills by non- compliant taxpayers; \n10. Making the beneficial owner liable for penal action a nd prosecution similar to \nthat of actual supplier/recipient, in cases where a supply has been made without the \nissuance of an invoice, or invoice has been issued without supply, or excess ITC has \nbeen availed/distributed; and \n11. Amendment in Section 83 of  the CGST Act to provide that provisional \nattachment of property can be done in respect of any other person who has retained \nbenefits of such transactions. \n****** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |681", "t": "Total Expenditure Budget", "head": " \nGovernment of India \nMinistry of Finance \nDepartment of Economic Affairs \n*** \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.681 \nANSWERED ON MONDAY, JULY 24, 2023 \n(SRAVANA 2, 1945-SAKA) \n \nTOTAL EXPENDITURE BUDGET \n \n681. SHRI S. VENKATESAN \n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state? \n(a)  the details of the amount allocated to Scheduled Caste Sub Plan (SCSP) and the Tribal Sub \nPlan (TSP) and the details of the total expenditure budget during the last five financial \nyears; \n(b)  the details of the amount spent under SCSP and TSP during the last five financial years; \n(c)  the details of the amount under Divisible schemes of SCSP and TSP during the last five \nfinancial years; \n(d)  whether the Government has taken care of unspent amount of SCSP/TSP in any year by \ncarrying over to next financial year during the last five financial years; and \n(e)  if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE \n(SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY) \n(a), (b) & (c): The guidelines for allocation of Budget towards Scheduled Castes Sub Plan (SCSP) \nand Tribal Sub Plan (TSP) are issued by NITI Aayog and final allocations are made by respective \nMinistries/Departments accordingly. The details of budget allocations & utilization under SCSP \nand TSP for last five years, Ministry -wise, scheme- wise and year -wise are available in the \nStatement 10-A & 10-B of the Expenditure Profile of respective years. \n(d) & (e) Government of India follows cash based budgeting under which the unutilized budget \nallocations lapse at the end of financial year. Hence, the unspent balances cannot be carried \nforward to next financial year. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |682", "t": "e-waste", "head": "OIH \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 682 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 24.07.2023 \n \ne-waste \n \n682. SHRI BALAK NATH: \nSHRI FEROZE VARUN GANDHI: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether there is any estimate of e-waste generated in the country and if so, the details \nthereof; \n(b) the details of the action taken by the Government against the violators of the provisions of \nthe e-waste (Management and Handling) Rules, 2011; \n(c) the measures taken by the Government to reduce the number of the persons engaged in \nrecycling/dismantling of e-waste in the country drastically specially in Rajasthan during \nthe last three years; \n(d) whether as per the e-waste management law, the Central Pollution Control Board and \nState Pollution Control Board have to conduct random checks on the registered \ncompanies on e-waste collection/ recycling targets; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof the random checks that have been conducted by the Government \nso far in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n(a) The Central Pollution Control Board (CPCB) estimates the e-waste generation at national \nlevel based on the countrywide sales data provided by producers, and average life of notified \nelectrical and electronic equipment (EEE), as mandated under the E-waste Management \nRules, 2016. As per the CPCB, e-waste generated in the country from twenty-one (21) types \nof EEE notified under the E-Waste (Management) Rules, 2016 in the financial year (FY) \n2020-21 and 2021-22 was estimated as 13,46,496.31 Tonnes and 16,01,155.36 tonnes  \nrespectively. \n    \n(b) & (c )  Based on the inspections carried out by CPCB and State Pollution Control Boards \n(SPCBs)/Pollution Control Committees (PCCs), violation of E-Waste (Management) Rules, \n2016 was reported in case of ten (10) Extended Producer Responsibility (EPR) Authorised \nProducers. Accordingly, in April 2019, their EPR Authorisation were suspended. SPCBs \nverified more than 400 collection centers of these ten (10) producers submitted its \nverification reports to CPCB. Suspension of EPR authorisation was later revoked in case of \neight (08) producers on the basis of corrective measures taken by them and their verification. \nFurther, CPCB in January 2020 carried out inspection of three authorized dismantlers and \nrecyclers in the State of Uttar Pradesh and they were found to be violating E-Waste \n(Management) Rules, 2016 and Implementation Guidelines for E-Waste (Management) \nRules, 2016. Accordingly, CPCB issued direction under section 18(1)(b) of the Water \n\n(Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1974 and the Air (Prevention and Control of \nPollution) Act 1981 to Uttar Pradesh Pollution Control Board in May, 2020 for taking action \nagainst them. In September 2022 CPCB issued directions under Section 18(1) (b) of the \nWater (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act 1974, and Air (Prevention and Control of \nPollution) Act 1981 for checking Informal E-Waste activities, verification of authorized \ndismantlers/recyclers of E-waste and drives for mass awareness to all SPCBs/PCCs. The \nGovernment has not taken any step to reduce the number of the persons engaged in \nrecycling/dismantling of e-waste in the country rather proposes to promote recycling sector. \n \n(d) & (e)  Ministry has comprehensively revised the previous set of Rules and notified the \nE-Waste (Management) Rules, 2022 in November, 2022 and the same is enforce since 1st \nApril, 2023.  As per the provision of these rules, CPCB has to conduct random check for \nascertaining compliance of the e-waste rules and may take help of Customs/ State \nGovernment or any other agency (ies) and SPCBs/PCCs have to conduct random inspection \nof recycler and refurbisher and monitor recycling capacity utilization. Following \ninspections/ verifications have been carried out in recent years by CPCB/SPCBs/PCCs: \n \n The Regional Directorate Bhopal, Bengaluru, Kolkata, Vadodara and Lucknow of \nCPCB carried inspections/ verifications of 33 dismantlers and recyclers during \nMarch to May 2019.  \n In the year 2019, based on the directions of CPCB for compliance verification of \ndismantler/ Recyclers, 17 SPCBs/PCCs inspected around two hundred and sixty-\nthree (263) dismantling and recycling facilities. \n In the Year 2018, CPCB directed all SPCBs/PCCs to carry out verification of \ndismantlers/ Recyclers. Based on the reports submitted, 09 SPCBs/PCCs have \ninspected around 167 dismantlers & recycler. \n An Action Plan for enforcement of E-Waste (Management) Rules, across the \ncountry is in place and is being implemented by all the SPCBs)/PCCs. The Action \nPlan contains action points on inspections of dismantlers/ recycler, verification of \nfacilities of producers and also checking informal e-waste units. During financial \nyear (FY) 2019-"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |683", "t": "Voluntary Exit of Corporate Companies", "head": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CORPORATE AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 683 \nANSWERED ON MONDAY THE 24TH JULY, 2023 \nSRAVANA 2, 1945 (SAKA) \n \nVOLUNTARY EXIT OF CORPORATE COMPANIES \nQUESTION \n683. SHRI CHANDRA SEKHAR BELLANA: \n        SHRI SANJAY KAKA PATIL: \n           \nWill the Minister of CORPORATE AFFAIRS                                                      \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) the number of corporate companies that have voluntarily exited during the last \nfive years; \n \n(b) the average time and process that is currently taken for a corporate company to \nvoluntarily wind up and exit; \n \n(c) whether there is any mechanism in place that is comparable to the international \nstandards; \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(e) the number of cases of voluntary corporate exit that are currently pending in the \ncountry? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE MINISTRY OF STATISTICS AND \nPROGRAMME IMPLEMENTATION; MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF \nTHE MINISTRY OF PLANNING; AND MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF \nCORPORATE AFFAIRS \n                                                                                           [RAO INDERJIT SINGH] \n(a) (b) & (e):   Voluntary exit of companies is covered under Section 248(2) of the \nCompanies Act, 2013 and Section 59 of the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Cod e \n(IBC), 2016.  Under Section 248(2) of the Companies Act, 2013, a company that \nseeks to have its name removed from th e Register of Companies is to apply to \nthe Registrar of Companies (RoC).  On receipt of the application, the RoC seeks \npublic objections by way of newspaper advertisements and  publication in the \nOfficial Gazette.  After receipt of objections, if any, RoC scrutinizes the whole \ncase and approves the application of the company, if found in order.  The same \nis then published in the Official Gazette  and from the date of publication, the \ncompany’s name is struck off.   \n \n\n2 \n \nIn compliance with Section 59 of the IBC, 2016, companies which intend to \nliquidate themselves voluntarily and which have not committed any default may \ninitiate voluntary liquidation proceedings.  A fter passing special resolution of \nMembers and after consent of the creditors holding 2/3rd of the value of debt, if \nany, a Liquidator is appointed.  The Liquidator, after following due process  for \nrealization of the assets and distribution of the proceeds to the creditors and \nshareholders, submits the final report to the Adjudicating Authority, in this case \nthe NCLT.  \n \nFrom 1.04.2018 to 31.03.2023, 96,261 companies have exited voluntarily under \nsection 248(2) of the Companies Act, 2013. Under section 59 of the IBC, 2016 \nfinal reports of 1013 companies have been submitted by Liquidators, of which \nfinal dissolution orders have been passed by the NCLT in 510 cases during the \nsaid period.  \n \nIn the last 5 years , the time taken for voluntary exit under Section 248(2)  of \nCompanies Act, 2013  has varied between  an average of approximately 6 -8 \nmonths to even 12-18 months in some cases.  Under the IBC, the average time \ntaken for dissolution of the company after submission of final report by the \nliquidator has been in the range of 7 -9 months.  The average time taken by \nliquidator to submit the final report for adjudication to the NCLT has been about \n14 months. \n \nKeeping the above in view, the Central Government has set up the Center for \nProcessing Accelerated Corporate Exit (CPACE) to centralize and expedite \nvoluntarily exit of the companies  under Section 248(2) of the Companies Act, \n2013 at one central place at Manesar .  Since its operationalization on \n01.05.2023, the tim e taken for voluntary exit has been found to be about 4 \nmonths. \n \n520 cases are currently pending for voluntary liquidation under section 59 of \nthe Code till March 2023. Further 11,037 cases are pending for voluntary \ncorporate exit under section 248(2) of the Companies Act, 2013 till date. \n \n(c) & (d): The Report of the Bankruptcy Law Reforms Committee which was the \nprecursor to the enactment of the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code, 2016 (Code) \nin 2016 had considered the well-established principles of collective insolvency \nresolution regime provided in UNCITRAL Legislative Guide on Insolvency while \nrecommending the framework of the Code. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |684", "t": "Public-Private Partnership", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF ECONOMIC AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 684 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 24th July, 2023  \n \nPublic Private Partnership  \n \nPQ No. 684. SHRI JAYADEV GALLA:  \n \nWill the Minister of Finance be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has entered into Public Private Partnership (PPP) \nagreements with the private sector companies; \n(b) if so, the number of PPPs entered by the Government from 2014 onwards in \nthe road, power and gas distribution sector classified year-wise; \n(c)  the list of these projects in the road, power and gas distribution along with the \ndetails of the concessionaire involved under each project along with the cost \nof the project from 2014; \n(d) The total number of tender bids that have been received fo r each project for \nthe purpose; and \n(e) The list of PPP projects that the Government is willing to call for tenders in the \nnext five years, sector wise? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN MINISTRY OF FINANCE \n(SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY) \n \n \n(a): Yes.  \n(b) to (e) : PPP projects in road, power and gas distribution are undertaken by the \nrespective line ministries  on the basis of several factors such as demand assessment, \nmarket appetite, financial viability  and suitability of the project for PPP . The inputs \nreceived from the respective line ministries is placed at Annexure-I. \n***** \n  \n\nAnnexure - I \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (b) TO (e) OF LOK \nSABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO.684 ON 24TH July, 2023  \n                                                                                   \nSl Name of Project Name of \nConcessionaire \nTPC \n(Rs in cr) \nNo of \nbidders  \n1  \n4 laning of Solapur - Akkalkot from \nkm 99.400 to km 138.352 section of \nNH-150E \nM/s GR Infra 807 4 \n2  4-laning of Sangli- Solapur (Pkg-I) \nSolapur to Borgaon section of NH-166 M/s DBL 1102.4 5 \n3  \n4-laning of Sangli- Solapur (Pkg-II) \nBorgaon to Watambare) section of NH-\n166 \nM/s DBL 1029.4 6 \n4  \n4-laning of Sangli – Solapur (Pkg-III) \nWatambare to Mangalwedha section of \nNH-166 \nM/s GR Infra 957 7 \n5  \n4-laning of Sangli- Solapur (Pkg-IV) \nMangalwedha to Solapur section of \nNH- 166 \nM/s DBL 1141 6 \n6  \n8-laning of Vadape at Km 539.202 to \nThane at km 563.000 Section of NH-\n848 \nM/s MEP Infra \