tar Pradesh 7 7 \n29 Uttarakhand 4 4 \n30 West Bengal 3 3 \n  Total 198 191 \nComponent B- Dairying through Cooperatives \n1 Andhra Pradesh 1 1 \n2 Bihar 4 4 \n3 Madhya Pradesh 1 1 \n4 Punjab 2 2 \n5 Rajasthan 5 5 \n6 Telangana 1 1 \n7 Uttar Pradesh 7 7 \n8 Uttarakhand 1 1 \n Total 22 22 \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |769", "t": "Beneficiaries under PMAY-G", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 769 \n  ANSWERED ON 25/07/2023 \n \nBENEFICIARIES UNDER PMAY-G \n \n769.  SHRI PRATAP CHANDRA SARANGI: \nSHRI SANGAM LAL GUPTA: \nSHRI C.R. PATIL: \nSHRI ANURAG SHARMA: \nDR. RAMAPATI RAM TRIPATHI: \nSHRI P.P. CHAUDHARY: \nSHRI SURESH KUMAR KASHYAP: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the number of houses completed out of sanctioned under \nPradhan Mantri Awaas Yojana-Gramin (PMAY-G); \n(b)  the number of houses allocated and completed, State and \ndistrict-wise particularly in Balasore district of Odisha, \nPratapgarh, Jhansi and Deoria districts of Uttar Pradesh, \nGujarat, Pali district of Rajasthan and Shimla district of \nHimachal Pradesh; \n(c)  the mechanism adopted by the ministry to ensure that only \ngenuine beneficiaries are selected to avail the benefit of the \nsaid yojana; \n(d)  the details of the parameters used by the ministry in selecting \nthe beneficiaries; \n(e)  whether any me chanism has been devised by the ministry for \neffective monitoring of the programme; and \n(f)  if so, the details thereof and if not, reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a):  In order to achieve the target of “Housing for All” in rural areas, \nthe Ministry of Rural Development is implementing Pradhan  Mantri \n\nAwaas Yojana- Gramin (PMAY-G) with effect from 1 st April, 2016 to \nprovide assistance to eligible rural households with an  overall \ntarget to construct 2.95 crorepucca houses with basic amenities by \nMarch, 2024.  Against the target of 2.95 crore houses, a total of 2.92 \ncrore houses have been sanctioned to the beneficiaries by various \nStates/Union Territories and 2.41 crore houses have been completed \nas on 19.07.2023.   \n(b):  The State/UT-wise number of houses allocated and completed \nunder PMAY-G is given at Annexure I.   The District level data of all \nStates/UTs related to houses allocated and completed can be seen \nat programme  website at www.pmayg.nic.in ---->AwaasSoft----\n>Reports---->Houses progress against the target financial year. \n  \nThe details of number of houses allocated, sanctioned and \ncompleted particularly in Balasore district of Odisha, Pratapgarh, \nJhansi and Deoria districts of Uttar Pradesh, Gujarat, Pali district of \nRajasthan and Shimla district of Himachal Pradesh is given at \nAnnexure II. \n(c) & (d) : Under PMAY -G, the beneficiaries have been identified \nbased on the housing deprivation parameters prescribed under \nSocio Economic Caste Census (SECC) 2011. After due verification by \nthe Gram Sabha and completion of Appellate Process, Gram \nPanchayat-wise Permanent Wait List (PWL) is prepared. The auto \ngenerated priority list of households from SECC, 2011 database was \nprovided to the States/Union Territories (UTs) for conducting Gram \nSabha meetings to finalize the PWL. As on 19.07.2023, a total of \n2.04 crore households have been identified and included in PWL \nfrom SECC, 2011. Further, the details of such households who \nclaimed to be left out from the SECC 2011 based PWL  and eligible \nfor inclusion in PWL, have been captured in the Awaas+ Survey, \n2018. The Survey was conducted during January, 2018 to 7 th March, \n2019. In this exercise, the States/UTs have uploaded details of \nadditional households. In order to fill the gap of 91 lakh houses (2.95 \ncrore -2.04 crore), Awaas+ data is being utilized. Out of this, a target \nof 91 lakh has been allocated to the States/UTs till date.   \n  \n\n(e) & (f): Monitoring of PMAY-G is done through r eal time capture of \nprogress using workflow enabled transactional data in MIS i.e. \nAwaasSoft. For process monitoring, inspection is done by central \nteams [Area Officers and National Level Monitors (NLM)], monitoring \nis also done by District Development Coo rdination and Monitoring \n(DISHA) Committee headed by Member of Parliament, Social Audit, \netc. \nThe National Level Monitoring system of Ministry of Rural \nDevelopment is a third party monitoring and reporting mechanism \nworking towards regular assessment of im plementation of Rural \nDevelopment Programmes/Schemes including PMAY -G in the \ncountry. \n***** \n  \n\nAnnexure I \n   \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PART (b) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 769 TO BE ANSWERED ON 25.07.2023 REGARDING \nBENEFICIARIES UNDER PMAY-G   \n  \nThe de tail of number of houses allocated to States/UTs and \ncompleted under PMAY-G (as on 19.7.2023) \n  \n  \nSl.No. Name of the State/UT Target allocated Houses \ncompleted \n1 ARUNACHAL \nPRADESH \n36,241 \n18,641 \n2 ASSAM 19,10,997 15,37,493 \n3 BIHAR 37,03,355 36,12,016 \n4 CHHATTISGARH 11,76,146 8,65,738 \n5 GOA 257 180 \n6 GUJARAT 6,07,515 4,20,725 \n7 HARYANA 29,441 26,246 \n8 HIMACHAL PRADESH 15,457 14,265 \n9 JAMMU AND \nKASHMIR \n3,42,575 \n1,66,203 \n10 JHARKHAND 15,92,553 15,33,154 \n11 KERALA 35,189 30,591 \n12 MADHYA PRADESH 38,02,248 35,53,934 \n13 MAHARASHTRA 14,"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |77", "t": "Toll Plazas", "head": " \n \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 77 \nANSWERED ON 20TH JULY, 2023 \nTOLL PLAZAS \n77. SHRI SUDHEER GUPTA: \nSHRI SANJAY SADASHIVRAO MANDLIK: \nSHRI SHRIRANG APPA BARNE: \nSHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV: \nSHRI DHAIRYASHEEL SAMBHAJIRAO MANE: \nSHRI BIDYUT BARAN MAHATO:  \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether the daily toll collection through  FASTag system is achieving \nhistoric milestones in the country; \n(b) if so, the details of the collections made  during each of the last three \nmonths; \n(c) the total number of toll plazas including State  toll plazas installed on \nvarious National Highways in the country during the last one year and the \nnumber and location of those toll plazas that are likely to be installed; \n(d) whether National Highways Authority of India (NHAI) is working towards \nfinalizing the necessary  requirements for the implementation of Global  \nNavigation Satellite System (GNSS) based tolling  system on the National \nHighways and Expressways; \n(e) if so, the details thereof and salient features of the said system; and \n(f) the time by which it is likely to come into force? \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n(a) to (b) User fee collection at fee plazas on National Highways has \nimproved considerably upon  implementation of FASTag  Programme in the \ncountry.  The FASTag Collection on fee plazas of National Highways on \n29.04.2023 was Rs 162.10 Crore.  \nUser fee  collection through Fastag in past three months for plazas \nentrusted to National Highways Authority of India is as under: \nMonth FASTag Collection \n(in Crore Rupees) \nApril, 2023 4,314 \nMay, 2023 4,554 \nJune, 2023 4,349 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nThere is increasing trend in average monthly fee collection through \nFASTags. \n(c) Total number of fee plazas started in F inancial Year 2022-23 is 112 \nunder National Highways Authority of India. The details of fee plazas is at \nAnnexure A. \nFee plazas are constructed during the execution of project and put to \ncommercial use after issuance of Provision Completion Certificate. \nAverage Monthly Fee Collection \nthrough FASTags for the Quarter \nApril to June, 2023 \nRs. 4,406 Cr. \nAverage Monthly Fee Collection \nthrough FASTags for previous \nQuarter (January-March, 2023) \nRs. 4,083 Cr. \nAverage Monthly Fee Collection \nthrough FASTags for Previous \nYears 2022-23 \nRs. 3,841 Cr. \n\n \n \n \n \n \n(d) to (f ) Government has appointed a consultant to provide advisory \nservices on implementation of new technologies  like Global Navigation \nSatellite System (GNSS) based barrier less free flow tolling. \nGlobal Navigation Satellite System (GNSS) has the capacity to retrieve the \npositioning of the vehicle on the National Highway and calculates the user \nfee based upon the distance travelled.  \n\n \n \n \n \n \nANNEXURE A \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (c ) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 77 FOR ANSWER ON 20.07.2023 ASKED BY \nSHRI SUDHEER GUPTA, SHRI SANJAY SADASHIVRAO MANDLIK , SHRI \nSHRIRANG APPA BARNE , SHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV , SHRI \nDHAIRYASHEEL SAMBHAJIRAO MANE , SHRI BIDYUT BARAN MAHATO  \nREGARDING TOLL PLAZAS \n------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ \nDetails of fee plazas that are likely to be installed at National Highways , \nstate-wise: \n \nNew fee Plazas added in Financial Year 2022-23 \nS.No Regional \noffice \nSection and Tollable reach Location and \nname of the \nplaza \nplaza \nadded \n1 Ro, \nLucknow \n(west UP) \nPuranpur-Khutar Section \nKm 38.00 - Km 82.00 \nAt Km 69.640 \nHitouta in \nShahjahanpur \ndist. on dated \n19.04.2022 \n1 \n2 Ro, \nLucknow \n(West UP) \nPilibhit - Puranpur \nKM 0.000 - Km 38.000 \nAt Km 9.590 \nSarai Sundar Pur \non dated \n20.04.2022 \n1 \n3 RO, Odisha Rimuli - Koida section \nKm 163.000 - Km 206.200 \nAt Km 176.060 \nKandra on dated \n03.04.2022 \n1 \n \n4 \n \nRO, Jaipur \nSanchore - Dhanera - Deesa \nSection Km 0.00 to Km 33.600 \n(existing km 0.00 to km 33.600) \nAt Km 8.400 \nChoti Villor \n21.04.2022 \n \n1 \n5 RO, Jaipur Raisinghnagar to Poogal \nSection from design kilometer \n101.000 to kilometer 263.460  \nat km 102.500 \nThakri (Raisingh \nnagar) 1.12.2021 \n1 \n6 RO, Jaipur At km 163.000 \nPatroda \n01.12.2021 \n1 \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n7 \n \nRO, \nVijaywada \nRapthadu-Bathalapalli section \nKm 76.000 - Km 99.971 (4LPS)  \nand Km 99.971 - Km 134.000  \n(2LPS) \nBathalapalli Km \n104.405 \n16.07.2021 \n \n1 \n8 RO, \nHyderabad \nNakrekal-Tanamcherla Kothagudem Km \n42.950 \n03.01.2022 \n1 \n \n9 \n \nRO, Bhopal \nPachore-Shujalpur  \nKm 2.700 - Km 33.000 \n(existing 2.900 - Km 33.200) \nTalen (Mirzapur) \nKm 14.600 \n30.05.2022 \n \n1 \n10 RO UP East Indo Nepal Border - Rudahuali \nKm 0.000 - Km 65.870 \nBelgada at Km \n53.630 \n01.04.2022 \n1 \n11 RO \nBangalore \nBellary Byrapura section (HAM) \nKm 253.600 - km 308.550 \nJajirakallu at Km \n281.430 \n04.05.2022 \n1 \n12 RO-Bhopal \nPIU jabalpur"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |770", "t": "Development of Border Villages", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 770 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 25TH JULY, 2023/ SRAVANA 3, 1945 (SAKA) \n \nDEVELOPMENT OF BORDER VILLAGES \n \n770.   SHRI SANGAM LAL GUPTA: \nSHRI ANURAG SHARMA: \nDR. RAMAPATI RAM TRIPATHI: \nSHRI P.P. CHAUDHARY: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of the existing schemes launched by the Government for the \ndevelopment of border villages in States of Uttar Pradesh and Rajasthan; \n \n(b) whether the Government is planning to include such border villages of \nUttar Pradesh and Rajasthan in the Vibrant Village Programme (VVP) or \nintroduce another scheme for providing similar facilities to the border \nvillages in Uttar Pradesh and Rajasthan; and \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI NISITH PRAMANIK) \n \n(a): In all the census villages/towns, semi urban and urban areas located \nwithin 0-10 kms. distance (aerial distance) from the first habitation at  \nInternational         Boundary    (IB)     in      16  States  &  2 UTs     abutting  \n\n-2- \nL.S.US.Q.N.O. 770 FOR 25.07.2023 \n \nland borders (including those in the States of Rajasthan and Uttar Pradesh) \nworks/projects for identified gaps in essential infrastructure pertaining to \nroad & bridges; health; education; agriculture; sports; drinking water & \nsanitation; Anganwadi; community centre; small scale industries etc. \nhave been approved under the Border Area Development Programme \n(BADP).   \n(b) to (c): Government has approved Vibrant Villages Programme (VVP) on \n15th February 2023 for comprehensive development of select villages in 46 \nblocks of 19 districts abutting northern border in the States of Arunachal \nPradesh, Sikkim, Uttarakhand, Himachal Pradesh and UT of Ladakh. \n***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |771", "t": "Tribal and Rural Local Sports", "head": "Page 1 of 2 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †771 \nANSWERED ON 25.07.2023 \n \nTribal and Rural Local Sports \n \n†771. SHRI OMPRAKASH BHUPALSINH ALIAS PAWAN RAJENIMBALKAR: \nSHRI VINAYAK RAUT: \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has formulated any policy in collaboration with \nthe State Government in order to promote tribal and rural local sports; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) the details of the policies implemented and proposed  to be implemented \nin the country particularly in the State of Maharashtra; \n \n(d) whether the Government has chalked out any plan to organize sports \ncompetitions of these sports at a large level in the country during the last \nthree years; \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof, particularly for the State of Maharashtra; and \n \n(f) the steps taken by the Government to secure the future and provide \nemployment to the players participating in these sports regularly? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n{ SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR } \n \n(a) to (c) ‘Sports’ being a State subject, the responsibility for development  \nof sports and promoting tribal and rural local sports primarily rests with the  \nState/Union Territory Governments. Central Government supplements their  \nefforts. However, this Ministry runs a Central Sector Scheme, namely,  \n‘Khelo India – National Programme for Development of Sports’ Scheme since \n2016, wherein one of the sub -components, namely, ‘Promotion of rural and  \nindigenous/tribal games’, is dedicated to development of rural and  \nindigenous/tribal games in the country, including in the State of  \n\nPage 2 of 2 \n \nMaharashtra. \n \n(d) and (e) Under the ‘Promotion of rural and indigenous/tribal games’ sub - \ncomponent of the Khelo India Scheme, Mallakhamb, Kalaripayyattu, Gatka,  \nThang-Ta, Silambam and Yogasan have been receiving support for \ninfrastructure development, equipment support, appointment of coaches, \ntraining of coaches and scholarships to athletes. \n \n(f) Under the ‘Khelo India Centres’ component of the Khelo India Scheme, as \na part of Government’s vision to strengthen the sports ecosystem in the \ncountry at the grassroots level, a low -cost, effective sports training \nmechanism has been developed. State/ UT Governments can engage former \nsportspersons as coaches and mentors for youngsters. Further, Sports \nAuthority of India (SAI), an autonomous body under the Ministry of Youth \nAffairs and Sports, provides opportunity to former sportspersons to be \nemployed as coaches in various sports disciplines. \n \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |772", "t": "Buses for Disabled Persons", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF EMPOWERMENT OF PERSONS WITH DISABILITIES \n  \nLOK SABHA  \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 772 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.07.2023 \n  \nBuses for Disabled Persons \n  \n772.     DR. SHASHI THAROOR: \nWill the Minister of SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT be pleased to state \n(a) the measures taken by the Government to ensure the implementation of disability rights and \naccessibility for persons with disabilities across various sectors under the Accessible India \nCampaign; \n(b) whether the Government is aware that just 6% of 1.4 lakh buses are fully accessible for disabled \npersons, despite the initial targets set under the Accessible India Campaign of 25 percent by June \n2022, if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; and; \n(c) the reasons for the extension of the target till March 2024? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT \n(SUSHRI PRATIMA BHOUMIK) \n \n (a) Sir, to ensure the implementation of disability rights and accessibility for persons with \ndisabilities across various sectors under the targets of the Accessible India Campaign, the measures \ntaken and achievement made so far by the Government is attached at Annexure-I. \n(b) Ministry of Road Transport and Highways has issued the Accessibility Guidelines for Bus \nterminals and Bus Stops on 29.08.2022 which covers the planning and design aspects of areas in bus \nports/bus terminals and bus stops, so that these areas can be made accessible to all. Special emphasis \nhas been laid on ensuring ease of access for the use of these facilities by end users. The aim is to \nensure seamless inclusion of Divyangjan, irrespective of their disability in the context of \naccessibility to any such infrastructure. Following is the status of Government owned buses for the \nmonth of May 2023: \n  \nAccessibility of Buses \n  \nNos. of Buses held by \nSRTUs \nTotal Nos. of \nbuses \nNos. of \naccessible/Fully \naccessible buses \n% age  of \naccessibility \nIntercity Operations 101841  27133 27% \nUrban Operations 44361  24022 54% \nTotal 146202  51155 34.99% \n \n(c) As the Ministry is implementing Creation of Barrier Free Environment sub-scheme under the \nScheme for Implementation of Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, 2016 (SIPDA) with similar \nobjective therefore the sunset date for the Accessible India Campaign (AIC) has been set for March \n2024 after which AIC will be merged with the Creation of Barrier Free Environment component \nunder SIPDA.  \n\n   \nAnnexure-I \nAnnexure referred to in part (a) of the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 772 raised by DR.  \nSHASHI THAROOR for answer on 25.07.2023 regarding “Buses for Disabled Persons” \n  \nAchievements made under the targets of the Accessible India Campaign \n  \nSl \nNo \nTarget Status \n1 Target of enhancing the \nproportion of Accessible \nGovernment Buildings: \nTarget 1.1 : Completing \naccessibility audit of at least \n25-50 most important \ngovernment buildings in 50 \ncities and making them fully \naccessible; \nTarget 1.2 :  Making 50% of \nall the government buildings of \nthe National Capital and all the \nState Capitals fully accessible; \nTarget 1.3: Completing \naccessibility audit of 50% of \ngovernment buildings and \nmaking them fully accessible in \n10 most important cities/towns \nof states not covered in targets \n1.1 and 1.2;  \n  \nFor Target (1.1) – State Government buildings \n In the State Governments / UTs, auditors \ncompleted Access Audit of 1671 buildings in 48 \ncities. 1671 access audit reports have been \nsubmitted to the State’s/UTs Nodal Officers. \nFurther, financial proposals for retrofitting of \n1484 buildings have been received so far. \nSanction has been issued by the Department in \nrespect 1314 buildings and 20 States/UTs have \nreported to have completed retrofitting work in \n611 buildings.  \n For Target 1.2 and 1.3 \n8 States/UTs have informed that 2851 State \nGovernment buildings have been selected to be made \naccessible under Targets/Phases (1.2) and (1.3) from \ntheir own funds and reported work completion in 354 \nbuildings. \n         For Central Government building under the \ntargets of built up environment: \n In the Central Government, CPWD \nreported completion of retrofitting work in 1100 \nCentral Government buildings targeted under \nAccessible India Campaign.  \n2 Target of enhancing the \nproportion of Accessible \nAirports \n  \nTarget 2.1 and 2.2 - \nAirports: All international \nairports and domestic airports \nto be made fully accessible; \n All 35 international airports & 55 out of 69 \ndomestic airports provided with accessibility \nfeatures (ramps, accessible toilets, helpdesks \nand lifts with Braille and auditory information \nsystems), Further, all international/customs \nairport are reported to have been provided with \naerobridges. \n\n3 Target of enhancing the \nproportion of Accessible \nRailway Stations \n  \nTarget 3.1 and 3.2 - \nRailways: A1, A & B \ncategories of railway stations to \nbe made fully accessible; 50% \nof all railway st"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |773", "t": "Khelo India Women League", "head": "Page 1 of 2 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †773 \nANSWERED ON 25.07.2023 \n \nKhelo India Women League \n  \n†773. SHRI ANIL FIROJIYA: \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) the exent to which the maximum limit of standards of food and housing \nhas been increased for athletes and team officials; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c) the details of works being undertaken to promote sports on the lines of \npromoting the success of Khelo India Women League? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS  \n{ SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR } \n \n(a) and (b) Details of the  old and the revised norms of financial assistance \nprovided by the Ministry  to athletes and support s staff during training and \ncompetitions are given at Annexure – I. \n \n(c) The ‘Sports for Women’ sub-component under the  Khelo India scheme \nendeavours to provide additional avenues for development of sports, \ncompetitions, leagues, and tournaments specifically for wom en and offer \nopportunities to women for participating and excelling in sporting activities. \nIn this regard , the government has started Khelo India Women’s League in \n14 sports disciplines. Total 248 competitions with 23,267 participants in 55 \ncities across 26 States/ UTs have been organised. \n\nPage 2 of 2 \n \nANNEXURE – I \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART S (a) AND (b) OF LOK SA BHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †773 TO BE ANSWERED O N 25.07 .2023 \nREGARDING “ KHELO INDIA WOMEN LEAGUE ” ASKED BY SHRI ANIL \nFIROJIYA, HON’BLE MEMBER OF LOK SABHA \n \nBoarding and Lodging charges for Athletes and Supports Staffs per \nperson per day: \n \n \n**** \nCategory Particulars Old \nNorms \nRevised \nNorms \nRemarks \nDomestic A) Boarding Charges    \ni) X & Y Category City INR 1500 INR 1500   \nii) Other cities INR 1200 INR 1200   \nB) Diet Charges       \ni) Senior  INR 650 INR 690   \nii) Junior INR 450 INR 480   \nC) Additional Diet \nCharges \n      \ni) Heavy & Middle \nWeight \nINR 700 INR 750   \nii) Endurance, team, \nSprint & low weight \nINR 400 INR 430   \niii) Skill Event INR 300 INR 320   \nInternational Boarding &  Lodging \nCharges \n  \n  \n \n \n \n \nUSD 150 \n  \nUSD 235 When NSF is making its own \narrangements \nUSD 235 When Organizers making \narrangement & Hospitality \npackage separately mentions \nlocal transport, entry fees etc. \nUSD 250 Where Hospitality Package \nincludes combined services for \nboarding & lodging, local \ntransport, entry fee, misc., etc. "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |774", "t": "Allocations under NPDD", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nDEPARTMENT OF ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.774 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25TH JULY, 2023 \n \nALLOCATIONS UNDER NPDD \n \n774: SHRI POCHA BRAHMANANDA REDDY: \n  \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nमत्स्यपाऱन, पशुपाऱन और डेयरी मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) the main features of National Programme for Dairy Development (NPDD); \n(b) the details of funds sanctioned, allocated and utilised under this programme during last three \nyears, State wise; \n(c) whether it is true that over 100 million farmers still rely on dairy farming for a portion of their \ndaily income; and \n(d) if so, the details of farmers trained under the NPDD, State-wise? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER FOR FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n(SHRI PARSHOTTAM RUPALA) \n \n(a) Department of Animal Husbandry & Dairying (DAHD) is implementing “National Programme \nfor Dairy Development (NPDD)”scheme across the country since Feb-2014. The scheme has been \nrestructured/ realigned in July 2021 for implementation from 2021 -22 to 2025 -26with \nthefollowing two components: \n(i) The Component ''A'' of NPDD focuses on creating/strengthening of infrastructure for \nquality milk testing equipment as well as primary chilling faci lities for State Cooperative Dairy \nFederations/ District Cooperative Milk Producers‟ Union/SHGs/Milk Producer Companies/Farmer \nProducer Organizations. \n(ii) The Component „B‟ of the NPDD scheme \"Dairying through Cooperatives” aims to \nincrease sale of milk a nd dairy products by increasing farmer‟s access to organized market, \nupgrading dairy processing facilities and marketing infrastructure and enhancing the capacity of \nproducer owned institutions. \n\n(b) NPDD is demand driven scheme and no state wise allocations were made  during the last three \nyears. Details of State-wiseapproved project outlays, funds releasedduring last three yearsand their \ncorresponding utilization is given at Annexure-I. \n(c) As per 20th Livestock Census (2019), there are 80.83 million farmers household engaged in \ndairy farming activities (having either cattle or buffalo) and dairy farming contributes for a portion \nof their daily income. \n(d) Under Component A of NPDD scheme, training is provided to farmers on good hygienic \npractices/good manufacturing practices  etc.Under Component B of NPDD scheme, trainings are \nprovided to farmers on clean milk production and good hygienic practices, milch animals rearing, \nadoption of cattle feed, gre en fodder and mineral mixture, etc. The details of State -wise numbers \nof farmers approved for undergoing such training is given at Annexure-II. \n \n**** \n \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \nState-wisefund releases, utilization and unspent under “National Programme for Dairy \nDevelopment” (NPDD) scheme during last three year \n(Rs in crore) \nS. No.  State/UTs \nApproved Outlay@ Funds released  \n(Last three \nyears) \nFunds \nUtilized  Total Central \nShare \nComponent A#  \n1 Andhra Pradesh 235.05 162.25 40.07 6.72 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 11.91 11.26 0.00 0.00 \n3 Assam 34.36 32.65 0.00 0.00 \n4 Bihar 263.23 210.19 102.41 82.72 \n5 Chhattisgarh 23.39 20.96 2.51 2.27 \n6 Goa 16.90 13.93 0.40 0.00 \n7 Gujarat 327.77 201.27 141.40 34.04 \n8 Haryana 25.24 21.33 5.03 0.87 \n9 Himachal Pradesh 46.43 42.67 21.39 13.04 \n10 Jammu & Kashmir 151.12 139.81 83.68 51.05 \n11 Jharkhand 20.94 17.66 4.11 1.08 \n12 Karnataka 374.38 261.19 125.36 72.30 \n13 Kerala 170.64 125.97 33.23 21.21 \n14 Madhya Pradesh 63.61 54.74 10.13 3.53 \n15 Maharashtra 49.47 45.07 23.51 14.20 \n16 Manipur 30.29 27.85 14.17 5.11 \n17 Meghalaya 63.94 57.80 30.97 26.25 \n18 Mizoram 11.01 10.31 0.20 0.20 \n19 Nagaland 13.06 12.15 4.11 2.17 \n20 Odisha 62.60 55.33 11.77 3.88 \n21 Puducherry 3.42 3.25 0.39 0.39 \n22 Punjab 251.21 167.19 64.37 35.67 \n23 Rajasthan 237.80 181.27 57.59 43.91 \n24 Sikkim 53.72 49.62 21.67 21.57 \n25 Tamil Nadu 236.80 167.88 70.83 66.92 \n26 Telangana 55.24 48.85 18.50 9.20 \n27 Tripura 22.92 20.26 0.79 0.75 \n28 Uttar Pradesh 79.85 66.49 0.00 0.00 \n29 Uttarakhand 75.04 64.12 9.32 1.48 \n30 West Bengal 4.03 3.93 0.71 0.71 \n  Total 3015.35 2297.25 898.65 521.25 \n# Under Component A, funds were released to State Implementing Agency of the scheme \n@ Approved outlay is cumulative of projects approved under NPDD scheme from 2014 -15 to \n2022-23  \n \nContd../… \n\n \n(Rs in crore) \nODA loan- Official Development Assistance Loan from JICA; PI-Participating Institution \n*Under Component B, an amount of Rs.9.31 crore funds were released to NDDB for projects \napproved in Bihar, Uttar Pradesh and Telangana during last three years against which utilisation of \nRs.6.35 crore has been reported. \n \n$ Approved outlay is cumulative of projects approved from 2022-23 to 2023-24 (Till 19.07.2023) \n \n \n  \nComponent-B* \nS. No. State \nApproved Outlays$ Loan \nreleased  \nGrant \nreleased  Total ODA Loan Grant   PI’s \nContribution \n1 Andhra Pradesh 129.00 63.82 62.94 2.24 - - \n2 Bihar 58.05 19.44 3"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |775", "t": "Increase in Kharif MSP", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 775 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 25TH JULY, 2023 \n \n     INCREASE IN KHARIF MSP \n \n775.     SHRI RAVNEET SINGH BITTU:  \n            SHRI THIRUNAVUKKARASAR SU: \n \n          Will the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE क ृ िष एवं िकसान कʞ ाण \n          मंũी be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Minimum Support Price (MSP) of the Kharif crops have been increased \nrecently by the Government for the year 2023-24;  \n(b) if so, the details of the increase in MSP, crop-wise;  \n(c) whether the Government has estimated any loss in the Kharif crops due to \nunseasonal rains etc. during the current year and if so, the details thereof;  \n(d) whether the Government has made any provisions for compensation and additional \nbenefits to farmers to offset any losses in Kharif crops;  \n(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and  \n(f) whether the Government has conducted any study for assessing the impact of MSP \nand the benefits likely to be available to the farmers as a result thereof, if so, the \ndetails thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nक ृ िष एवं िकसान कʞ ाण मंũी      (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) & (b):   Yes, Sir. Details of Minimum Support Price (MSP) fixed by the Government for \nKharif Crops for Marketing Season 2022-23 and 2023-24 are given in the Annexure.  \n(c) to (e): The primary responsibility for disaster management rests with the State \nGovernments.  The State Governments provide financial relief to the affected people in the \nwake of notified disasters from the State Disaster Response Fund (SDRF). However, in the \nevent of disaster of a severe nature, additional financial assistance is extended from the \n\nNational Disaster Response Fund (NDRF), as per the laid down procedure, which includes \nan assessment based on the visit of an Inter-Ministerial Central Team (IMCT). \n Department of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare (DA&FW) coordinates relief \nmeasures for drought, hailstorm, pest attack and cold wave/frost upon receipt of \nMemorandum from the State for additional assistance from NDRF. During the current year, \nno memorandum has been received so far, for financial assistance from NDRF from any \nState Government in the wake of notified calamities coordinated by DA&FW. \n(f): The NITI Aayog had released a study entitled “Efficacy of Minimum Support Prices on \nfarmers” in 2016, covering 14 States, 36 Districts, 72 Blocks, 144 Villages and 1440 \nHouseholds. The study has found, among other things, that MSP declared by the \nGovernment has encouraged 78% of the farmers covered under the study to adopt \nimproved methods of farming such as high yielding varieties of seeds, organic manure, \nchemical fertilizer, pesticides and improved methods of harvesting etc. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure  \n \nAnnex referred to in reply to part (a) & (b) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question \nNo.775 due for reply on 25.07.2023 \n \nMinimum Support Price (MSP) of Kharif Crops \n(in ₹ per quintal) \nSl. No. Commodity Variety MSP \n \n2022-23 2023-24 \n1 Paddy Common 2040 2183 \n  Grade A 2060 2203 \n2. Jowar Hybrid 2970 3180 \n  Maldandi 2990 3225 \n3. Bajra  2350 2500 \n4. Ragi  3578 3846 \n5. Maize  1962 2090 \n6. Tur (Arhar)  6600 7000 \n7. Moong  7755 8558 \n8. Urad  6600 6950 \n9. Cotton Medium Staple 6080 6620 \n  Long Staple 6380 7020 \n10. Groundnut  5850 6377 \n11. Sunflower Seed  6400 6760 \n12. Soyabean  4300 4600 \n13. Sesamum  7830 8635 \n14. Nigerseed  7287 7734 \n \n******** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |776", "t": "Awareness on use of Drones in Farming", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 776 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 25TH JULY 2023 \n \nAWARENESS ON USE OF DRONES IN FARMING \n      \n776. DR. SANJEEV KUMAR SINGARI: \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE क ृ िष एवं िकसान \nकʞ ाण मंũी be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of efforts made by the Government to create awareness on use \nof drones to ease farming;  \n(b) whether the Government has started institutions for creating drone \nawareness and drone demonstration to farmers; and  \n(c) if so, the details of such institutions and if not, the reasons therefor, State-\nwise? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE क ृ िष एवं िकसान \nकʞ ाण मंũी   (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n \n(a) to (c): To create awareness on use of Kisan Drones, the Department of \nAgriculture & Farmers Welfare under Sub-Mission on Agricultural \nMechanization (SMAM)  is providing financial assistance @ 100% of the cost of \ndrone up to a maximum of Rs. 10 lakhs per drone for  its purchase and  \ndemonstration on the farmers’ fields by the institutes under Indian Council of \nAgricultural Research, Farm Machinery Training & Testing Institutes, Krishi \nVigyan Kendras (KVKs), State Agriculture Universities (SAUs), State and other \nCentral Government Agricultural Institutions/Departments and Public Sector \nUndertakings (PSUs) of Government of India engaged in agricultural activities. \nThe Farmers Producers Organizations (FPOs) are provided grants up to 75% \nof the cost of Kisan Drone for its demonstrations on the farmers’ fields. A \ncontingency expenditure of Rs.6000 per hectare is provided to these \nimplementing agencies that do not want to purchase drones but will hire drones \nfor demonstrations from Custom Hiring Centres (CHCs), Hi-tech Hubs, Drone \nManufacturers and Start-Ups. The contingent expenditure to implementing \nagencies that purchase drones for drone demonstrations is limited to Rs.3000 \nper hectare. In order to make available drone services to farmers on rental basis, \nfinancial assistance @ 40% up to a maximum of Rs. 4.00 lakhs are provided for \npurchase of drones by CHCs under Cooperative Society of Farmers, FPOs and \nRural entrepreneurs. Agriculture graduates establishing CHCs are eligible to \n\nreceive financial assistance @ 50% of the cost of drone up to a maximum of \nRs.5.00 lakhs per drone. For purchase of drones on individual ownership basis, \nthe Small and Marginal, Scheduled Caste/Scheduled Tribe, Women and North \nEastern State farmers are provided financial assistance @ 50% of the cost up \nto a maximum of Rs. 5.00 lakhs and other farmers @ 40% up to a maximum of \nRs. 4.00 lakhs. \n \n  Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs) for pesticide and nutrient \napplication and crop specific SOPs for pesticide application have been released, \nwhich provide concise instructions for effective and safe operations of drones.   \n \n  So far, funds amounting to Rs. 134.55 crores have been released \ntowards Kisan Drone promotion which includes Rs. 52.50 crores released to the \nICAR for purchase of 300 Kisan Drones and organizing their demonstrations on \nthe farmers’ fields in 75000 hectares through 100 KVKs, 75 ICAR institutions \nand 25 SAUs. The funds have been provided to the State Governments for \nsupply of 347 Kisan Drone to farmers on subsidy and establishment of 1573 \nKisan Drone CHCs to provide drone services to the farmers. The State-wise list \nof identified KVKs/SAUs and ICAR institutes for demonstration of Kisan Drones \nis enclosed as Annexure-I.  \n \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \n \nState-wise list of identified KVKs/SAUs and ICAR institutes for \ndemonstration of Kisan Drones \n \nList of KVKs \nS. \nNo. \nKVK State/ UT \n1 Krishi Vigyan Kendra, Sugarcane Research \nCentre, Bajpur Road, Kashipur, \nDistt. Udham Singh Nagar-244713 \nUttarakhand \n2 Krishi Vigyan Kendra, Dhanauri \nDistt. Haridwar-249404 \nUttarakhand \n3 Krishi Vigyan Kendra, Sundernagar, \nDistt. Mandi-174 402 \nHimachal Pradesh \n4 Krishi Vigyan Kendra, PO. & Teh. \nKandaghat, Distt. Solan-173215 \nHimachal Pradesh \n5 Krishi Vigyan Kendra, Pombay, Distt. \nKulgam \nJammu & Kashmir \n6 Krishi Vigyan Kendra, R.S. Pura, \nDistt. Jammu-181102 \nJammu & Kashmir \n7 Krishi Vigyan Kendra, Samrala, \nDistt. Ludhiana-141 114 \nPunjab \n8 Krishi Vigyan Kendra, VPO. Budh Singh \nWala, Distt. Moga-142 001 \nPunjab \n9 Krishi Vigyan Kendra, Kheri, \nDistt. Sangrur-148 001 \nPunjab \n10 Krishi Vigyan Kendra, Handitya, \nBarnala District \nPunjab \n11 Krishi Vigyan Kendra, Village Tepla, PO. \nSaha, Distt. Ambala-133 104 \nHaryana  \n12 Krishi Vigyan Kendra, \nDistt. Mahendergarh-123029 \nHaryana  \n13 Krishi Vigyan Kendra, Jagdishpur, \nRathdhana, Narela Road, PB No. 28, \nDistt. SonipAt.131 001 \nHaryana  \n14 Krishi Vigyan Kendra, IARI, Shikohpur, \nDistt. Gurgaon-122 001 \nHaryana  \n15 Krishi Vigyan Kendra, Ujwa, \nNew Delhi-110 073 \nDelhi \n16 Krishi Vigyan Kendra, P.Box No.56 \nDistt. Bhilwara-311001 \nRajasth"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |777", "t": "Horticulture College in  Uttar Pradesh", "head": " \nOIH \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURAL RESEARCH & EDUCATION \n \n \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 777 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25th July, 2023 \n \n \n \nHORTICULTURE COLLEGE IN UTTAR PRADESH \n \n \n777.  SHRI ARUN KUMAR SAGAR: \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE               \nd`f\"k vkSj fdlku dY;k.k ea=h  be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has taken or proposes  to take any steps to set up a \nHorticulture College in Shahjahanpur Parliamentary Constituency in Uttar Pradesh; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) the details of the progress made so far in this regard? \n \n \nANSWER \n \n \n THE MINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE      \nd`f\"k vkSj fdlku dY;k.k ea=h        (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n \n(a) to (c):  No, sir. Agriculture including agricultural education being a state subject, \nsetting up a Horticulture College/Horticultural University comes under the purview of \nState Government.   \n Indian Council of Agricultural Rese arch (ICAR) is involved in strengthening and \ndevelopment of higher agricultural education through curriculum development and \naccreditation of courses/colleges/universities across the country. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |778", "t": "Anti-Trafficking Cases", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 778 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 25TH JULY, 2023/ SRAVANA 3, 1945 (SAKA) \n \nANTI-TRAFFICKING CASES \n \n778.   SHRI RAJENDRA DHEDYA GAVIT: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has developed any Portal to track anti-\ntrafficking cases as proposed under the Anti-Human Trafficking Unit \n(AHTU) Scheme; \n \n(b) if so, the details of the said portal and if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n(c) the details and the number of trafficking cases recorded and resolved \nthrough this portal; and \n \n(d) the activities undertaken to counter human trafficking in tribal areas? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI AJAY KUMAR MISHRA) \n \n(a)to (c): The Ministry of Home Affairs has launched a national level \ncommunication platform - Crime Multi Agency Centre (Cri-MAC) in the year \n2020. Cri-MAC facilitates dissemination of information about significant \ncrimes, including human trafficking cases, across the country on real time  \n \n \n\n \n-2- \nL.S.US.Q.N.O. 778 FOR 25.07.2023 \n \nbasis.  Cri-MAC has been launched for generating alerts on significant \nincidents of crime and for communication among police units across the \ncountry. The coordination mechanism available to Police Officers across \nthe States and Union Territories through Cri-MAC is an efficient and fast \nenabling-tool for sharing information about any crime in a safe and secure \nmanner, which helps in locating and identifying the victims and also helps \nin prevention, detection and investigation of crimes.  \nThe National Crime Records Bureau compiles crime statistics reported to \nit by the States and Union Territories and publishes the same in its annual \npublication ‘Crime in India’. The latest published report is of the year 2021. \nA total of 2189 cases of human trafficking were reported and 1645 charge \nsheeted during the year 2021.  \n(d): ‘Police’ and ‘Public Order’ are “State-List” subjects under the Seventh \nSchedule to the Constitution of India. Therefore, the responsibility for \npreventing and countering the crime of human trafficking primarily vests \nwith respective State Governments/Union Territories. However, the \nMinistry of Home Affairs has also been supplementing the efforts of the \nStates/UTs in this regard by providing them guidelines on preventing and  \n\n \n-3- \nL.S.US.Q.N.O. 778 FOR 25.07.2023 \n \ncountering human trafficking, etc. in the form of various Advisories issued \nfrom time to time. These advisories are available at www.mha.gov.in. The \nMinistry of Home Affairs has advised the States and Union Territories that \nappropriate ‘intelligence’ and ‘surveillance’ mechanisms may be \nestablished by them for identifying the persons/gangs involved in human \ntrafficking cases and for gathering information about their affiliations, \nmodus operandi, etc. States/Union Territories have also been advised to \ntake suitable measures for generating awareness about incidents of \nhuman trafficking at all levels and for organizing community awareness \nprogrammes and to engage with representatives of local panchayats to \neffectively counter the human trafficking incidents.  \nThe Ministry has also provided financial assistance to all States/Union \nTerritories for establishing Anti Human Trafficking Units covering all \ndistricts of the country. The Ministry of Home Affairs also provides \nfinancial assistance to the States/Union Territories to hold ‘Judicial \nColloquiums’ and ‘State level conferences’ for sensitizing law \nofficers/Police Officers, etc. on issues relating to trafficking. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |779", "t": "MSP of Kharif Crops", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 779 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 25TH JULY, 2023 \n \n                MSP OF KHARIF CROPS \n \n \n779. SHRI MANICKAM TAGORE B: \n      \n     Will the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE क ृ िष एवं िकसान कʞ ाण मंũी \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether it is a fact that farmers across the country were unhappy with the hiked \nMinimum Support Price (MSP) for Kharif 2023;  \n \n(b) whether it is also true that the new MSPs were not linked to the real expenses of \nfarming;  \n \n(c) whether it is also true that the new-announced MSP for kharif crops is nowhere near \nthe C2+50% formula proposed by the Swaminathan Commission;  \n \n(d) whether it is also true that the cost of production estimates given by the Government \nare faulty and underrated and thus the MSP figures are also very unsatisfactory; and  \n \n(e) if so, whether the Government is considering making a rethink on MSP? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nक ृ िष एवं िकसान कʞ ाण मंũी      (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) to (e): Government has notified Minimum Support Prices (MSP) for 14 mandated Kharif \nCrops for Marketing Season 2023-24, on the basis of the recommendations of the \nCommission for Agricultural Costs and Prices (CACP), views of State Governments and \nCentral Ministries/Departments concerned. \nWhile recommending MSP, CACP considers important factors like overall demand-\nsupply conditions, cost of production, domestic and international prices, inter-crop price \nparity, terms of trade between agricultural and non-agricultural sectors, the likely effect on \n\nthe rest of the economy, besides ensuring rational utilization of land, water and other \nproduction resources and a minimum of 50 percent as the margin over cost of production.  \nThe Cost of production is one of the important determinants of MSP and its \nestimation is based on the methodology recommended by Expert Committees from time to \ntime. CACP projects cost for ensuing marketing season on the basis of actual paid out \ncosts and imputed value of family labour and Composite Input Price Index (CIPI) which is \nbased on latest prices of major inputs like human labour, bullock labour, machine labour, \nfertilisers and manures, seeds, pesticides and irrigation. \nNational Commission on Farmers (NCF), constituted under the Chairmanship of \nProf. M.S. Swaminathan in 2004, had, inter alia, recommended that MSP should be at least \n50 percent more than the weighted average cost of production. To give effect to this \nrecommendation, Government, in its Union Budget for 2018-19, had made an \nannouncement to keep MSP at a level of one and half times of the cost of production as a \npre-determined principle. Accordingly, MSPs for all mandated Kharif, Rabi and other \ncommercial crops have been fixed with a margin of at least 50 per cent over all India \nweighted average cost of production since the year 2018-19 \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |78", "t": "Facilities at Khajuraho Airport", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 78\n(To be answered on the 20th July 2023)\n \nFACILITIES AT KHAJURAHO AIRPORT\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) whether Khajuraho airport has facilities of tourist information and facilitation\ncounters, guides and transport services to and fro tourist spots, so as to help encourage\ntourism ecosystem of the Khajuraho;\n(b) if so, the details thereof; and\n(c) if not, the reasons therefor?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of State in the Ministry of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) to (c) : Yes, Sir. A tourism information counter is located at Khajuraho Airport to\nassist travelers. Guide services are not at the airport but are available at various tourist\nspots. Transportation services are easily accessible through the local Taxi services at\nKhajuraho airport.\n \n \n*****\n78. SHRI VISHNU DATT SHARMA\n be pleased to state:-\n (GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD))"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |780", "t": "Establishment of Cold Storage and Chain", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 780 \nANSWERED ON 25.07.2023 \n  \n‘ESTABLISHMENT OF COLD STORAGE AND CHAIN’ \n  \n 780.  SHRI KANUMURU RAGHU RAMA KRISHNA RAJU: \n  \n         Will the Minister of FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES be pleased to state: \n  \n(a)    whether the Government is planning to establish cold storages and chain at the district level in near \nfuture; \n  \n(b)   if so, the number of districts of Andhra Pradesh where such cold storages and chain would be set up; \nand \n  \n(c)   if not, the reasons therefor and its likely impact on the food processing industry? \n  \n  \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n  \n(a)   The Ministry of Food Processing Industries (MoFPI) does not have any plan to establish cold storages \nand chain at the district level in near future; \n  \n(b) & (c) Under the scheme “Pradhan Mantri Kisan Sampada Yojana\" (PMKSY), MoFPI has approved 72 \nprojects comprising 91 cold storages with a capacity of 85463 MT as part of integrated food processing and \npreservation infrastructure in the State of Andhra Pradesh.   MOFPI neither provides financial support to \nstandalone cold storages nor establishes any infrastructu re facility on its own. Further, the Department of \nAgriculture & Farmers Welfare provides financial assistance through “Mission for Integrated Development \nof Horticulture\" (MIDH) and Scheme for “Capital Investment Subsidy for Construction /Expansion \n/Modernization of Cold Storages and Storages for Horticulture Products”. All these schemes are \nimplemented on demand driven basis.  \n  \n  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |781", "t": "Supervision of Social Media Platforms", "head": " \n  GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.781 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.07.2023 \n \nSUPERVISION OF SOCIAL MEDIA PLATFORMS  \n781.  DR BEESETTI VENKATA SATYAVATHI: \n \nWill the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government proposes to set up organisations to weed out fake \ninformation and fact- check reports; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(c) whether the Government is planning to cooperate with the social media platforms \nand establish a regulatory agency to supervise the operation of social media \nplatforms and make them accountable for the material that is posted on their \nplatforms; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING; AND \nMINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR) \n \n(a) to (d) The Government has statutory and institutional \nmechanisms in place to combat fake news, including the following:   \ni) A Fact Check Unit has been set up under Press Information \nBureau under the Ministry of Information and Broadcasting in \nNovember, 2019 which takes cognizance of fake news relating \nto the Central Government both suo-motu and by way of queries \nsent by citizens on its portal or through e-mail and social media. \nThe Unit responds to the relevant queries with correct and \nupdated information related to the policies, schemes, guidelines, \ninitiatives, etc of the Government of India.  \n\nii) For Print Media, Press Council of India (PCI), a statutory \nautonomous body set up under the Press Council Act, 1978, has \nframed “Norms of Journalistic Conduct” for adherence by the \nmedia. These norms provide for “accuracy and fairness, pre-\npublications verification, distinction between conjecture, \nspeculation, comment and fact avoiding sensational/ provocative \nheadings and justification for the matter printed under them, \netc”. \n      In case of violation of Norms, Press Council of India under \nthe provisions of Section 14 of the Act, after holding an inquiry, \nmay warn, admonish or censure the newspaper, the news \nagency, the editor or the journalist or disapprove the conduct of \nthe editor or the journalists as the case may be. \niii) For electronic media, all TV Channels are required to adhere to \nthe Programme Code under the Cable Television Networks \n(Regulation) Act, 1995, including that programmes broadcast by \nthem should not contain anything obscene, defamatory, \ndeliberate, false and suggestive innuendos and half-truths;  \niv) For digital media, the Government has notified the Information \nTechnology (Intermediary Guidelines and Digital Media Ethics \nCode) Rules, 2021 under the IT Act, 2000 on 25th February, 2021, \nwhich inter alia provide for due diligence to be observed by \nintermediaries, including social media intermediaries, and for a \nCode of Ethics to be adhered to by the publishers of news and \ncurrent affairs on digital media and OTT platforms. These rules \nalso provide for a three tier grievance redressal mechanism to \nlook into complaints related to violation of Code of Ethics. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |782", "t": "Cyber Security Training Institutes", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 782 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 25TH JULY, 2023/ SRAVANA 3, 1945 (SAKA) \n \nCYBER SECURITY TRAINING INSTITUTES \n \n782.   SHRI GNANATHIRAVIAM S.: \nSHRI VIJAYAKUMAR ALIAS VIJAY VASANTH: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to establish any cyber security \ntraining institutes to develop trained cyber crime police officers in the \ncountry to combat fast increasing incidence of cyber crimes from/within \nthe country or instigated from foreign countries; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the Government’s action in this regard; and \n \n(c) the funds allocated to States/UTs for creating infrastructure facilities \nwith advanced gadgets to facilitate cyber crime police? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI AJAY KUMAR MISHRA) \n \n(a) to (c):‘Police’ and ‘Public Order’ are State subjects as per the Seventh \nSchedule to the Constitution of India. It is the primary responsibility of the \nState Governments/UT Administration to equip their police forces with \nadequate infrastructure facilities, state-of-art technology gadgets,  \n\n-2- \nL.S.US.Q.N.O. 782 FOR 25.07.2023 \nmanpower and training for tackling cybercrimes. However, the Central \nGovernment supplements the initiatives of the State Governments through \nvarious advisories and schemes for the capacity building of their Law \nEnforcement Agencies (LEAs). \n Under the Cybercrime Prevention against Women and Children \n(CCPWC) Scheme, financial assistance to the tune of Rs. 122.24 Crore has \nbeen provided to all State/UTs for setting up of cyber forensic cum training \nlaboratories, hiring of junior cyber consultants and capacity building of \nLaw Enforcement Agencies (LEAs), public prosecutors and judicial \nofficers. So far, cyber forensic-cum-training laboratories have been \ncommissioned in 33 States/UTs. \nMassive Open Online Courses (MOOC) platform, namely ‘CyTrain’ \nportal has been developed under the Indian Cyber Crime Coordination \nCentre (I4C), for capacity building of police officers/judicial officers \nthrough online courses on critical aspects of cyber crime investigation, \nforensics, prosecution etc. along with certification. More than 38,300 \nPolice Officers from States/UTs are registered and more than 19,100 \nCertificates issued  \n\n-3- \nL.S.US.Q.N.O. 782 FOR 25.07.2023 \n \nthrough the portal. In addition, training curriculum has been prepared \nfor police personnel, public prosecutors and judicial officers for better \nhandling of investigation and prosecution and States/UTs have been \nrequested to organize training programmes accordingly.36118 LEAs, 2022 \nJudicial Officer and 2240 Public Prosecutors have been trained by Ministry \nof Home Affairs, State Government and BPR&D. \n \n Ministry of Home Affairs has provided central assistance under \n‘Assistance to States & UTs for Modernization of Police’ Scheme to the \nStates & UTs for the acquisition of latest weaponry, training gadgets, \nadvanced communication/forensic equipment, Cyber Policing equipment \netc. The State Governments formulate State Action Plans (SAPs) as per \ntheir strategic priorities and requirements including combating cyber \ncrimes. The amount of Central financial assistance disbursed under this \nscheme during the last 3 financial years is as below: \n \n\n-4- \nL.S.US.Q.N.O. 782 FOR 25.07.2023 \n \n(Rs. in Crore) \nYear Allocation Released \n2020-21 770.76 (Budget Estimate) \n103.27 (Revised Estimate) \n103.25 \n2021-22 620.45 (Budget Estimate) \n188.00 (Revised Estimate) \n158.57 \n2022-23 620.45 (Budget Estimate) \n150.52 (Revised Estimate) \n36.69 \n(Funds against allocations could not be released to most of the States as \nthey had substantial unspent balances.) \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |783", "t": "PM-KISAN Beneficiaries", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 783 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25th July, 2023 \n \n PM-KISAN BENEFICIARIES \n \n783.  SHRI RAJESHBHAI CHUDASAMA:  \nSHRI RAMESH CHAND BIND: \nWill the Minister of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare क ृ िष एवं िकसान कʞ ाण मंũी be pleased \nto state: \n \n(a) the details of money sent/disbursed to the beneficiaries accounts along with number \nof beneficiaries under the Pradhan Mantri Kisan Samman Nidhi Yojana (PM-KISAN) since \nits inception year/State-wise specially in Uttar Pradesh, district-wise; \n(b)  whether the Government is aware of the fact that more than Rs. 3000 crore has been \nsent to the accounts of people with unauthorised usernames and thousands of \nunintended beneficiaries;  \n(c)   if so, the details of the beneficiaries, State-wise specially in Uttar Pradesh; \n(d)  the mechanism through which Government plans to recover the lost amount; \n(e)  the steps taken for effective implementation of the PM-KISAN scheme; and \n(f)  whether the Government has any plan to increase the amount given to farmers and \nif so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE  \nक ृ िष एवं िकसान कʞ ाण मंũी   (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) to (e): Pradhan Mantri Kisan Samman Nidhi (PM-KISAN), one of the largest Central \nSector Schemes, is being implemented to supplement financial needs of the land holding \nfarmers of the country subject to certain exclusion criteria relating to higher income status. \nUnder the scheme, financial benefit of Rs 6000/- annually, in three equal installments, is \ntransferred into the Aadhaar seeded bank accounts of farmers. \n \n\n Benefits of the Scheme are given to the farmer family. The farmer family should \nown cultivable land, as per land record system of the concerned State/UT. Cut-off date \nfor land ownership is 1st February 2019, except in case of inheritance due to death of a \nbeneficiary. \n \n As per Operational Guidelines of the Scheme, State/UTs identify and verify the \neligible farmers before uploading the data on PM-KISAN Portal. Benefits are transferred \nto the beneficiaries through Direct Benefit Transfer mode after validation of the uploaded \ndata on PM-KISAN Portal, which has been integrated with Unique Identification Authority \nof India (UIDAI) for Aadhaar authentication; with Public Finance Management System \n(PFMS) for validation of account and Government employees/pensioners data; with \nIncome Tax Department for validation of income tax payee status; and with National \nPayment Corporation of India (NPCI) for account validation and Aadhaar based \npayments. \n  \n Eligible famers are being enrolled and deceased/ineligible beneficiaries are being \nremoved by way of continuous verification and validation of the beneficiary’s data. \n \n In view of the above, the benefits are transferred only to intended beneficiaries \nafter due verification and validation.  \n \n The number of beneficiaries under PM-KISAN Scheme from inception, state-wise, \ninstallment-wise is enclosed at Annexure-I. \n \nThe details of amount disbursed under PM-KISAN Scheme from inception, state-\nwise, installment-wise, is enclosed at Annexure-II. \n \nThe details of beneficiaries in Uttar Pradesh under PM-KISAN Scheme district-\nwise and the amount disbursed to them is enclosed at Annexure III. \n \n \nThe Government has taken the following steps to ensure that all eligible farmer \nfamilies get benefit under the PM-KISAN Scheme: - \n \n(i) A special facility has been provided to the farmers in the PM-KISAN Portal, namely \nFarmers Corner. Through this facility, farmers can do their self-registration. \n \n(ii) A special mobile APP has been launched on 24th February 2020. The APP provides \nthe facilities available through Farmers Corner. The app has been  revamped in June \n2023 to include facial authentication facility. \n \n(iii) To further help farmers a 24x7 IVRS based helpline 155261 has been set up. \n \n(iv) Common Service Centres have been authorized to assist the farmers in registration \nfor the PM-KISAN Scheme, updation of their data and in ascertaining the status of \n\nprocessing of their payment. For this the farmers have to pay a nominal fee of Rs. 15/- \nfor new registration and Rs. 10/- for updation of record/data. \n \n(v) State Governments have appointed nodal officers for the scheme at Block, District \nand State Level. The Union Government conducts regular meetings with the State \nOfficials through Video Conferencing to ensure smooth implementation of the Scheme. \n \n(vi) The States /UTs have organized publicity/awareness camps to ensure expeditious \nverification of the data of the registered beneficiaries. \n \n(vii) States/UTs have been asked to expedite the process of saturation of the Scheme by \nensuring enrollment of all eligible farmer families. \n \n(f): Presently there is no such proposal to increase the amount given to farmers. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |784", "t": "Drought Management Code-2016", "head": "O.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.  784 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.07.2023 \n \nDROUGHT MANAGEMENT CODE-2016 \n  \n784.# SHRI ARJUN LAL MEENA: \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE क ृ िष एवं िकसान कʞ ाण मंũी \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to make necessary amendments in the Drought \nManagement Code-2016 with respect to extension of the period of declaration of drought \ntill the month of July of the next year or till the onset of monsoon, whichever is earlier; \naccording to the impact of the drought; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and  \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nक ृ िष एवं िकसान कʞ ाण मंũी  (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) to (c):  No, Sir. As per the Manual for Drought Management, 2016 (Drought Manual), \nthe State Government is required to declare drought through a notification for Kharif, not \nlater than 31st October and for Rabi, not later than 31st March. The validity of such drought \nnotification is not more than 6 months. In case of delayed sowing/transplanting, the States \ncan ask for extension of drought declaration date (31st October / 31st March) of/upto 3 weeks \non providing documentary evidence. \n \nThe Manual for Drought Management has been prepared through a consultative process, \ninvolving the concerned Central Ministries/Departments, State Governments, Scientific, \nTechnical and Research Organizations. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |785", "t": "Khushal Kisan Yojana", "head": "O.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 785 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 25Th JULY, 2023 \n \nKHUSHAL KISAN YOJANA \n \n \n785. DR. BHARATIBEN DHIRUBHAI SHIYAL: \n  \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE क ृ षि एवं ककसान  कल्याण मंत्री  \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government proposes to increase the area under Khushal Kisan Yojana;  \n \n(b)  if so, the details of the work being done in this regard, State-wise; and  \n \n(c)  the details of the area on whi ch natural farming is being done at present in the country, \nState-wise? \n \n \nANSWER \n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nक ृ षि एवं ककसान  कल्याण मंत्री     (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) & (b): State Govt. of Himachal Pradesh has informed that it is  implementing ‘Prakritik \nKheti Khushhal Kissan’  (PK3Y) Yojana which is a State run scheme of Government of  \nHimachal Pradesh since 2018. The techniques of Prakritik Kheti Khushhal Kissan are low cost, \nlocal resourced based, climate resilient and non chemic al based. The major ingredients of the \nvarious concoctions, which are used in the techniques, are indigenous cow dung, cow urine, \npulse flour and natural plant extracts, which are generally available at home and nothing is to \nbe purchased from external mar ket.  The PK3Y is being implemented in a mission mode by \n‘State Project Implementing Unit’ (SPIU). The Yojana is being monitored by a ‘State Level Apex \nCommittee’ headed by Hon’ble Chief Minister of Himachal Pradesh \n \nTo promote the scheme and to cover maxi mum farmers under the scheme the 5 tier approach \nis being followed in the State: \n1. Mass sensitization of farmers. \n2. 2-6 days village /district/state level trainings. \n3. Exposure visits (intra/inter-state) to showcase the natural farming models. \n4. Adoption by farmers. \n5. Regular visits and handholding. \nAdditionally to support the farmers, the following assistances are being given under the Yojna: \n\n1. Purchase of Indigenous cow - ₹25,000/- or 50 % of total cost whichever is less + ₹5,000/ -\n(transportation). \n2.  Lining of cowshed for collection of cow urine- ₹8,000/- or 80 % of cost whichever is less. \n3. On farm input generation (purchase of drums for preparation of various formulations) - \n₹750/- per drum (max. 3 drums /farmer). \nSansadhan Bhandar (a shop at village level for sale of various natural farming formulations on \npre decided rates to those farmers who does not have cow - ₹ 10,000/- one time for opening of \nSansadhan Bhandar.   \n \n(c): Government of India is promoting natural farming si nce 2019-20 under Bhartiya Prakritik \nKrishi Paddh ati (BPKP), a sub scheme of P arampargat Krishi Vikas Yojna  (PKVY). Natural \nFarming is chemical free farming based on the integrated agriculture  and animal husbandry \napproach using livestock and local resourc es. It is largely based on on -farm biomass recycling \nwith major stress on biomass mulching, use of on -farm cow dung-urine formulations from local \nlivestock's. About 4.09 lakh ha. area under 8 states viz. Andhra Pradesh, Chhattisgarh, Kerala, \nHimachal Pradesh, Jharkhand, Odisha, Madhya Pradesh and Tamil Nadu are covered under \nthe BPKP scheme. \n \nThe State wise details of area covered under Natural Farming under BPKP \nSl. No. States Area in Ha \n1. Andhra Pradesh 100000 \n2. Chhattisgarh 85000 \n3. Kerala 84000 \n4. Himachal Pradesh 12000 \n5 Jharkhand 3400 \n6. Odisha 24000 \n7. Madhya Pradesh 99000 \n8. Tamil Nadu 2000 \n Total 409400 \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |787", "t": "Pradhan Mantri Formalisation of Micro Food Enterprises", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 787 \nANSWERED ON 25.07.2023 \n \nPRADHAN MANTRI FORMALISATION OF MICRO FOOD ENTERPRISES \n \n787.  SHRI ARVIND DHARMAPURI: \n \n  Will the MINISTER OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES be pleased to state: \n(a) the funds allocated and utilized under Pradhan Mantri Formalisation of Micro food processing \nEnterprises (PMFME) since its inception especially for Telangana, State-wise; \n(b) the details of the total number of beneficiaries, district-wise in Telangana and the total amount \nof loan disbursed to the beneficiaries since the inception of the scheme; and \n \n(c) whether the Government is planning to take any special steps in order to promot e more \nparticipation by women, SC, ST, Minorities and other backward groups and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n \n(a): State/UT-wise allocation of funds is not allocated under the Pradhan Mantri Formalization of \nMicro Food Processing Enterprises (PMFME) scheme. A total of ₹986.966 crores has, however, been \nreleased to different States/UTs, including ₹65.35 9 crores to Telangana towards implementation of \nthe scheme. The State-wise details of fund released is given at Annexure-I. \n \n(b): The details of number of beneficiaries supported along with amount of loan disbursed to the \nbeneficiaries since the inception of the scheme is given at Annexure-II.  \n \n(c): Besides extending credit linked subsidy support to PMFME beneficiaries, Seed Capital is \nprovided to members of women centric SHGs which are engaged in food processing activity.   \nEstablishment of Mini -TRIFOOD Units for primary and secondary processing of Minor Fo rest \nProduces is also supported under the scheme, in convergence with TRIFED. Extension of benefit of \nadditional interest subvention is also facilitated for SC/ST beneficiaries in convergence with National \nScheduled Tribes Finance & Development Corporation  (NSTFDC), and National Scheduled Caste \nFinance & Development Corporation (NSFDC).  \n \n \n \n***** \n  \n\n                                                                        \n   ANNEXURE-I \nANNEXURE REFERRED IN REPLY TO PART ( a) OF THE LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 787 FOR ANSWER ON 25.07.2023 REGARDING ‘PRADHAN MANTRI \nFORMALISATION OF MICRO FOOD ENTERPRISES’ \nDetails of fund released under PMFME Scheme to States/UTs \nSN State/UT Release (₹ in crore) \n1 Andaman & Nicobar Island 3.739 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 104.227 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 7.497 \n4 Assam 36.448 \n5 Bihar 45.321 \n6 Chandigarh 1.739 \n7 Chhattisgarh 23.576 \n8 D & N Haveli & D n D 1.290 \n9 Delhi 1.732 \n10 Goa 6.120 \n11 Gujarat 25.023 \n12 Haryana 6.372 \n13 Himachal Pradesh 19.746 \n14 Jammu & Kashmir 12.140 \n15 Jharkhand 4.428 \n16 Karnataka 60.307 \n17 Kerala 23.782 \n18 Ladakh 2.932 \n19 Lakshadweep 1.010 \n20 Madhya Pradesh 28.618 \n21 Maharashtra 158.474 \n22 Manipur 6.411 \n23 Meghalaya 5.735 \n24 Mizoram 10.424 \n25 Nagaland 12.547 \n26 Odisha 58.229 \n27 Puducherry 2.538 \n28 Punjab 12.497 \n29 Rajasthan 43.952 \n30 Sikkim 6.790 \n31 Tamil Nadu 47.559 \n32 Telangana 65.359 \n33 Tripura 13.339 \n34 Uttar Pradesh 116.857 \n35 Uttarakhand 8.209 \n  Grand Total  984.966 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nANNEXURE-II \nANNEXURE REFERRED IN REPLY TO PART ( b) OF THE LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 787 FOR ANSWER ON 25.07.2023 REGARDING ‘PRADHAN MANTRI \nFORMALISATION OF MICRO FOOD ENTERPRISES’ \nDistrict-wise Details of Beneficiary & Loan Disbursed in Telangana \nSN District \nNo. of Beneficiaries \nsupported / Loans \nSanctioned \nNo. of Loans \nDisbursed \nAmount of Loans \nDisbursed (₹ in \nlakhs) \n1 Adilabad 72 53 116 \n2 Bhadradri Kothagudem 136 85 156 \n3 Hyderabad 2 1 9 \n4 Jagitial 177 109 474 \n5 Jangoan 119 60 104 \n6 Jayashankar Bhupalapally 33 8 17 \n7 Jogulamba Gadwal 44 37 69 \n8 Kamareddy 103 45 92 \n9 Karimnagar 159 95 680 \n10 Khammam 119 83 217 \n11 Kumuram Bheem Asifabad 46 12 21 \n12 Mahabubabad 35 22 62 \n13 Mahabubnagar 59 37 33 \n14 Mancherial 148 93 181 \n15 Medak 146 66 156 \n16 Medchal Malkajgiri 37 16 179 \n17 Mulugu 16 11 28 \n18 Nagarkurnool 106 87 162 \n19 Nalgonda 60 35 159 \n20 Narayanpet 48 42 177 \n21 Nirmal 103 56 152 \n22 Nizamabad 98 53 338 \n23 Peddapalli 60 46 153 \n24 Rajanna Sircilla 135 67 428 \n25 Ranga Reddy 46 18 72 \n26 Sangareddy 237 143 213 \n27 Siddipet 193 139 495 \n28 Suryapet 221 147 397 \n29 Vikarabad 88 54 427 \n30 Wanaparthy 49 29 68 \n31 Warangal Rural 45 19 83 \n32 Warangal Urban 45 28 127 \n33 Yadadri Bhuvanagiri 143 99 230 \nGrand Total 3128 1895 6277 \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |788", "t": "Artificial Inseminations", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING  \nDEPARTMENT OF ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING  \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 788 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25TH JULY, 2023 \n \nARTIFICIAL INSEMINATIONS \n \n788. SHRI JAYANT SINHA: \nWill the Minister of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying  \nमत्स्यपाऱन, पशुपाऱन और डेयरी मंत्री \nbe pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the achievements of the Rashtriya Gokul Mission in enhancing milk production and \nproductivity of bovines since inception;  \n(b) the detailed manner in which the Mission has led to an increase in income of farmers, \nState and district-wise including Jharkhand; and  \n(c) the number of artificial inseminations conducted under the Mission since its inception, \nState and district-wise including Jharkhand? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER FOR FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n (SHRI PARSHOTTAM RUPALA) \n \n(a) The Department is implementing Rashtriya Gokul Mission  (RGM) for development \nand conservation of indigenous breeds, genetic upgradation of bovine population and \nenhancement of milk production and productivity of bovines since 2014. The scheme has \nbeen continued under umbrella scheme Development Programme from 2021 to 2026 with an \nallocation of Rs.2400 crore. The major achievementmade under the scheme is enclosed at \nAnnexure-I. \n \n(b)and (c) The scheme is playing important role in enhancing milk production and \nproductivity of bovines to meet growing demand of milk and making dairying more \nremunerative to the rural farmers of the country. The scheme has been leading to \nmultiplication of elite animals of indigenous breeds and increased availability of indigenous \nstock and benefits of the scheme has been percolating to 8 crore farmers engaged in dairying \n\nespecially small and marginal farmers and landless labours. Due to implementation of \nscheme and other measures taken by Government of India milk production in the country has \nincreased from 146.3 million tonnes 2014 -15 to 2 21 million tonnes in 2021 -22 with annual \ngrowth rate of 6.3 %.The value of milk output in 20 20-21 was Rs. 9.32 lakh crore surpassing \ntotal value of output from food grains. Under the Rashtriya Gokul Mission  Nationwide \nArtificial Insemination Programme (NAIP) has been initiated for delivery of free of cost \nquality artificial insemination services at farmers’ doorstep in 604 districts  with less than \n50% AI coverage. Under the programme, as on date 5.82 crore a nimals have been \ninseminated,7.24 core artificial inseminations performed, and 3.80 crore farmers benefitted. \nThe number of artificial inseminations conducted under the Nationwide Artificial \nInsemination Programme (NAIP) since its inception, State and dist rict-wise including \nJharkhand is enclosed at Annexure-II. \n\nAnnexure-I \n \nAchievements of Rashtriya Gokul Mission scheme \n \n1. Nationwide AI Programme has been initiated in September 2019 and under the \nprogramme AI services delivered free of cost at farmers’ doorstep.  Nationwide \nArtificial Insemination Programme (NAIP) Phase-IV has been initiated from 1 August \n2022 covering 3.3 crores animals through artificial insemination in 604 districts with \nless than 50% Al coverage.  Till date, 5.82 crore animals have been covered, 7.26 core \nartificial inseminations performed, and 3.81 crore farmers benefitted under NAIP. \n \n2. Accelerated Breed Improvement Programme for establishing 2 lakh IVF pregnancies \nin the identified milk pockets has been initiated. Farmers taking IVF technology are \nIncentivized @ Rs 5000 per pregnancy and balance amount will be met by the farmer \ntaking up IVF pregnancy. Further 19 Embryo Transfer Technology (ETT)/ In -Vitro \nFertilization (IVF) labs have been made functional. From these labs 18110 viable  \nembryos have been produced and 1525 calves born through embryo transfer. \n \n3. Accelerated Breed Improvement Programme using sex sorted semen is implemented \nto produce female calves with 90% accuracy. Farmers taking Al with sex sorted \nsemen are incentivized @ Rs 750 per pregnancy and balance amount will be met by \nthe farmer taking up pregnancy through sex sorted semen. So far 36.62 lakh doses of \nsex sorted semen has been produced at Government semen stations assisted under \nRashtriya Gokul Mission and 38.92 lakh  doses from Milk Federation, NGO and \nprivate semen stations. \n \n4. Breed Multiplication Farm component has been initiated to attract entrepreneurship \nfor the dairy sector, and to simultaneously create the opportunity for developing a hub \nand spoke model of dairy farming where small and marginal dairy farmers can thrive \nwith the help of a local hub of reliable dairy services. Subsidy of 50% (up to Rs 2 \ncrores per farm) on capital cost (excluding land cost) is proposed to be provided to \nprivate entrepreneurs under this scheme for establishment of breed multiplication \nfarms of minimum herd size of 200 heads of bovines in the country except for Arid \ndistricts, hilly states and north eastern "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |79", "t": "Funds for Highways", "head": "O.I.H.\n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 7\n9\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 25\nTH\n \nJULY, 2023\n \n \n \n“\nDIGITAL TECHNOLOGY IN AGRICULTURE\n”\n \n \n*\n7\n9\n.\n \n \nSHRI\n \nSUNIL \nKUMAR PINTU\n:\n \nSHRI \nJUGAL KISHORE SHARMA\n:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n \n(a)\n \nwhether the Government agrees with the view that the digital technology can play \na transformative role in modernising our agriculture;\n \n \n(b)\n \nif so, the details of initiatives taken/proposed by the Government to promote digital \nagriculture in Hooghly of West Ben\ngal, Amravati of Maharashtra, Sitamarhi and \nBhagalpur of Bihar, Jammu and Kashmir, Madhya Pradesh, Jharkhand and Sonipat \ndistrict of Haryana; and \n \n \n(c)\n \nif not, the reasons therefor?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n     \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n(a) \nto (\nc\n): \n      \nA statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT IN RESPECT OF PARTS (a) TO (\nc\n) OF THE LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 7\n9\n \nDUE FOR ANSWER ON 25.07.2023 REGARDING \n“\nDIGITAL \nTECHNOLOGY IN AGRICULTURE\n”\n.\n \n \n(a):\n \nYes, Sir. \nDigital technologies are finding increasing use in the agricultural value \nsystem, and farmers are increasingly becoming more informed. \n \n \n(b)\n: \n \nGovernment has taken various measures to provide access to technology and \ninformation across the country, through \nvariou\ns Digital Initiatives, such as:\n \n \ni.\n \nNational e\n-\nGovernance Plan in Agriculture (NeGP\n-\nA) wherein, funds are \nprovided to the State(s)/UT(s) fo\nr project involving use of modern \ntechnologies viz. Artificial Intelligence (AI), Machine Learning (ML), \nRobotics, Drones, Data Analytics, Block Chain etc. \nAfter receiving \nproposals from the States, funds are released for development of various \nsolutions. \n \nii.\n \nThe Government has announced for development of Digital Public \nInfrastructure (DPI) for agriculture as an open source, open standard and \ninter\n-\noperable public good to enable inclusive farmer centric solutions \nthrough relevant information services for crop\n \nplanning and health, \nimproved access to farm inputs, credit and insurance, help for crop \nestimation, market intelligence etc\n \nincluding states of \nWest Bengal, \nMaharashtra, Bihar, Jammu and Kashmir, Madhya Pradesh, Jharkhand and \nHaryana\n.\n \nIn this regards, fo\nllowing action has been taken so far:\n \n\n \nArchitecture of three core registries i.e. Farmer registry, Geo \nreferencing of village map registry, crop sown registry has been \nfinalized.\n \n\n \nTo generate crop sown registry, Digital crop survey has been \nlaunched on \npilot basis in 12 states from Kharif 2023.\n \n\n \nA\nn\n \nMoU\n \nhas been signed \nwith \nPixxel Space India Pvt. Limited\nto \ndevelop use cases with the hyperspectral data of Pixxel for crop \nidentification and mapping, crop health monitoring and soil organic \ncarbon estimation \nover selected regions on pilot basis. \n \n \n\n \niii.\n \nSub Mission on Agricultural Mechanization (SMAM) is being implemented \nw.e.f\n \nApril, 2014.The scheme aims at 'reaching the unreached' by bringing \nto the small and marginal farmers in the core and giving the benefits of farm \nmechanization, by Promoting 'Custom Hiring Centers' , creating hubs for hi\n-\ntech & high value farm equipments,\n \ndistribution of various agricultural \nequipments, creating awareness among stakeholders through \ndemonstration and capacity building activities, and ensuring performance\n-\n \ntesting and certification at designated testing centers located all over the \ncountry.\n \niv.\n \nNational Agriculture Market (e\n-\nNAM) is a pan\n-\nIndia electronic trading portal \nwhich networks the existing Agricultural Produce Market Committee \n(APMC) mandis to create a unified national market for agricultural \ncommodities. Digital services are provided to \ntraders, farmers, Farmers \nProducer Organizations (FPO), Mandis through various modules of e\n-\nNAM \nplatform such as FPO trading module, warehouse based trading module.\n \nv.\n \nUnder PM KISAN Scheme, fund is directly transferred into the bank \naccounts of the eligible \nfarmers under Direct Benefit Transfer mode. \nFarmers can do their self\n-\n \nregistration through the Farmers Corner in the \nportal. PM\n-\nKISAN Mobile App was launched to broaden the reach of the \nscheme where farmers can view beneficiary status, update or carry out\n \ncorrections of name based on their Aadhaar card and also they can see \nhistory of benefits transferred to their bank accounts.\n \nRecently, face \nauthentication feature has also been included in PM\n-\nKISAN mobile App.\n \nvi.\n \nAgriculture Infrastructure Fund (AIF): To mo\nbilize a medium \n-\n \nlong term debt \nfinances facility for investment in viable projects for post\n-\nharvest \nmanagement Infrastructure and community farming assets through \n"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |790", "t": "Fall in Participation of Women under MGNREGS", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 790 \n  ANSWERED ON 25/07/2023 \n \nFALL IN PARTICIPATION OF WOMEN UNDER MGNREGS \n \n790.  SHRI MAHESH SAHOO: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the number of women participants in Mahatma Gandhi \nNational Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme (MGNREGS) \nduring each of the last five years, State-wise; \n(b)  whether there has been a gradual fall in the participation of \nwomen over the last five years and if so, the reasons therefor, \nState-wise; \n(c)  the details of the States which has seen the fall; and \n(d)  the participation of women in Odisha during such period, \ndistrict-wise? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n  \n(a):   State/UT-wise number of women who availed employment \nunder Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee \nScheme (Mahatma Gandhi NREGS) in last five financial year s from \n2018-19 to 2022-23 is given at Annexure-I. \n  \n(b): No, Sir. \n  \n(c): Does not arise. \n  \n(d):    District-wise the  rate of participation of women (women \nperson-days out of total in percentage) in the State of Odisha under \nMahatma Gandhi NREGS in last fiv