re-II \n \nAnnexure-II referred in reply to part (d) of Lok Sabha Unstarred  Question \nNo. 790 dated 25.07.2023. \n  \nDistrict-wise the rate of participation of women (women person -days out \nof total in percentage) in the State of Odisha under Mahatma Gandhi \nNREGS in last five financial years from 2018-19 to 2022-23. \nSl. \nNo. District \nPercentage of women participation \n2018-19 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 2022-23 \n1 Angul 45.49 46.05 45.57 49.31 54.06 \n2 Baleshwar 40.04 43.27 40.38 42.20 43.98 \n3 Bargarh 34.43 35.21 37.41 36.99 39.44 \n4 Bhadrak 35.04 39.81 41.46 42.90 44.24 \n5 Bolangir 41.19 40.59 41.47 42.32 43.45 \n6 Boudh 40.82 41.63 40.40 41.86 44.43 \n7 Cuttack 34.06 39.41 33.10 35.82 38.85 \n8 Deogarh 48.21 48.34 49.66 48.34 50.52 \n9 Dhenkanal 43.74 46.17 46.44 46.96 46.83 \n10 Gajapati 44.57 45.78 47.19 48.34 49.10 \n11 Ganjam 51.00 53.44 54.13 55.52 58.56 \n12 Jagatsinghapur 34.13 38.08 38.75 40.03 40.72 \n13 Jajpur 29.66 35.26 38.30 37.04 37.91 \n14 Jharsuguda 40.28 42.51 45.00 44.84 45.09 \n15 Kalahandi 38.38 39.83 42.89 44.28 46.85 \n16 Kandhamal 42.95 44.16 47.47 48.47 50.25 \n17 Kendrapara 33.43 39.78 33.09 36.24 38.90 \n18 Kendujhar 44.20 45.33 47.78 48.21 50.47 \n19 Khordha 39.01 40.80 40.64 40.68 41.75 \n20 Koraput 39.02 38.23 42.94 44.78 46.28 \n21 Malkangiri 33.90 37.50 42.24 42.81 43.77 \n22 Mayurbhanj 44.82 43.49 45.17 47.09 49.34 \n23 Nabarangapur 39.12 41.10 43.13 42.74 44.24 \n24 Nayagarh 41.42 41.40 41.57 43.33 44.46 \n25 Nuapada 41.36 42.05 44.61 45.01 46.80 \n26 Puri 35.68 38.02 40.24 40.88 40.10 \n27 Rayagada 42.63 44.33 46.25 46.68 47.54 \n28 Sambalpur 41.79 41.71 42.54 41.96 42.84 \n29 Sonepur 32.62 39.50 36.79 36.37 36.79 \n30 Sundargarh 44.59 44.53 46.68 46.37 46.97 \n \nState Level 41.99 43.30 44.74 46.14 47.94 \n(As per NREGASoft) \n**** \n  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |792", "t": "Disaster Assessment Teams", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 792 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 25TH JULY, 2023/ SRAVANA 3, 1945 (SAKA) \n \nDISASTER ASSESSMENT TEAMS \n \n792.      SHRI MAGUNTA SREENIVASULU REDDY: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware that it takes considerable time for \ndisaster assessment teams to be constituted and plan and arrange \nlogistics for these teams to assess the site of a disaster after it has \nstruck; \n \n(b) if so, the measures taken by the Government to redress this alongwith \nthe details of the time taken to constitute a disaster assessment team and \ndeploy it to the site after a disaster has struck during the last three years; \n \n(c) whether the Government has taken any steps to improve the \nmethodology through which disaster sites are assessed which at present \nare primitive and do not provide an accurate picture; \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and \n \n(e) the details of the sites of disaster and the days the disaster \nmanagement team spent assessing the site including the exact area of the \nsite? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI NITYANAND RAI) \n \n(a) to (e): The primary responsibility for disaster management rests with \nthe State Governments concerned. The State Governments provide \nfinancial relief to the affected people in the event of notified natural \ndisasters, from the State Disaster Response Fund (SDRF), already placed \nat its disposal, in accordance with approved items and norms of \nGovernment  of  India (GOI).  However,  in  the event of disaster of a severe \n \n\n-2- \nL.S.US.Q.NO. 792 FOR 25.07.2023 \n \n nature, additional financial assistance is extended from the National \nDisaster Response Fund (NDRF), as per laid down procedure, which \nincludes an assessment based on the visit of an Inter-Ministerial Central \nTeam (IMCT).   \n \nPreviously, the IMCT used to be deputed after the receipt of Memorandum \nfrom the State Government affected by a disaster of ‘severe’ nature.  In \norder to expedite the assessment and release of additional financial \nassistance to States, Ministry of Home Affairs had taken a significant \ndecision on 19.08.2019, to depute an IMCT immediately in the aftermath of \nany natural calamity of ‘severe nature’, and without waiting for the \nmemorandum from the State Government. \n \nIMCT comprises of representatives of sectoral Ministries/ Departments, to \nassess the sectoral damages, such as agriculture, power, rural \ndevelopment, irrigation, road transport. IMCT visit is finalized in \nconsultation with the State Government, and it covers those areas, which \nare severely affected, along with the officials of the State concerned. \nIMCT assess the ground situation on the basis of data provided by the \nState Government and inputs of scientific and technical agencies of the \nCentral Government, such as India Meteorological Department, Central \nWater Commission, National Remote Sensing Centre.  \n \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |793", "t": "PMGSY Phase-III in Rajasthan", "head": " O.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 793 \n  ANSWERED ON 25/07/2023 \n \nPMGSY PHASE-III IN RAJASTHAN \n \n793.  SHRI DEVJI M. PATEL: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the details of the proposals received from Rajasthan and the \nfunds allocated to it under the third phase of Pradhan Mantri \nGram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY), district-wise; \n(b)  the current status of the proposals received in respect of \nRajasthan under this yojana; \n(c)  whether the Government contemplates to approve the \nremaining tasks of road extension under the third phase of \nPMGSY; and \n(d)  if so, the details thereof along with the time by which it is \nlikely to be done? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a) to (d) The State of Rajasthan has been allocated 8,662.5 km road \nlength under Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY) -III. The \ntimeline of implementation of PMGSY III is March, 2025. Against the \nallocated target,   8,221.76  km road length has already been \nsanctioned at an outlay of Rs. 4,888.26 crore. The district wise \ndetails of road length sanctioned and value of projects are given in \nAnnexure. \n  \nThe State Government has  also submitted proposals of 38 roads of \n435 km for sanction under PMGSY III. The technical observations of \nNational Rural Infrastructure Development Agency (NRIDA) on the \nsample Detailed Project Reports (DPRs) have been sent to the State \nGovernment for compliance.  \n  \n***** \n\n Annexure \nAnnexure referred to in part (a) to  (d) of Lok Sabha Unstarred \nQuestion No. 793 for 25.07.2023 \nDistrict wise details of road length sanctioned under PMGSY-III \nalongwith Value of Sanctioned Project  \n  \nName of the District Number of \nroads \nRoad Length \n(in km) \nValue of Projects \n(Rs. in crores) \nAjmer 25 287.91 190.68 \nAlwar 39 311.39 179.14 \nBanswara 23 172.34 113.43 \nBaran 23 182.16 96.85 \nBarmer 38 439.46 224.95 \nBaharatpur 27 212.69 149.29 \nBhilwara 30 253.95 148.16 \nBikaner 25 263.86 114.39 \nBundi 23 177.38 130.08 \nChittaurgarh 33 252.25 153.33 \nChuru 23 222.74 128.83 \nDausa 28 235.75 121.20 \nDholpur 25 241.19 119.53 \nDungarpur 25 267.22 177.40 \nHanumangarh 22 217.64 103.43 \nJaipur 53 525.96 305.92 \nJaisalmer 20 196.90 96.43 \nJalor 17 139.78 71.53 \nJhalawar 13 104.40 48.59 \nJhunjhunun 28 214.41 128.12 \nJodhpur 45 524.47 296.99 \nKarauli 18 149.32 85.30 \nKota 16 116.69 76.80 \nNagaur 9 117.80 65.06 \nPali 41 408.14 269.53 \nPartapgarh 12 114.78 76.98 \nRajsamand 23 245.15 182.49 \nSawaimadhopur 28 188.37 103.13 \nSikar 31 325.98 205.17 \nSirohi 11 102.49 96.79 \nSri Ganganagar 18 192.53 90.71 \nTonk 32 239.34 139.20 \nUdaipur 56 577.33 398.81 \nTotal 880 8221.76 4,888.26 \n*** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |794", "t": "Study to Review Work under MGNREGS", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 794 \n  ANSWERED ON 25/07/2023 \n \nSTUDY TO REVIEW WORK UNDER MGNREGS \n \n794.  MS. CHANDRANI MURMU: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has conducted any study to review \nthe works done under Mahatma Gandhi National Rural \nEmployment Guarantee Scheme (MGNREGS) in States across \nthe country; \n(b)  if so, the details of the study particularly for the State of \nOdisha;  and \n(c) the details of work done and funds allocated and utilised in \nKeonjhar and Mayurbhanj districts? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a)&(b): Government of India has made assessment of \nimplementation of the Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment \nGuarantee Scheme (Mahatma Gandhi NREGS) through third party \nstudy in terms of increase in household income, poverty alleviation \netc. sponsored by NITI Aayog in 2020. Some of the key findings of \nthe study are as under: \n  \n(i) Mahatma Gandhi NREGA is a powerful instrument for inclusive \ngrowth in rural India through its impact on social protection, \nlivelihood security and democratic empowerment. \n  \n(ii) Mahatma Gandhi NREGS provide livelihood security for the poor \nthrough creation of durable assets, improved water security, soil \nconservation and higher land productivity. \n  \n(iii) Mahatma Gandhi NREGS has had a positive impact on the \nstandard o f living of households through increase in household \nincome by improvements in production from agriculture. \n\n  \n(iv) Mahatma Gandhi NREGA has led to major increases in wages of \nrural workers \n  \n(v) SC, ST and Women along with socially excluded communities \nbenefit from and are included in Mahatma Gandhi NREGA plans \nwhich could be a significant indicator of poverty alleviation in rural \nareas. \n  \n(c):  Mahatma Gandhi NREGS is a demand driven wage employment \nScheme. There is no district wise allocation of funds under the \nScheme. \n  \nThe details of works completed and expenditure incurred in the \ndistricts of Keonjhar and Mayurbhanj of Odisha under Mahatma \nGandhi NREGS in the previous financial year 2022 -23 and current \nfinancial year 2023-24 (as on 21.07.2023) are given below:  \n  \n District Keonjhar Mayurbhanj \nFinancial Year \n2022-23 \n2023-24 \n(as on \n21.07.2023) 2022-23 \n2023-24 \n(as on \n21.07.2023) \nWorks completed (in \nnumber) 24793 3079 34896 6575 \nTotal expenditure (in \nlakh) 28583.83 10379.85 47909.09 17106.38 \n(As per NREGASoft) \n  \n  \n****** \n  \n  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |795", "t": "Beneficiaries under PM-KISAN Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 795 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25th JULY,  2023 \n \nBENEFICIARIES UNDER PM-KISAN SCHEME \n \n795. MS. NUSSRAT JAHAN: \n \nWill the Minister of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare क ृ िष एवं िकसान कʞ ाण मंũी \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government is aware that the number of   beneficiaries under Pradhan \nMantri Kisan Samman Nidhi Yojana (PM-KISAN) has reduced drastically since its \ninception in the year 2018; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof along with the details of the beneficiaries under PM-KISAN \nScheme, State-wise since its inception; and \n(c)  the amount that has been transferred to beneficiaries under said Scheme, State-\nwise since its inception? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE क ृ िष एवं िकसान कʞ ाण मंũी \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) The number of beneficiaries under Prime Minister’s  Kisan Samman Nidhi (PM-Kisan) \nhas increased from 3.16 crore in the 1st period