ides demand driven, \nplacement oriented National Skill Qualification Framework (NSQF) compliant skilling programme to \nsupplement the efforts of the industry in creating jobs in the organized textile and related sectors, covering \nthe entire value chain of textiles, excluding Spinning and Weaving, which have been converged under \nPradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana (PMKVY) of M/o Skill Development & Entrepreneurship. Samarth \nScheme has been extended upto March, 2024. State/UT -wise details of training centres operating in the \ncountry under Samarth scheme are given in Annexure-I. \n \n(b): ISDS was operational upto 2017 -18. The details of number of benefici aries under Samarth \nduring the last three years and the current year, State/UT -wise including Tamil Nadu are given in \nAnnexure-II. \n \n(c) & (d): Ministry of Textiles is implementing Samarth Scheme on Pan India basis, including small \ncities. Ministry of Textiles has been implementing various schemes viz, Production Linked Incentive (PLI) \nScheme, Pradhan Mantri Mega Integrated Textile Region and Appa rel (PM MITRA), Amended \nTechnology Upgradation Scheme (ATuFS), Silk Samagra, National Handloom Development Programme, \nNational Handicraft Development Programme, Integrated Wool Development Programme (IWDP), \nNational Technical Textile Mission (NTTM), Scheme  for Integrated Textile Parks (SITP) etc. catering \nexclusively for development of textile sector in the country. \n\nAnnexure-I \nDetails of textiles training centres operating in the country under Samarth scheme, State/UT -\nwise: \n(as on 20.07.2023) \nS. No. State/ UT Number of training centres  \n1 Andaman and Nicobar 1 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 40 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 4 \n4 Assam 106 \n5 Bihar 35 \n6 Chandigarh 1 \n7 Chhattisgarh 16 \n8 Delhi 24 \n9 Goa 2 \n10 Gujarat 43 \n11 Haryana 43 \n12 Himachal Pradesh 5 \n13 Jammu and Kashmir 24 \n14 Jharkhand 19 \n15 Karnataka 435 \n16 Kerala 7 \n17 Madhya Pradesh 41 \n18 Maharashtra 42 \n19 Manipur 3 \n20 Meghalaya 4 \n21 Mizoram 3 \n22 Nagaland 13 \n23 Odisha 32 \n24 Puducherry 3 \n25 Punjab 6 \n26 Rajasthan 46 \n27 Sikkim 2 \n28 Tamil Nadu 504 \n29 Telangana 45 \n30 The Dadra and Nagar Haveli and \nDaman and Diu 7 \n31 Tripura 13 \n32 Uttar Pradesh 284 \n33 Uttarakhand 7 \n34 West Bengal 20 \n Total 1,880 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure-II \nDetails of beneficiaries under Samarth Scheme during the last three years and current year, \nstate/UT wise including Tamil Nadu: \n (as on 20.07.2023) \n**** \n \n \n \nS. No. State/ UT Number of beneficiaries trained during last 3 \nyears & current year  \n1 Andaman and Nicobar 66 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 5,011 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 57 \n4 Assam 5,641 \n5 Bihar 3,615 \n6 Chandigarh 87 \n7 Chhattisgarh 1,837 \n8 Delhi 3,187 \n9 Goa 55 \n10 Gujarat 6,781 \n11 Haryana 9,512 \n12 Himachal Pradesh 287 \n13 Jammu and Kashmir 1,711 \n14 Jharkhand 1,510 \n15 Karnataka 40,348 \n16 Kerala 1,500 \n17 Madhya Pradesh 3,691 \n18 Maharashtra 7,431 \n19 Manipur 1,077 \n20 Meghalaya 110 \n21 Mizoram 448 \n22 Nagaland 810 \n23 Odisha 4,984 \n24 Puducherry 304 \n25 Punjab 661 \n26 Rajasthan 7,457 \n27 Sikkim 66 \n28 Tamil Nadu 41,742 \n29 Telangana 5,476 \n30 The Dadra and Nagar Haveli and \nDaman and Diu 1,522 \n31 Tripura 1,072 \n32 Uttar Pradesh 23,786 \n33 Uttarakhand 485 \n34 West Bengal 1,517 \n Total 1,83,844 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |927", "t": "Number of Villages with Internet Services", "head": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS  \nDEPARTMENT OF TELECOMMUNICATIONS \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 927 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26TH JULY, 2023 \n \nNUMBER OF VILLAGES WITH INTERNET SERVICES  \n \n†927.  SHRI VIVEK NARAYAN SHEJWALKAR:  \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the number of villages in the country being provided with telecommunication and internet \nservices at present;  \n \n(b)  the target fixed to connect the left out villages with telecommunication and internet services;  \n \n(c)  the number of villages connected with telecom and internet services in Madhya Pradesh, \ndistrict-wise; and  \n \n(d)  the target fixed to connect the remaining villages? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR COMMUNICATIONS \n(SHRI DEVUSINH CHAUHAN) \n \n \n(a) to (d)    Government and Telecom Service Providers (TSPs) provide telecommunication and \nmobile internet service in the country in a phased manner including the state of Madhya Pradesh. As \nper data provided from Telecom Service Providers (TSPs), Department of Telecom  field unit and \nState Government as of March '2022, out of 6,44,131 villages (villages are as per data of Registrar \nGeneral of India as of November 2019), around 6,05,230 number of villages are covered with \ntelecommunication and mobile internet services. In Madhya Pradesh out of 55,058 (as per RGI data \nof November 2019) village, 51,966 village are connected with telecommunication and mobile \ninternet services. District wise details of telecom and internet service is attached in Annexure-I \n \nFurther, the Union Cabinet on 27.07.2022, has approved 4G Saturation scheme for providing \n4G mobile coverage to the remaining villages of the country including the State of Madhya Pradesh. \nIn addition, Government is implementing various schemes under Universal Service Obligation Fund \n(USOF) to connect the left out villages with telecommunication and internet services. Details of \nmajor schemes with timelines are at Annexure-II. \n. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n2 \n \n \n \n Annexure-I \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply of part (c) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 927 to be \nanswered on 26 th July, 2023 raised by Hon’ble Member of Parliament regarding “ Number of \nVillages with Internet Services” \n \nSR. NO. DISTRICTS TOTAL \nVILLAGES \nVillages covered \nwith telecom and internet \nservices \n1 Agar-Malwa 499 488 \n2 Alirajpur 544 501 \n3 Anuppur 579 559 \n4 Ashoknagar 880 844 \n5 Balaghat 1394 1218 \n6 Barwani 707 566 \n7 Betul 1457 1208 \n8 Bhind 939 918 \n9 Bhopal 514 486 \n10 Burhanpur 265 245 \n11 Chhatarpur 1238 1116 \n12 Chhindwara 2002 1842 \n13 Damoh 1216 1157 \n14 Datia 663 657 \n15 Dewas 1128 1069 \n16 Dhar 1561 1464 \n17 Dindori 928 804 \n18 Guna 1333 1283 \n19 Gwalior 590 545 \n20 Harda 550 497 \n21 Hoshangabad 966 868 \n22 Indore 621 616 \n23 Jabalpur 1395 1383 \n24 Jhabua 821 812 \n25 Katni 947 931 \n26 Khandwa (East Nimar) 718 683 \n27 Khargone (West Nimar) 1409 1301 \n28 Mandla 1247 1145 \n29 Mandsaur 951 925 \n30 Morena 847 782 \n31 Narsimhapur 1058 1032 \n32 Neemuch 808 759 \n33 Niwari 281 281 \n34 Panna 1035 921 \n35 Raisen 1486 1413 \n36 Rajgarh 1723 1680 \n37 Ratlam 1085 1055 \n38 Rewa 2711 2681 \n39 Sagar 2025 1953 \n40 Satna 1953 1889 \n41 Sehore 1082 1035 \n42 Seoni 1574 1516 \n43 Shahdol 836 806 \n44 Shajapur 632 617 \n45 Sheopur 609 506 \n46 Shivpuri 1355 1265 \n47 Sidhi 1055 994 \n48 Singrauli 761 683 \n49 Tikamgarh 682 674 \n50 Ujjain 1107 1102 \n51 Umaria 647 612 \n52 Vidisha 1644 1579 \n\n3 \n \n \nAnnexure-II \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply of part (b) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 927 to be \nanswered on 26 th July, 2023 raised by Hon’ble Member of Parliament regarding “ Number of \nVillages with Internet Services” \n \nDetails of major schemes/projects to improve the communication network/system in rural and \nremote areas across the country: \n \n(a) USOF 4G Saturation Scheme to cover 24,680 uncovered villages with 4G and 6,279 villages \nhaving 2G/3G connectivity are to be upgraded to 4G.  The estimated project cost is Rs. 26,316 \nCr. Target date of completion is December 2023.  \n(b) Provision of 4G Mobile Services i n North Eastern Region of the country under \nComprehensive Telecom Development Program for NER. The estimated cost for schemes is \nRs. 3,637 Cr. \n(c) Hiring of 10 Gbps International Bandwidth through BSNL for Internet Connectivity to \nAgartala from BSCCL, Banglade sh via Cox Bazar. Approval has been issued for hiring of \nadditional 10 Gbps International Bandwidth also. \n(d) Provision of 4G mobile connectivity in 354 uncovered villages of J&K, Ladakh, Himachal \nPradesh, Uttar Pradesh, Bihar, Rajasthan, Gujarat, Uttarakhand, Border area & other priority \nareas. The estimated project cost is Rs. 337 Cr. \n(e) Provision of 4G mobile connectivity in 7,287 Aspirational District villages (Andhra Pradesh, \nChhattisgarh, Jharkhand, Maharashtra and Odisha). The estimated cost for projects is  Rs. \n6,466 Cr. Target date of completion is November 2023 \n(f) Under LWE Phase -I scheme, 2,343 mobile towers ha"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |928", "t": "Illegal SAnd Mining", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 928 \nANSWERED ON 26.07.2023 \n \nILLEGAL SAND MINING \n  \n928. SHRI ARVIND DHARMAPURI: \n  \nWill the Minister of MINES be pleased to state :  \n(a) whether it is a fact that there have been instances of illegal sand mining in \nTelangana; \n(b) if so, the details thereof including the steps taken by the Government to curb \nthe rampant illegal sand mining in Telangana; \n(c) the details and the number of cases of illegal sand mining reported in \nTelangana over the past three years, district and year-wise; and \n(d) the details of the action proposed and directions issued to the Telangana State \nGovernment to curb the rampant levels of illegal sand mining in the State? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF MINES, COAL AND PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS \n(SHRI PRALHAD JOSHI) \n \n(a) to (d):  Sand is a minor mineral under Section 3(e) of the Mines and Minerals \n(Development & Regulation) (MMDR) Act, 1957. Section 15 of the MMDR Act, 1957, \nempowers the State Governments to make rules for regulating th e grant of minor \nmineral concessions. Moreover, Section 23C of the MMDR Act, also empowers the \nState Governments to make rules for preventing illegal mining, transportation and \nstorage of minerals and for the purposes connected therewith. The administratio n of \nthe mining of minor minerals is a subject that is completely within the domain of the \nrespective State Governments. \n  \nHowever, based on the quarterly returns submitted by State Governments on illegal \nmining to Indian Bureau of Mines (a subordinate office under the administrative control \nof Ministry of Mines), the number of illegal mining cases identified / reported by \nGovernment of Telangana and subsequent action taken during the last three years is \nprovided at Annexure. \n  \nFurther, Central Government has, inter-alia, taken following measures to curb illegal \nmining: \n(i) Penalty for illegal mining has been made more stringent by amendment of the \nMMDR Act in 2015. Penalties for contravention of Section 4(1) and 4(1A) of \nthe Act have been increased from Rs. 25 thousand per hectares to Rs. 5 Lakh \nper hectare and the term of imprisonment has been increased from 2 years to \n5 years. Further, Section 30B of the Act provides for constitution of Special \ncourts by State Governments for speedy trial of Illegal \nmining/Transportation/Storage cases and 30C of the Act provides that such \nSpecial Courts shall be deemed to be a Court of Session. \n\n(ii) The Ministry of Mines, through Indian Bureau of Mines, has developed the Mining \nSurveillance System (MSS) to use space te chnology for reporting any illegal mining \nactivity to the State Government who will take necessary action. Mining Surveillance \nSystem (MSS) is a satellite -based monitoring system which aims to detect illegal \nmining activity beyond the lease area through use of satellite images. \n(iii) In pursuance of the provisions of Section 23(C) of the Act, 21 State Governments \nhave framed rules to curb illegal mining. Also, 22 State Governments have set up Task \nForces to control illegal mining and review the action taken  by member departments \nfor checking the illegal mining activities at state and district levels. \n \n(iv) Ministry of Mines has prepared a ‘Sand Mining Framework’ in consultation with \nMining Departments of the States incorporating best practices amongst States  and \nsuggestions based on the objectives of sustainability, availability, affordability and \ntransparency in sand mining. The ‘Sand Mining Framework’ was circulated to all the \nStates for necessary action. \n   \n***** \n  \n   \n  \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n  \n\nAnnexure \n(The number of illegal mi ning cases identified/reported by Government of Telangana \non the quarterly returns submitted to IBM) \n  \nSr. \nNo. \nYear No. of \nCases \nFIR Lodged \n(Nos.) \nCourt \nCases Filed \n(Nos.) \nVehicle \nSeized \n(No.) \nFine Realized \nby State Govt. \n(Rs. Lakh) \n1. 2019-20 7039 0 0 0 1175.6 \n1. 2020-21 5620 0 0 0 820.3 \n2. 2021-22 2831 0 0 73 793.8 \nTotal 15,490 0 0 73 2789.7 \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |929", "t": "New Routes for Vande Bharat", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 929 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26.07.2023 \n \nNEW ROUTES FOR VANDE BHARAT \n \n929. DR. SUJAY RADHAKRISHNA VIKHE PATIL:  \n   PROF. RITA BAHUGUNA JOSHI:  \n SHRI UNMESH BHAIYYASAHEB PATIL:  \n DR. SHRIKANT EKNATH SHINDE:  \n DR. KRISHNA PAL SINGH YADAV:  \n DR. HEENA VIJAYKUMAR GAVIT:                            \n           \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state:  \n(a) whether the Ministry has laid out new routes for Vande  Bharat \nparticularly in UP, MP and Maharashtra, if so, the details thereof;  \n(b) whether the total number of Vande Bharat Trains will be increased \nexpansively to cover major metropolitan connectivity, if so, the details in \nthis regard;  \n(c) whether the Min istry envisions reconstruction of major railway stations \nin India to equip the platforms with better and modern facility with the \nupcoming of the Vande Bharat express and if so, the details thereof; and  \n(d) whether the Ministry plans to increase the number of Vande Bharat \nTrains with sleeper coaches, if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n(a) to (d) Indian Railways do not operate train services, including Vande \nBharat services, on State -wise basis as Railway network straddles across \nState boundaries. Of the presently operational 50 Vande Bharat services,  8 \ntrains, 6 trains and 10 trains ser ve various stations located in the States of \n\nUttar Pradesh, Madhya Pradesh and Maharashtra respectively.   Besides, \nintroduction of new trains, including Vande Bharat services, is an ongoing \nprocess on Indian Railways which is dependent on operational feasi bility, \ntraffic justification etc. Recently, the Amrit Bharat Station Scheme has been \nlaunched for development of Railway stations on Indian Railways. Under \nthis scheme, platforms are also to be equipped as per requirements with \nbetter and modern facilitie s such as provision of High Level platform, \nextension of platform, improvement to platform surface and cover over \nplatforms etc. \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |93", "t": "National Small Industries Corporation", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 93\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON: 20.07.2023\n \n \nNATIONAL SMALL INDUSTRIES CORPORATION \n \n \n \n93.  \n \nDR. JAYANTA KUMAR ROY:\n \n \nSHRIMATI SANGEETA KUMARI SINGH DEO:\n \n \nDR. \nSUKANTA MAJUMDAR:\n \n \nSHRI VINOD KUMAR SONKAR:\n \n \nSHRI BHOLA SINGH:\n \n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n \n(\na\n)\n \nwhether the National Small Industries Corporation (NSIC) provides integrated support \nservices to MSMEs and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(\nb\n)\n \nthe details of projects/schemes being implemented and physical achievements/progress made \nduring the last three years, s\ncheme/State\n-\nwise;\n \n(\nc\n)\n \nthe funds sanctioned under various schemes by NSIC during the last three years, \nscheme/State\n-\nwise;\n \n(\nd\n)\n \nthe details of beneficiaries under the National Scheduled Caste and Scheduled Tribe Hub for \nestablishment of new enterprises during the las\nt three years;\n \n(\ne\n)\n \nwhether the Government has reviewed the functioning of NSIC to make it more effective; and\n \n(\nf\n)\n \nif so, the steps being taken by the Government in this regard?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI BHANU PRATAP \nSINGH VERMA)\n \n \n(a)\n \nto\n \n(c):\n \nThe National Small Industries Corporation Limited (NSIC), a CPSE under the \nMinistry of Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises, provides integrated support services to MSMEs \nthrough its various programmes which inter\n-\nalia include:\n \n \n(\ni\n)\n \nRa\nw Material Assistance (RMA) Scheme\n-\n \nUnder RMA Scheme, NSIC provides financial \nassistance to MSMEs against bank guarantee for payment to the suppliers for procurement of raw \nmaterial. NSIC also facilitates MSMEs to meet their raw material requirement by mak\ning \narrangements with bulk manufacturers for procuring the material and supplying the same to \nMSMEs.\n \n \n(\nii\n)\n \nSingle Point Registration Scheme (SPRS)\n:\n-\n \nNSIC registers micro and small enterprises \n(MSEs) under its SPRS. The units registered under SPRS are eligible \nto get the benefits under \nPublic Procurement Policy for Micro & Small Enterprises Order 2012 as amended from time to \ntime.\n \n \n(\niii\n)\n \nMSME GlobalMart Portal\n:\n-\n \nNSIC is offering infomediary services through its B2B portal \nhttp://www.MSMEMart.com\n \nwhich is a one\n-\nstop on\ne\n-\nwindow bouquet of aids that provides \ninformation on business, technology and finance and also exhibit the core competence of Indian \nSMEs by way of enabling their digital presence for sustenance and growth.\n \n \n\n \n: 2 :\n \n \n \n(\niv\n)\n \nNSIC Technical Services Centres\n:\n-\n \nNSIC\n \nthrough its Technical services Centres and \nIncubation Centres offer industry oriented demand driven entrepreneurship/skill development \ntraining programmes (ESDP). The salient features of ESDP Training Programmes are self\n-\nemployment generation and skill de\nvelopment, providing opportunity to first generation \nentrepreneurs to acquire skills for enterprise building and integrated support by way of providing \nhands on training and entrepreneurship development.\n \n \nState\n-\nwise details of credit facilitation under RMA\n \nand achievement under the above programmes \nduring last three years are given at Annexure\n-\nI, II, III and IV respectively.\n \n \n(d)\n:\n \nNSIC implements National Scheduled Caste and Scheduled Tribe Hub (NSSH) Scheme \nacross the country to promote entrepreneurship am\nong the SC\n-\nST and to fulfill the obligations under \nthe Public Procurement Policy for Micro and Small Enterprises. To boost entrepreneurship among \nSC\n-\nST, the Hub has taken several interventions which include capacity building programmes, market \nlinkage prog\nrammes viz, participation in domestic and foreign exhibitions, organization of Special \nVendor Development programmes, workshops/awareness programmes, subsidy on purchase of \nmachinery/equipment, subsidy for registration under Single Point Registration Schem\ne, subsidy for \nenrollment on Government promoted e\n-\ncommerce portals such as GeM, e\n-\nkhadi, TRIFED, Tribes \nIndia, MSME Mart, etc. \n \n \nFunds allocated and released to NSIC for meeting the expenditure under NSSH during last \nthree years is given below:\n \n(Rs. in \ncrore)\n \nYear\n \nBE\n \nRE\n \nActual\n \n2020\n-\n21\n \n150.00\n \n120.00\n \n120.00\n \n2021\n-\n22\n \n150.00\n \n120.00\n \n101.87\n \n2022\n-\n23\n \n150.00\n \n135.00\n \n135.00\n \n  \n \n          \nState\n-\nwise details of beneficiaries under NSSH during last three years is given at Annexure\n-\nV.\n \n \n(e)\n \n&\n \n(f): \n \nAs per the guidelines of Department of Public Enterprises, MoU is being signed every \nyear between the Ministry of MSME and NSIC. The purpose of the MoU is to measure the \nperformance of the management of the CPSE on key selected parameters against the targe\nts agreed \nupon so as to improve the critical performance indicators of the organization. The MoU rating of \nNSIC during last three years are as under:\n \n \nYear\n \nMoU Rating\n \n2018\n-\n19\n \nGood\n \n2019\n-\n20\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |930", "t": "Earth Science Related Activities", "head": " \n \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EARTH SCIENCES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 930 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY, 26TH JULY, 2023 \n \nEARTH SCIENCE RELATED ACTIVITIES \n \n†930.  SHRI VINOD L. CHAVDA: \n \n Will the Minister of EARTH SCIENCES be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government proposes to hold earth science related activities for the students in \nthe country for the next three years; and  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF EARTH SCIENCES \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n(a) - (b)  Yes Sir. Ministry has been supporting various awareness activities on earth sciences for \nstudents through its outreach program. Following are the events proposed to be \nconducted in a yearly basis:  \n \n(i) Days of significance like Earth Day (22 April), Ozone Day (16 September), etc. \n(ii) Indian National Earth Science Olympiad (INESO). \n(iii) International Earth Science Olympiad (IESO) for school students in Class IX to \nXII. \n(iv) Earth science Exhibitions, Science Festivals, Workshops, Seminars, Popular \nLectures, etc. \n \n \n \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |931", "t": "Telecom technology Exporter", "head": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS  \nDEPARTMENT OF TELECOMMUNICATIONS \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 931 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26TH JULY, 2023 \n \nTELECOM TECHNOLOGY EXPORTER  \n \n931.  SHRI MANNE SRINIVAS REDDY:  \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether India has proven its strength with its 2G, 3G, 4G, 5G technology which is poised to \nemerge as a major telecom technology exporter to the world in the future and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(b)  whether the Government has any details of the 5G services that are rolled out in number of \ncities till date and if so, the details thereof including the private players who are providing their \nservices, city/ State-wise; and  \n \n(c)  the details of the funds sanctioned/spent by the Government for these services till now and \nthe response received therefrom compared with 4G? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR COMMUNICATIONS \n(SHRI DEVUSINH CHAUHAN) \n \n(a) India has designed & developed its own 4G/5G Technology stacks and the deployment has \ncommenced in Bharat Sanchar Nigam Limited. Further, Department of Telecommunications (DoT) \nhas taken the following initiatives aims to promote the ecosystem for research & development and \nmake India a global manufacturing hub: \n• Production Linked Incentive Scheme was launched on 24.02.2021 to promote domestic \nmanufacturing of specified telecom and networking products including 4G, 5G products, \nand also design-led manufacturing in the country.  The companies under the scheme have \nmade an export of Rs. 6911 crores up till 31.05.2023. \n• Telecom Technology Development Fund scheme with 5% of annual collections from the \nUniversal Service Obligation Fund made available for funding research & development \nof technologies, products, and services. Under the scheme, DoT has approved 2 projects \nwith a grant of Rs. 240.5 crores.  \n• Digital Communication Innovation Square (DCIS) under Champion Services Sector \nScheme (CSSS) to catalyze start -up culture and build a strong and inclusive ecosy stem \nfor innovation and entrepreneurship. Under it, 126 startups and MSMEs have been funded \nby DoT with Rs. 105.87 crores as grant-in-aid.   \n \n(b) & (c)  The city/State-wise list of 5G services rolled out by the Telecom Service Providers \n(TSPs) is attached as Annexure. The infrastructure required to operate a telecom network is \ndeveloped by the Telecom Service Providers.  \n \n  \n\n2 \n \n \nAnnexure to the Lok Sabha Unstarred Parliament Question No.931 for 26.07.2023 \n \n \nDistrict-wise 5G Base Transceiver Stations (BTSs) as on 07.07.2023 \nS.No State Name District Airtel Reliance Jio Vodafone \nIdea Total \n1 AP \nAnantapur 131 992   1123 \nChittoor 165 964   1129 \nEast Godavari 244 1108   1352 \nWest Godavari 178 702   880 \nGuntur 199 1080   1279 \nY S R 117 706   823 \nKrishna 220 1103   1323 \nNTR 3 2   5 \nKurnool 117 688   805 \nSri Potti Sriramulu  \nNellore \n108 562   670 \nPrakasam 97 540   637 \nSrikakulam 70 346   416 \nVisakhapatnam 230 1080   1310 \nVizianagaram 62 346   408 \n2 Telangana \nAdilabad 18 114   132 \nKumuram Bheem  \n(Asifabad) \n8 92   100 \nMancherial 47 240   287 \nNirmal 21 90   111 \nHyderabad 640 1919   2559 \nMedchal Malkajgiri 524 1906   2430 \nRanga Reddy 498 1204   1702 \nVikarabad 22 166   188 \nJagtial 52 200   252 \nKarimnagar 77 328   405 \nPeddapalli 41 174   215 \nRajanna Sircilla 26 100   126 \nBhadradri Kothagudem 44 286   330 \nKhammam 46 264   310 \nMahabubabad 16 72   88 \nJogulamba Gadwal 12 122   134 \nMahabubnagar 46 302   348 \nNagarkurnool 18 124   142 \nNarayanpet 10 66   76 \nWanaparthy 20 80   100 \nMedak 20 124   144 \nSangareddy 121 504   625 \nSiddipet 54 202   256 \nJangaon 14 84   98 \nNalgonda 46 326   372 \nSuryapet 23 244   267 \nYadadri Bhuvanagiri 20 248   268 \nKamareddy 25 146   171 \n\n3 \n \nNizamabad 73 350   423 \nJayashankar  \nBhupalpally \n10 56   66 \nMulugu 6 30   36 \nWarangal Rural 18 88   106 \nWarangal Urban 89 366   455 \n3 Assam \nBaksa 27 26   53 \nBarpeta 43 284   327 \nBongaigaon 20 122   142 \nChirang 21 42   63 \nDhubri 24 158   182 \nGoalpara 33 148   181 \nKokrajhar 29 78   107 \nNalbari 20 68   88 \nSouth Salmara- \nMankachar \n21 18   39 \nDibrugarh 54 196   250 \nTinsukia 47 176   223 \nCharaideo 10 18   28 \nGolaghat 26 90   116 \nJorhat 45 188   233 \nMajuli  2     2 \nSivasagar 23 128   151 \nKamrup 72 337   409 \nKamrup (Metro) 231 605   836 \nHojai 33 38   71 \nKarbi Anglong 21 74   95 \nMorigaon 13 112   125 \nNagaon 47 368   415 \nWest Karbi Anglong 14 8   22 \nCachar 81 190   271 \nDima Hasao 11 36   47 \nHailakandi 14 46   60 \nKarimganj 28 86   114 \nBiswanath 14 14   28 \nDarrang 19 88   107 \nDhemaji 26 90   116 \nLakhimpur 32 110   142 \nSonitpur 62 206   268 \nUdalguri 16 28   44 \n4 Bihar \nBhojpur 46 176   222 \nBuxar 28 98   126 \nBegusarai 77 280   357 \nWest Champaran 111 112   223 \nBanka 29 54   83 \nBhagalpur 100 308   408 \nGopalganj 143 72   215 \nSaran 134 240   374 \nSiwan 199 76   275 \nDarbhanga 96 228   324 \nArwal 15 36   51 \n\n4 \n \nAurangabad 62 128   190 \nGaya 106 284 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |932", "t": "Interrupted Services of BSNL in Maharashtra", "head": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS  \nDEPARTMENT OF TELECOMMUNICATIONS \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 932 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26TH JULY, 2023 \n \nINTERRUPTED SERVICES OF BSNL IN MAHARASHTRA  \n \n†932.  SHRIMATI BHAVANA PUNDALIKRAO GAWALI:  \nSHRI KRUPAL BALAJI TUMANE:  \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the services of Bharat Sanchar Nigam Limited (BSNL) remains interrupted in \nMaharashtra particularly in rural areas and if so, the details thereof along with its causes;  \n \n(b)  whether the Government has received complaints from the State Government in this regard;  \n \n(c)  if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(d)  the steps taken by the Government to address the said problem particularly in the rural areas;  \n \n(e)  whether the Government has fixed any accountability in this regard, if so, the details thereof; \nand  \n \n(f)  the instant measures taken by the Government to solve the said problem and to provide the \nhigh speed network in the rural areas? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR COMMUNICATIONS \n(SHRI DEVUSINH CHAUHAN) \n \n(a)   No major interruption has occurred in the services of Bharat Sanchar Nigam Limited (BSNL) \nMaharashtra circle including in its rural areas in recent past. However, there were some interruptions \nin its services due to power outage, damage of Optical Fibre Cables (OFC) / underground copper \ncables and due to various ongoing development works such as construction of roads, drainage works, \nwater pipe lines etc., which were restored promptly. \n(b) to (f)  No such complaint has been received from the State Government of Maharashtra. \nHowever, BSNL is offering high-speed broadband services in rural areas to its customers on Fibre to \nthe Home (FTTH) network. The old copper cable connections are being migrated to high-speed FTTH \nnetwork. As on 01/07/2023, 2.33 lakh FTTH connections are working in Maharashtra Cir cle out of \nwhich 66,772 connections are in rural areas.  \nFurther, BharatNet project is being implemented with the support of Universal Service \nObligation Fund (USOF) in a phased manner to provide broadband connectivity to all the Gram \nPanchayats (GPs) and inhabited villages in the country.   As on 30.06.2023, 6,43,450 km Optical \nFibre Cable has been laid, and 1,94,909 GPs have been made service ready.   The infrastructure \ncreated under BharatNet project is a national asset, accessible on a non -discriminatory basis to the \nService Providers, and the same can be utilized to provide broadband services, such as Wi -Fi \n\n2 \n \nHotspots, Fibre to the Home (FTTH) connections, leased lines, dark fibre, backhaul to mobile towers, \netc. Using BharatNet, more than 5.65 lakh high speed Fiber to the Home (FTTH) broadband \nconnections have been provided by BSNL & others ISPs. \n \nIn addition to BharatNet, the Union Cabinet on 27.07.2022 approved a project for saturation \nof 4G mobile services in uncovered villages across the country, which is being executed by \nBSNL.     The project will provide 4G mobile services in 24,680 uncovered villages in remote and \ndifficult areas with a provision to include 20% additional villages.  In addition, 6,279 villages having \nonly 2G/3G connectivity shall be upgraded to 4G. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |933", "t": "Connectivity to Calicut Railway Station", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 933 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26.07.2023 \n \nCONNECTIVITY TO CALICUT RAILWAY STATION \n \n933. SHRI M.K. RAGHAVAN: \n \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state:   \n \n \n(a) whether the Government has observed that inspite of being a highly profitable Railway \nStation, connectivity to Calicut railway station is being neglected by the Railways; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the measures taken to enhance the connectivity to \nCalicut Railway station from various cities of the country;  \n(c) whether the Government has observed that rail connectivity to Bangalore from Calicut \nis not sufficient considering the passenger and cargo traffic in this sector, if so, the steps \ntaken to expand connectivity to Calicut from Bangalore;  \n \n(d) whether the Government has any proposal to introduce more passenger trains between \nCalicut-Bangalore, Calicut-Chennai, Calicut- Trivandrum, if so, the details thereof; and  \n \n(e) whether  the Government has observed that trains running through Kerala still have \nolder railway coaches, if so, the details of steps being taken to modernise the existing \nrailway coaches in these trains?  \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND  \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n \n(a) to (d)  Kozhikode is presently being served by 114 train services connecting it to cities \nlike Delhi, Mumbai, Kolkata etc. Further, Thiruvananthapuram, Chennai and Bengaluru  are \nalso connected with Kozhikode by 26, 4 and 2 pairs of train services respectively. \nBesides, introduction of trains is an ongoing process on Indian Railways subject to \noperational feasibility, traffic demand, availability of resources, etc. Demand/requ est for \nfreight rakes is received in the Railway system in the form of indents, and rakes are \nsupplied against these indents.  \n(e)  Indian Railways do not operate train services on State- wise basis as Railway network \nstraddles across State boundaries. On Indian Railways, only those coaches which are \nwithin their codal life  and fit for passenger operations are used in passenger carrying \ntrains. Besides, constant monitoring of the condition of coaches is done to ensure safety \nand convenience of passengers. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |934", "t": "4G Saturation Towers", "head": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS  \nDEPARTMENT OF TELECOMMUNICATIONS \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 934 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26TH JULY, 2023 \n \n4G SATURATION TOWERS  \n \n934.  SHRI SHRINIWAS PATIL:  \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the work of 4G saturation towers in Satara District of Maharashtra is not progressing \nat expected pace and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(b)  if not, the reasons therefor;  \n \n(c)  the number of 4G saturation towers planned to be erected in Satara district in Maharashtra; \nand  \n \n(d)  the details of the steps taken by the Government to speed up the pending progress work of \n4G Saturation towers? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR COMMUNICATIONS \n(SHRI DEVUSINH CHAUHAN) \n \n \n(a) to (c)  Under Universal Service Obligation Fund (USOF) funded project of 4G Saturation \nscheme, 69 4G mobile towers are planned for erection in Satara district of Maharashtra. Work of \nland acquisition & infrastructure creation is going on as per expected pace to complete the work in \nscheduled timelines. \n \n(d)  Regular review meetings with Bharat Sanchar Nigam Limited (BSNL), State governments, \nMinistry of Environment Forest and Climate Change (MoEFCC) & Ministry of Pow er are being \nheld for coordination & speeding up the pace of the execution of project.  \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |935", "t": "Funding to Training Centres under SAMARTH Scheme", "head": " \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 935 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26.07.2023 \n \nFUNDING TO TRAINING CENTRES UNDER SAMARTH SCHEME \n \n935. SHRI C.R. PATIL:  \n        SHRI ANURAG SHARMA:  \n        SHRI PRATAP CHANDRA SARANGI:  \n        DR. RAMAPATI RAM TRIPATHI:  \n        SHRI MAHENDRA SINGH SOLANKY:  \n        SHRI P.P. CHAUDHARY:  \n        SHRI SANGAM LAL GUPTA: \n  \n \n \nWill the Minister of TEXTILES वस्त्र मंत्री \nbe pleased to state : \n \n(a) whether the Government provides separate funding to each training centre  under the SAMARTH \nScheme and if so, details thereof;   \n \n(b) the details of the number of training programmes conducted in the states of Gujarat, Odisha, \nMadhya Pradesh, Rajasthan and Uttar Pradesh under the said scheme; and  \n \n(c) the number of beneficiari es trained so far and those provided with placements under the said \nscheme from the State of Gujarat and Parliamentary Constituencies of Balasore in Odisha, Dewas in \nMadhya Pradesh Pali, in Rajasthan and Deoria, Pratapgarh and Jhansi in Uttar Pradesh? \n \n \n \nउत्तर \nANSWER \nवस्त्र राज्‍ य मंत्री (श्रीमती दर्शना जरदोर्) \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR TEXTILES  \n (SMT. DARSHANA JARDOSH) \n \n(a): Funding of the training programme  under Samarth is based on the per trainee hourly rates \nprovided in the Common Norms prescribed by Ministry of Skill Development & Entrepreneurship and \nreleased directly to the Implementing Partners (IPs) based on the physical progress of training \nprogramme.   \n \n(b): Details of number of training programmes conducted in the States of Gujarat, Odisha, Madhya \nPradesh, Rajasthan and Uttar Pradesh under the scheme is given in Annexure-I.  \n \n(c): The web based Samarth portal captures data district wise and not based on the demarcation of \nParliamentary Constituencies. Number of beneficiaries trained and those provided with placements \nunder Samarth from the State of Gujarat and districts of Balasore in Odisha, Dewas in Madhya \nPradesh Pali, in Rajasthan and Deoria, Pratapgarh and Jhansi in Uttar Pradesh are given in   \nAnnexure-II. \n \n \n \n\n \n \nAnnexure-I \nDetails of number of training programmes conducted in the States of Gujarat, Odisha, Madhya \nPradesh, Rajasthan and Uttar Pradesh under Samarth \n                                                                                                        (as on 20.07.2023) \nS. No State No of operational training \ncentres  \nNo of beneficiaries \ntrained (Passed) \n1 Gujarat 43 6,798 \n2 Odisha 32 4,984 \n3 Madhya Pradesh 41 3,691 \n4 Rajasthan 46 7,457 \n5 Uttar Pradesh 284 23,786 \n \n \nAnnexure-II \nDetails of number of beneficiaries trained (passed) and provided with placements under \nSamarth scheme  \n                                                                                                       (as on 20.07.2023) \nS. No State No of beneficiaries trained \n(Passed) \nNo of beneficiaries Placed \n1 Gujarat 6,798 5,000 \n \nS. No State District No.  of beneficiaries \ntrained (Passed) \nNo of beneficiaries \nPlaced \n1 Odisha  Balasore 370 266 \n2 Rajasthan Pali 28 0 ** \n3 Uttar Pradesh Pratapgarh 284 199 \n*Note- As regards district of Dewas in Madhya Pradesh and Deoria &  Jhansi in Uttar Pradesh, \nno proposals have been received so far. \n** The training programme conducted in traditional handicraft sector courses having a \nprovision for self employment.  \n**** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|12|UNSTARRED |936", "t": "Robotics Framework", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ELECTRONICS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 936 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON: 26.07. 2023 \n \nROBOTICS FRAMEWORK \n \n936.     SHRI KOTHA PRABHAKAR REDDY: \n \nWill the Minister of Electronics and Information Technology be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Governments announced the launch of Robotics Framework under which an \neco-system would be created for innovation, entrepreneurship and research and \ndevelopment and if so, the details thereof; \n(b) whether the framework seeks to leverage robotics technology to drive growth across the \nfour domains of agriculture, healthcare, industrialautomation and consumer robotic; \n(c) if so, the details thereof along with funds sanctioned/spent to each State till date; and? \n(d) the details of the other progress made therein, State-wise? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR ELECTRONICS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n(SHRI RAJEEV CHANDRASEKHAR) \n \n(a):  No sir. However, an Inter -Ministerial Committee has been constituted to study global \nbest practices on the role of government in supporting and fostering the domestic robotics \necosystem. Currently, the committee is deliberating on theStrategy for fostering  an end-to-\nend robotics ecosystem that encompasses research & design, manufacturing, prototyping, and \nadoption. \n \n(b) to  (d): Ministry of Heavy Industries (MHI) has notified the Scheme on Enhancement of \nCompetitiveness in the Indian Capital Goods Sector - Phase-II for providing assistance to \nCommon Technology Development and Services Infrastructure.To seek leverage of Robotics \nin Industrial Automation, a State wise list of Projects sanctioned under the scheme is detailed \nin Annexure I. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure I \n \n***** \n  \nS.No. S