ountry-wise;  \n(d) whether there is tremendous increase in the import of cheap Steel thereby severely \naffecting domestic steel industries and if so, the details thereof along with the reasons \ntherefor;  \n(e) whether the Government is considering the imposition of a countervailing duty on \nstainless steel imports to safeguard the interests of local manufacturers and counter \nthe influx of cheap imports and if so, the details thereof; and  \n(f) the other steps taken by the Government to enhance steel production domestically \nand reduce the import of cheap steel? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE     (SHRI FAGGAN SINGH KULASTE) \nMINISTRY OF STEEL \n \n(a) Details of production of crude steel during last three years and current year are as \nunder:- \nYear Qty (MT) \n2020-21 103.54 \n2021-22 120.29 \n2022-23 127.20 \nApril-June 2023-24* 33.63 \nMT=million tonnes;   * Provisional \n \n(b)to(e): Steel is a deregulated sector where Government acts as a facilitator for \ndevelopment of steel sector in the country. Commercial decisions such as imports \ndepend  on  prevailing  market  dynamics. Government monitors  the import  of all  \n \nContd…2/- \n \n\n-2- \n \nproducts and takes measures in national interest. Details of import of finished steel \nduring last three years and current year is given below:- \n \nImport of Finished Steel \nYear Qty (MT) Value (Rs in crore) \n2020-21 4.75 32154 \n2021-22 4.67 46298 \n2022-23 6.02 64454 \nApril-June 2023-24* 1.40 13817 \nMT=million tonnes,  *provisional \n \nDetails of category wise and country-wise imports during the last three years and \ncurrent year are at Annexure-I and Annexure-II respectively. \n \n(f) Steps taken by the Government to enhance steel production domestically and \nreduce the import of steel are as under: -  \n \ni. Notification of Domestically Manufactured Iron & Steel Products (DMI&SP) \nPolicy for promoting procurement of Made in India Steel. \nii. Notification of Steel Scrap Recycling Policy to enhance the availability of \ndomestically generated scrap. \niii. Issuance of Quality Control Orders (QCOs) to stop manufacturing and \nimport of non-standardized steel \niv. Calibration of customs duty from time to time keeping in view the stakes of \nboth upstream and downstream industries & consumers. \nv. Notification and launch of Production Linked Incentive (PLI) Scheme for \nSpecialty Steel with an outlay of ₹ 6322 Crore with an aim to boost the \ndomestic production and attract significant investments for ‘specialty steel’ \nproduction. \n \n*****  \n\nAnnexure-I \n \nIMPORT OF TOTAL FINISHED STEEL \nCategory  \n2020-21 2021-22 2022-23 April-June 2023-\n24* \nQuantity Value Quantity Value \n(in Rs. \nCrores) \nQuantity Value \n(in Rs. \nCrores) \nQuantity Value  \n(in '000 \ntonnes) \n(in Rs. \nCrores) \n( in '000 \ntonnes) \n( in '000 \ntonnes) \n( in '000 \ntonnes) \n(in Rs. \nCrores) \nFinished Steel(Non-\nAlloy)   \nNon-Flat \n     Bars & Rods 143 714 63 455 115 834 41 271 \n     Structurals 31 176 14 104 9 83 1 10 \n     Rly.Materials 63 453 68 581 76 893 16 215 \nTOTAL Non-Flat 237 1342 145 1140 199 1811 58 496 \nFlat                 \n     Plates 379 2318 237 1977 161 1909 71 598 \n     HR Sheets 1 6 0 0 0 1 0 0 \n     HR Coils/Strips 828 3537 811 5349 1525 8951 308 1819 \n     CR Coils/Sheets 222 1247 340 2465 386 3119 81 614 \n     GP/GC Sheets 767 5008 735 6282 914 8798 251 2258 \n      Elec.Sheets 460 3139 430 5171 264 5932 66 1291 \n     TMBP 0 1 0 1 0 0 0 0 \n     Tin Plates 138 644 54 307 11 100 2 18 \n     Tin Free Steel 48 213 12 73 3 34 1 6 \n     Pipes 158 974 150 1111 215 2096 70 631 \nTOTAL Flat 3001 17087 2769 22735 3479 30941 850 7234 \nTOTAL Fin. Steel \n(Non-Alloy) 3238 18430 2913 23875 3678 32752 908 7730 \nAlloy/Stainless Steel           \n     Non-Flat  344 3055 214 3547 233 4199 55 1033 \n     Flat  1171 10670 1542 18876 2111 27503 439 5054 \nTOTAL FIN. STEEL \n(Alloy/Stainless) 1515 13724 1756 22423 2344 31702 494